window.IMPLANT_REVISION_DATA={"title":"Membership in Implant Dentistry — M Imp Dent RCSEd","author":"Dr. Kam O Man, Owen","notice":"Independent revision aid; not an official publication of, or endorsed by, the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh.","sections":[{"id":"introduction","title":"About the author & examination overview","start":1,"end":12,"pages":[{"sourcePage":1,"text":"ROYAL COLLEGE OF SURGEONS OF EDINBURGH\n\nMembership in Implant Dentistry\n\nM Imp Dent RCSEd\n\nA Candidate’s Revision Notebook\n\nBlueprint-mapped core revision notes\n\n800 single best answer questions with explanations\n\n58 OSCE stations with model answers and checklists\n\n51 structured oral cases with examiner question flows\n\nWritten throughout to the standard of a general dentist\n\nwith an interest in implant dentistry\n\nFor candidates sitting the examination\n\nfrom September 2026 onwards\n\nDr. Kam O Man, Owen\n\nBDS (HK) · MFDS RCSEd · MFDS RCPS (Glasg)\n\nMClinDent (Fixed and Removable Pros) (KCL)\n\nMImpDent (RCSEd) · MGDS RCSI\n\nMapped throughout to Appendix A — Learning Outcomes and Blueprint (April 2026),\n\nthe Regulations and the Guidance for Candidates\n\nAn independent revision aid. Not an official publication of, nor endorsed by, the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh."},{"sourcePage":2,"text":"Contents\n\nAbout the author 1\n\nMembership in Implant Dentistry (M Imp Dent RCSEd) 2\n\nHow to read this notebook 8\n\nPART 1 — EXAMINATION TECHNIQUE 11\n\nAPPENDIX — Quick self-declaration and eligibility checklist 13\n\nPART 2 — CORE REVISION NOTES 17\n\n§1 Patient assessment, history and risk 18\n\n§2 Examination, occlusion and space 21\n\n§3 Investigations 24\n\n§4 Surgical anatomy relevant to implant placement 27\n\n§5 Osseointegration, bone biology and healing 29\n\n§6 Implant materials, design and surfaces 31\n\n§7 Treatment planning, case complexity and consent 34\n\n§8 Surgical placement — decision making and procedural knowledge 39\n\n§9 Minor hard and soft tissue augmentation 44\n\n§10 Restoring implants — prosthodontics and occlusion 48\n\n§11 Complications and their management 52\n\n§12 Peri-implant health and disease, maintenance and outcomes 55\n\nPART 3 — HIGH-YIELD NUMBERS, CLASSIFICATIONS AND LISTS 60\n\nPART 4 — THE LITERATURE: WHICH BOOK, WHICH PAPER, WHAT NUMBERS 63\n\nPART 5 — WRITTEN PAPER: 800 SINGLE BEST ANSWER QUESTIONS 81\n\nPART 6 — OSCE: 58 PRACTICAL & PROCEDURAL STATIONS 226\n\nPART 7 — STRUCTURED ORAL EXAMINATION: 51 CLINICAL SCENARIOS 420\n\nTHREE SET-PIECE ANSWERS TO PREPARE BEFORE ANY CASE 424\n\nSet-piece 1 — Placement timing and loading protocol 425\n\nSet-piece 2 — Bone grafting: materials, indications, and complications 429\n\nSet-piece 3 — Incision and flap design 434\n\nHow to drill the three set-pieces 437\n\nSOE Case 1 — The bruxist, the posterior maxilla and the occlusal scheme 438\n\nSOE Case 2 — Missing 22 in a patient who works in the entertainment industry 445\n\nSOE Case 3 — A perio-endo lesion in a medically complex patient 453\n\nSOE Case 4 — The very deeply placed 46 implant, with mesial drifting of the 47 460\n\nSOE Case 5 — The long-term denture wearer and the mandibular implant overdenture 467\n\nSOE Case 6 — Recession and grey show-through at a malpositioned anterior implant 475\n\nSOE Case 7 — Stage IV Grade C periodontitis and a failing dentition 483\n\nSOE Case 8 — Peri-implantitis at linked LR6 and LR7 491\n\nSOE Case 9 — The atrophic posterior maxilla 499\n\nSOE Case 10 — Nerve proximity and the consent conversation 507\n\nSOE Case 11 — The medically complex patient on antiresorptives 515\n\nSOE Case 12 — The early failure 523\n\nSOE Case 13 — Repeated mechanical failure in a bruxist 527\n\nSOE Case 14 — Immediate placement in an intact anterior socket 535\n\nSOE Case 15 — The smoker with treated periodontitis 543\n\nSOE Case 16 — The edentulous maxilla and the fixed-prosthesis request 551\n\nSOE Case 17 — Two adjacent missing central incisors 559"},{"sourcePage":3,"text":"SOE Case 18 — The adolescent with missing lateral incisors 567\n\nSOE Case 19 — The free-end saddle and the shortened dental arch 576\n\nSOE Case 20 — The dissatisfied patient and the governance response 585\n\nSOE Case 21 — Failed endodontics in the aesthetic zone 594\n\nSOE Case 22 — The furcation-involved molar 603\n\nSOE Case 23 — The failing three-unit bridge 612\n\nSOE Case 24 — Well-controlled diabetes and a single posterior implant 621\n\nSOE Case 25 — Early peri-implant mucositis in a new patient 631\n\nSOE Case 26 — Progressive bone loss at five years 642\n\nSOE Case 27 — Sinus membrane perforation during a transcrestal elevation 653\n\nSOE Case 28 — An implant displaced into the maxillary sinus 663\n\nSOE Case 29 — Fracture of a narrow implant in a molar site 673\n\nSOE Case 30 — Aesthetic failure: the implant crown that looks too long 683\n\nSOE Case 31 — The severely anxious patient who asks for an implant 693\n\nSOE Case 32 — High smile line, thin phenotype, immediate implant request 704\n\nSOE Case 33 — Multiple missing posterior teeth on a limited budget 710\n\nSOE Case 34 — Adult hypodontia 715\n\nSOE Case 35 — The patient who will not commit to maintenance 721\n\nSOE Case 36 — Failing lower denture in an elderly patient with early dementia 726\n\nSOE Case 37 — The severely atrophic mandible 732\n\nSOE Case 38 — Failure to integrate in a previously grafted site 738\n\nSOE Case 39 — Recurrent ceramic fracture on a screw-retained bridge 744\n\nSOE Case 40 — Residual cement discovered incidentally 750\n\nSOE Case 41 — Post-orthodontic lateral incisor spaces 756\n\nSOE Case 42 — Parafunction and an anterior implant 762\n\nSOE Case 43 — Head and neck radiotherapy and xerostomia 768\n\nSOE Case 44 — Nerve injury caused by another clinician 774\n\nSOE Case 45 — The patient who wants a healthy implant crown removed 780\n\nSOE Case 46 — The second opinion 787\n\nSOE Case 47 — Maxillary overdenture versus fixed prosthesis 793\n\nSOE Case 48 — “Teeth in a day” 799\n\nSOE Case 49 — Imminent explantation in the aesthetic zone 805\n\nSOE Case 50 — Governance, audit and the quality improvement project 811\n\nSOE Case 51 — The lawyer: replacement of the 21, six weeks after extraction 819\n\nPART 8 — ANSWERS AND EXPLANATIONS 829"},{"sourcePage":4,"text":"About the author\n\nDr. Kam O Man, Owen BDS (HK), MFDS RCSEd, MFDS RCPS (Glasg), MClinDent (Fixed and Removable Pros) (KCL)\n\nMImpDent (RCSEd), MGDS RCSI\n\nDr. Kam graduated from the University of Hong Kong in 2012, and later completed his Master of Clinical Dentistry in Fixed and Removable Prosthodontics at King’s College London, where he was awarded the Henry Schein Leadership Scholarship.\n\nDr. Kam holds an array of postgraduate qualifications, including the Diplomas of Membership from the Faculties of Dental Surgery of the Royal Colleges of Surgeons of England, Edinburgh, and Glasgow (MFDS RCS Eng, RCSEd, RCPS Glasg). In 2025, he attained the Diploma of Membership in Implant Dentistry from the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh (MImpDent RCSEd), and the Diploma of Membership in General Dental Surgery from the Royal College of Surgeons of Ireland (MGDS RCSI). He is nominated to be a fellow member of the International College of Dentists (FICD), the College of General Dentistry (UK) (FCGDent), and the Faculty of Dentistry from the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Glasgow (FDS RCPS Glasg).\n\nEarly in his career, Dr. Kam co-founded and served in the Outreach Dental Care Programme for the elderly, serving over 8000 elderly per calendar year, demonstrating his commitment to community service. For the past decade, as founder and principal of a local dental group, he has focused on delivering innovative, evidence-based care with particular interests in oral implantology, prosthodontics, and orthodontics. He is also actively involved in research exploring traumatic sports injury, dental radiology, and the links between oral health and systemic conditions, including idiopathic scoliosis."},{"sourcePage":5,"text":"Membership in Implant Dentistry (M Imp Dent RCSEd)\n\nA Candidate’s Revision Notebook\n\nBlueprint-mapped core notes · 300 single best answers · 40 OSCE & SOE stations with model answers\n\nPrepared for candidates sitting the examination from September 2026 onwards\n\nHow to use this notebook This notebook is written to one specification only: Appendix A — Learning Outcomes and Blueprint (April 2026). Every section carries the blueprint domain it serves, and every question is tagged to the component that tests it (W = Written, OS-P = OSCE Practical, SCE = Structured Oral).\n\nThree points about level govern everything that follows, and they are the single most common reason candidates fail:\n\nRegulation 18.2: “It is important to note that the examination is not a specialist examination and that the level of knowledge expected in any area will not exceed that which would be expected of a general dental practitioner who has three years’ experience of clinical dental practice together with a particular interest in implant dentistry.”\n\nRegulation 2.1: The examination “is not intended to test the candidate’s competency at the execution of these procedures or the detailed knowledge of the more advanced procedures such as treatment planning with computerised scanning software, major bone grafting (block onlay grafting), external sinus grafting or full arch prosthodontic rehabilitation.”\n\nRegulation 2.1 (aims): “…harmless, independent overall patient care.”\n\nRead those three quotations again before every practice session. The examiners are not looking for the most sophisticated answer; they are looking for the safest defensible answer, delivered with insight into your own limits. A candidate who says “I would place an immediate implant with simultaneous horizontal GBR and an immediate provisional” in a high-aesthetic-risk case will score lower than one who says “this is a Complex case on the SAC classification; the aesthetic risk is high because of the thin pheno-type and high smile line, so I would either refer, or treat with a delayed protocol after ridge preservation, and I would explain both to the patient.” Recognising complexity and knowing when to refer is an examinable learning outcome in its own right (Appendix A: “Recognise the complexity of the case and the need for referral”).\n\nStart here: calibrate before you revise\n\nThree short sections are written to set your expectations before you start working through the material, and they are the most useful pages in this notebook if your time is short:\n\n• “Where the marks are in the OSCE”, at the start of Part 6 — how a practical circuit is constructed, and the six habits that separate a clear pass from a bare one. • “Where the marks are in the SOE”, at the start of Part 7 — the case archetypes that recur, and what each is really testing. • The three set-pieces, also at the start of Part 7 — placement timing and loading protocol; bone grafting materials with their advantages, disadvantages and complications; and incision and flap design. Each is written as a spoken 60–90 second answer with the follow-up questions you should expect.\n\nRead all three before you plan your revision. They will change your priorities — in particular, the proce-dural outcomes in the blueprint are weighted far more towards restorative and prosthodontic procedure, temporisation, communication with the technician and problem-solving around existing restorations than most candidates assume. Candidates who prepare only for surgery prepare for the wrong examina-tion.\n\nA note on scope. Nothing in this notebook is a record of any past examination. Every station, case and question is written by the author from the published learning outcomes and blueprint, the Regulations, the Guidance for Candidates and the standard texts, as teaching material. Candidates are reminded that"},{"sourcePage":6,"text":"the College’s regulations prohibit the disclosure of examination content, and that this obligation binds you both before and after you sit.\n\nSuggested six-week revision plan\n\nWeek Focus This notebook\n\n0 Calibration “Where the marks are” at the start of Parts 6 and 7, and the three set-pieces — read first 1 Assessment, risk, investigations, anatomy Part 2 §1–§4; SBAs 1–86, 301–440 2 Biology, materials, implant design Part 2 §5–§6; SBAs 87–132, 441–515 3 Planning, consent, SAC, sequencing Part 2 §7; SBAs 133–180, 516–590; SOE cases 1–10 4 Surgery and minor augmentation Part 2 §8–§9; SBAs 181–246, 591–700; OSCE 1–25 5 Prosthodontics, occlusion and temporisation Part 2 §10; SBAs 247–280, 701–760; OSCE 52, 53, 54, 56; OSCE 26–50 6 Complications, peri-implant disease, governance Part 2 §11–§12; SBAs 281–300, 761–800; SOE cases 11–51\n\nThen: Part 3 (numbers and classifications) daily for the last ten days, and one full timed SOE circuit out loud with a colleague.\n\n1. The examination at a glance (Source: Regulations and Guidance for Candidates, April 2026, and the Important Announcement of Changes, April 2026.)\n\nThree components. Each must be passed independently. No compensation between components.\n\nComponent 1 — Written\n\nFormat 2 papers × 2 hours (4 hours total) Questions Single Best Answer (SBA), maximum 90 per paper (≈180 total) Marking Machine marked; standard set by panel of examiners Compensation Allowed between the two papers (but not with other components) Result Pass / fail against the standard-set cut score\n\nImplication: at ~180 questions in 240 minutes you have ~80 seconds per question. That is enough time to read a stem once, eliminate two options, and commit — but not enough to agonise. There is no negative marking in a standard-set SBA paper; answer every question.\n\nComponent 2 — OSCE (Practical & Procedural Knowledge and Skills)\n\nFormat Circuit of 10 stations, ~70 minutes total Timing 2 minutes reading outside the station + 5 minutes inside Content Either undertake a practical task on a simulated model, or describe the steps of a procedure to the examiner Marking 10 marks per station, checklist-based, equal weighting; a global mark is recorded for quality assurance Extras There may be a rest station and/or a pilot station (pilot marks do not count) Examiner Present at every station; will not interact unless the task requires it\n\nImplication: the checklist rewards completeness and sequence, not elegance. Five minutes is short. Narrate continuously, in order, and cover the whole pathway even if you cannot finish the physical task. Silence scores zero. If you finish early, stay in the station and add detail (post-operative instructions, what you would record, what could go wrong) — those are almost always checklist items."},{"sourcePage":7,"text":"Component 3 — Structured Oral Examination (SOE) on Clinical Scenarios\n\nFormat 8 stations, each 25 minutes = 10 minutes preparation + 15 minutes oral Break 5 minutes between cases Materials Patient history, clinical examination, investigations, radiographs, scans, photographs, study casts, wax-ups Notes You may make notes in the prep room and take them in; they are surrendered at the end Examiners Two per case, both will question you, both mark Marking 5-point scale × 4 domains = 20 marks per examiner per case Pass Mean of 3 across all domains and all cases\n\nThe four SOE domains — memorise these, they are the marking sheet:\n\n1. Clinical Interpretation and Reasoning 2. Clinical Decision Making and Treatment Planning (including consent) 3. Decision Making in Relation to Treatment Procedures (surgical placement & prosthetic reconstruc-tion) 4. Interpretation & Problem Solving of Clinical Outcomes\n\nThe single most valuable habit in this notebook: in your 10 minutes of preparation, write four headings on your paper — Interpret / Plan & Consent / Procedures / Outcomes — and fill each with three or four bullets. You will then have an answer ready for whichever domain the examiners open with, and you will have structured your own 15 minutes.\n\nChange from September 2026\n\nThe Communications OSCE has been discontinued. The examination is now the three components above only. Communication is not untested, however — it has migrated into the SOE, where consent, explaining risk, managing expectation and handling dissatisfaction are all explicitly blueprinted to the SCE column.\n\nEligibility (Regulation 4 / Appendix B self-declaration)\n\n• Primary dental qualification acceptable to Council • 3 years / 36 months post-qualification full-time experience (part-time equivalent within 6 years) • 15 cases treated with surgery personally performed or under supervision, 2 with ≥1 year follow-up, comprising:\n\n– 4 single-unit replacements – 5 multiple implants in different quadrants or 2 implants supporting a 3-unit bridge – ≥1 edentulous case – ≥5 cases with some evidence of minor augmentation\n\n• ≥30 hours implant CPD with reflection in the past 18 months • A completed quality improvement project related to implant dentistry in the past 18 months\n\nFailure to submit the signed declaration = ineligible. Maximum four attempts (Regulation 9.1). Failed compo-nents may be re-sat alone for one diet only; after that all three must be retaken (Regulation 14.2). Feedback must be requested within 28 days of results.\n\nSOE tip: your quality improvement project is fair game as a discussion opener under domain 4 (“Demonstrate an understanding of the importance of quality assurance measures”). Have a 60-second account ready: the problem, the standard you audited against, your sample, your finding, your change, and your re-audit.\n\n2. What the blueprint actually asks of you Appendix A groups content under five domains. This notebook maps to them as follows."},{"sourcePage":8,"text":"Blueprint domain Blueprint sub-headings Notebook sections Tested in\n\nW, SCE (some OS-P)\n\nClinical Interpretation & Reasoning History taking; Patient examination §1 Assessment & risk; §2 Examination, occlusion & space; §3 Investigations; §4 Anatomy; §5 Biology; §6 Materials & design\n\nW, SCE, some OS-P\n\nClinical decision making & treatment planning incl. consent\n\n§7 Planning, SAC & consent; §7.6 Prescribing; §12.5 Records & governance\n\nDiagnosis & clinical reasoning; Drug therapy & safe prescribing; Record keeping; Consent; Treatment planning\n\nW, OS-P, SCE\n\nDecision making in treatment procedures & basic procedural knowledge/skills\n\n§8 Surgery; §9 Minor augmentation; §10 Prosthodontics\n\nSurgical placement, handling of tissues & complications; Restoring implants & managing complications\n\nW, SCE\n\nInterpretation & problem solving of clinical outcomes Patient safety; Quality & maintenance; Complications & clinical outcomes\n\n§11 Complications; §12 Peri-implant disease, maintenance & outcomes\n\nGeneral professional skills (threaded throughout) §12.5–§12.7 SCE\n\nLearning outcomes that appear in all three columns (W + OS-P + SCE)\n\nThese are the highest-yield topics in the whole syllabus, because they can be examined three different ways. Know them cold:\n\n1. Diagnostic wax-ups — role in planning, discussion and execution (also the published sample OSCE station) 2. Selection of guides (radiographic and surgical) and transfer of planning information into surgery 3. Bone quality and quantity and their influence on the plan 4. Need for soft and hard tissue augmentation, and differentiating augmentation materials 5. Types of bone defect and how to manage them 6. Minor augmentation — appropriateness and execution 7. Flap design and atraumatic flap elevation 8. Choice of suture material and technique 9. One-stage vs two-stage decision making 10. Principles of site preparation 11. Infection control and aseptic technique 12. Abutment selection; impression techniques; screw- vs cement-retained; laboratory prescription; digital workflows 13. Local anaesthetics — range, side effects, methods; and management of drug interactions\n\nEvery one of these has a dedicated OSCE station in Part 6 of this notebook.\n\nWhat is explicitly out of scope\n\nYou are not examined on detailed knowledge or planning of:\n\n• Block onlay grafting (major autogenous augmentation) • External (lateral window) sinus floor elevation — you must still know what it is, when it is indicated, and that it warrants referral • Full-arch prosthodontic rehabilitation and reconstruction • Treatment planning with computerised scanning/planning software at an advanced level • Execution competency of any procedure (this is a knowledge, judgement and procedural-knowledge examination, not a clinical competency test — no patient work is undertaken, Guidance 4.2)\n\nBut — and candidates get caught here — you are expected to recognise when these are indicated and to say that the case falls outside the scope of a GDP with a special interest and requires referral. “I would refer” is a scoring answer when it is justified; it is a losing answer when used to dodge something a GDP should manage."},{"sourcePage":9,"text":"3. The FGDP training standards context The examination is calibrated against the UK training standards for implant dentistry (Training Standards in Implant Dentistry, FGDP(UK), the successor to the GDC-convened 2005/2008/2012 standards). Two things from that document recur in the SOE:\n\nImplant dentistry is divided into two levels: 1. Straightforward placement and/or restoration 2. Com-plex placement and/or restoration\n\nThe GDP with a special interest — i.e. you, for the purposes of this examination — works in level 1 and refers or team-manages level 2.\n\nThe thirteen skills a dentist must have (paraphrased from the standards) map almost one-to-one onto the blueprint: clinical assessment and risk–benefit analysis; judging case complexity and knowing when to refer; communication and valid consent at every stage; imaging in line with IR(ME)R; aseptic surgical technique; raising mucoperiosteal flaps and suturing; minor alveolar augmentation using autogenous bone or substitutes; appropriate pharmaceutical agents; conventional restorative procedures; straightfor-ward implant-supported restorative procedures; diagnosing and dealing with complications; monitoring and maintaining implants over time; record-keeping, documentation and audit.\n\nGDC Standards Principle 7 is quoted in the training standards and is quotable in your SOE: - 7.1 provide good quality care based on current evidence and authoritative guidance - 7.2 work within your knowledge, skills, professional competence and abilities - 7.3 update and develop your professional knowledge and skills throughout your working life\n\nSOE gold: when an examiner asks “would you treat this patient?”, a strong answer names the complexity level, applies SAC, references GDC Principle 7.2, and states what would have to change for you to take it on (e.g. “if I had a mentor available, or if the patient accepted a delayed protocol”).\n\n4. Reference key Throughout this notebook, references are abbreviated. Where a topic is best learned from a single source, that source is named after the section heading. Use this key to know which book to open.\n\nAbbrev. Full reference Best used for\n\nMisch Misch’s Contemporary Implant Dentistry, Resnik RR (ed.), 4th edn, Elsevier Bone density classification, biomechanics, treatment planning, anatomy, complications, medical assessment. The single best “one book” for the written paper. Lindhe Clinical Periodontology and Implant Dentistry, Lang NP & Lindhe J (eds), 6th edn, Wiley-Blackwell\n\nBiology of osseointegration, peri-implant tissues, peri-implant disease, ridge alterations after extraction, evidence base. The authority for anything biological. SAC The SAC Classification in Implant Dentistry, 2nd edn, Dawson A, Chen S, Buser D, Cordaro L, Martin W, Belser U (eds), ITI/Quintessence\n\nSingle-page algorithms — ideal for last-week revision and for structuring SOE answers\n\nCase complexity, normative and modifying factors, aesthetic risk. Learn this book — it is the language of the SOE. ITI TG ITI Treatment Guide, vols 1–13, Quintessence Vol 1 (aesthetic zone), Vol 2 (loading protocols), Vol 3 (posterior), Vol 5 (sinus floor elevation), Vol 7 (ridge augmentation), Vol 8 (biological/hardware complications), Vol 10 (implants in extraction sockets), Vol 11 (digital), Vol 13 (peri-implantitis) ITI DT ITI Decision Trees (immediate placement; atrophic posterior maxilla; sinus floor elevation; peri-implant mucositis & peri-implantitis)\n\nOIR Oral Implantology Review: A Study Guide Rapid factual recall, definitions, numbers CCID Clinical Cases in Implant Dentistry, Karimbux N & Weber H-P (eds), Wiley 2017\n\nCase-based rehearsal for the SOE. Chapters: 1 Examination & Diagnosis; 2 Implant Design; 3 Prosthetic Design; 4 Soft Tissue Management; 5 Ridge Site Preparation; then implant placement, complications, maintenance"},{"sourcePage":10,"text":"Abbrev. Full reference Best used for\n\nThe definitive scope document\n\nNID Notes in Implant Dentistry, Chu AKY et al., 1st edn 2021 Condensed evidence summaries; excellent for revision-density Termeie Periodontal Review Q&A, Termeie DA, Quintessence Periodontal underpinning, classification, wound healing; strong Q&A format PCS Periodontics: The Complete Summary Fast periodontal recall for perio-related SOE stations Mosea MCQs in Implant Dentistry, Mosea A, 2016 Written-paper practice; answers with short evidence notes DSMT Dental Science, Materials and Technology: MCQ in Implant Dentistry Materials, surfaces, metallurgy, cements, impression materials FGDP Training Standards in Implant Dentistry, FGDP(UK) Scope of practice, straightforward vs complex, referral, portfolio App A–E RCSEd Appendices A (Blueprint), B (Self-declaration), C (Sample SBAs), D (Sample OSCE), E (Sample SOE case)\n\nGuidelines you should be able to name in the SOE:\n\n• IR(ME)R 2017 (and IRR 2017) — justification, optimisation, referrer/practitioner/operator roles • FGDP(UK) / Royal College of Radiologists — Selection Criteria for Dental Radiography • SDCEP — Drug Prescribing for Dentistry; Management of Acute Dental Problems; Prevention and Manage-ment of Dental Caries • SDCEP / SIGN — Management of Dental Patients Taking Anticoagulants or Antiplatelet Drugs • SDCEP — Oral Health Management of Patients at Risk of Medication-Related Osteonecrosis of the Jaw (MRONJ) • NICE CG64 — Prophylaxis against infective endocarditis (antibiotic prophylaxis not routinely recom-mended) • 2017 World Workshop classification of periodontal and peri-implant diseases and conditions (Caton et al.; Berglundh et al.) • EFP S3-level treatment guideline for periodontitis (2020) and for peri-implant diseases (2023) • GDC Standards for the Dental Team, especially Principles 3 (consent), 4 (records/confidentiality), 7 (competence) • Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] — the legal test for consent • HTM 01-05 — decontamination in primary care dental practices"},{"sourcePage":11,"text":"How to read this notebook\n\nThis notebook uses a small, consistent set of visual signals. Learn them once and you can find what you need on any page without reading around it.\n\nThe coloured boxes\n\nTHE CASE\n\nSets the scene. In the OSCE and SOE sections, this box carries the clinical scenario — the patient, the history, the findings and the radiographs. Read it twice before you read anything else on the page.\n\nCANDIDATE INSTRUCTIONS\n\nTells you what you are being asked to do, in the wording a station would use. Identify the verb — carry out, describe, discuss, explain to the patient — because it tells you who you are talking to and what will be marked.\n\nDIRECTION OF THOUGHT\n\nThe two minutes of thinking that should happen before you open your mouth: the framework, the sequence, and the trap in this particular scenario. This is the part of the notebook worth re-reading on the morning of the examination.\n\nMODEL ANSWER\n\nA full answer, written to be spoken aloud, at the level expected of a general dentist with an interest in implant dentistry. It is not a model of the most sophisticated answer available — it is a model of the safest defensible one, delivered with insight into your own limits.\n\nEXAMINER CHECKLIST\n\n1. The marking points, written as a checklist 2. Rehearse against it, mark yourself honestly, and note which points you dropped 3. The last two or three points are almost always records, patient instructions and review — the marks candidates most often leave unclaimed\n\nKEY POINT\n\nKey point. A priority, a rule of thumb, or something to commit to memory verbatim.\n\nThe other signals\n\nSignal Meaning\n\nQ in a navy chip An examiner’s question in a structured oral case. The shaded box beneath it is the answer as you would say it. Small grey type under a heading The blueprint outcome that heading maps to, or a cross-reference to another part of the notebook. A navy number in the question bank The question number. In the digital version it is a link — click it to jump straight to the answer, and click the number again in Part 8 to come back. Thumb tabs on the right edge Which part you are in. They step down the page from Part 1 at the top to Part 8 at the foot, so you can find a part with the book closed."},{"sourcePage":12,"text":"Four ways to use the book As a course. Work Parts 2 and 3 in order alongside the six-week plan in the introduction, using the question bank block by block as you finish each area.\n\nAs a question bank. Work a block of the written paper under timed conditions — 80 seconds per question — mark it from Part 8, then read every explanation in that block, including the ones you answered correctly.\n\nAs a rehearsal script. Take one OSCE station or one structured oral case a day and deliver it out loud, against the clock, to a colleague holding the checklist. Reading a model answer silently builds recognition; saying it builds fluency, and only fluency survives the examination.\n\nAs a reference. Use the thumb tabs and the contents list at the front of each part. Part 3 (numbers and classifications) and Part 4 (which book, for what) are designed to be looked things up in rather than read through."}]},{"id":"technique","title":"Examination technique & eligibility","start":13,"end":17,"pages":[{"sourcePage":13,"text":"Examination technique\n\nHow to sit each component, the twelve commonest mistakes, and what to do the day before.\n\nIN THIS PART\n\n1.1 The written papers 11 1.2 The OSCE 11 1.3 The structured oral examination 11\n\n1.4 The twelve mistakes that fail candidates 12 1.5 The day before 12 1.6 How you are actually marked — the examiner’s side of the table 13"},{"sourcePage":14,"text":"PART 1 — EXAMINATION TECHNIQUE\n\n1.1 The written papers\n\n• ~180 SBAs in 240 minutes = ~80 seconds each. Do not linger. • Answer every question. There is no negative marking in a standard-set paper. • Read the last line of the stem first if the stem is long — it tells you what is actually being asked. • Look for the qualifier: most likely, first, best, least appropriate, initial. These change the answer. • Watch the units — the RCSEd sample question about surface roughness deliberately offers nanometres and micrometres. • Where two options both look right, ask which is the safest and most defensible at GDP level. That is usually the intended answer. • Flag and move on; return at the end. Do not change an answer without a clear reason.\n\n1.2 The OSCE\n\n• Use the 2 minutes to build a sequence, not to worry. Write three or four words on your hand-out if allowed. • Narrate continuously. A silent candidate scores nothing on a checklist. • Work in a fixed order: indication →preparation →procedure →completion →records and warnings. • If the task is physical and you are struggling, keep describing what you are doing and why — the checklist rewards knowledge as well as execution. • If you finish early, stay in the station and add: post-operative advice, what you would record, what could go wrong, when you would review. These are almost always unclaimed marks. • The examiner will not help you. Do not interpret silence as disapproval. • If you make an error, say so and correct it. Recovering visibly is better than pretending.\n\n1.3 The structured oral examination\n\n• Use the full 10 minutes of preparation. Write the four domain headings and fill them. Take the notes in. • Open with a framed summary, not a fact. • Signpost: “There are three issues here — first…, second…, third…” Examiners can only give marks for what they can identify. • Give the whole option list, including no treatment, before giving your recommendation. • Say SAC, say material risk, say what you would record, say when you would review and what you would measure. These are the phrases that map onto the mark sheet. • Know your limits out loud. “This is beyond what I would do as a general dentist with a special interest, so I would…” is a scoring answer, not an admission of weakness — but only when justified. • If you don’t know, say so and reason from principles. “I’m not certain of the exact figure, but the principle is…” scores far better than a confident invention. • If an examiner pushes back, listen. Sometimes they are testing whether you will defend a correct position; sometimes they are giving you a lifeline. Reconsider genuinely, then either hold your position with reasons or change it with reasons. Do not simply capitulate, and do not become defensive. • Watch the clock in your own answers. Fifteen minutes across four domains is under four minutes each. Do not spend eight minutes on the history."},{"sourcePage":15,"text":"1.4 The twelve mistakes that fail candidates\n\n1. Treating the examination as a specialist examination and over-reaching into complex procedures. 2. Never mentioning referral — or using it to avoid everything. 3. Giving a treatment plan without giving the options, including no treatment. 4. Forgetting consent — especially the origin of graft materials, and the maintenance burden. 5. Forgetting records — no candidate has ever lost marks for saying what they would record. 6. Forgetting maintenance and review — the answer does not end when the crown is fitted. 7. Not recognising peri-implantitis risk factors in the history in front of them. 8. Talking about implants before controlling disease and establishing prognosis. 9. Failing to be prosthetically driven — planning from bone rather than from the wax-up. 10. Reciting a memorised list rather than interpreting the specific case. 11. Being silent in an OSCE station. 12. Not answering the question that was actually asked.\n\n1.5 The day before\n\n• Re-read Part 3 (numbers and classifications) and the four ITI Decision Trees. • Re-read the four SOE domains and the four-heading preparation framework. • Re-read the three quotations at the front of this notebook about the level of the examination. • Check your passport (Regulation 15.1 — it is required at every registration session). • Sleep. You know more than you think you do."},{"sourcePage":16,"text":"APPENDIX — Quick self-declaration and eligibility checklist\n\nConfirm before the closing date (Regulation 4, Appendix B):\n\n□Primary dental qualification acceptable to Council □36 months post-qualification experience (or part-time equivalent within 6 years) □15 cases with surgery personally performed or under supervision □2 cases with ≥1 year follow-up □4 single-unit replacements □5 multiple implants in different quadrants or 2 implants supporting a 3-unit bridge □≥1 edentulous case □≥5 cases with evidence of minor augmentation □≥30 hours implant CPD with reflections in the past 18 months □Quality improvement project in implant dentistry in the past 18 months □Signed declaration emailed to implantdentistry@rcsed.ac.uk by the closing date □Passport (or acceptable photographic ID) ready for every registration session\n\nFailure to submit the signed statement makes you ineligible to sit.\n\n1.6 How you are actually marked — the examiner’s side of the table Everything above is about what you say. This section is about what happens to it once you have said it, because the marking instruments are not secret and knowing their shape changes how you answer. The College publishes the components and the blueprint; what follows is the ordinary anatomy of marking a clinical examination of this kind, written so that you can rehearse against it.\n\nThe written papers\n\nOne mark a question, no negative marking, and roughly 80 seconds a question if you divide the time by the paper. Two consequences follow, and candidates lose marks on both. First, never leave a blank — an eliminated-down guess is free. Second, do not audit your own paper twice; the second pass changes more right answers to wrong than the reverse. Flag the genuinely uncertain, answer everything, and revisit only the flags.\n\nThe OSCE: a checklist, and the “say it out loud” rule\n\nEach station is marked against a list of explicit points — in this notebook, ten to a station, which is the usual shape. The examiner is not writing an impression; they are ticking things they heard or saw.\n\nWATCH OUT\n\nThe single rule that matters: the mark is awarded when you say the thing, not when the examiner believes you know it. A step performed silently, an instrument used without being named, a force applied without being quantified, a finding noticed but not spoken — none of them scores. This is why the discussion stations, where everything must be verbalised, are harder than they look, and why narrating is not showing off but the mechanism by which you are paid.\n\nThree habits follow directly from a checklist format:\n\nFrame in the first thirty seconds. “I will check the history and consent, work through X, Y and Z, and finish with what I would record and what I would tell the patient.” You have just told the examiner where each tick is coming from, and they will wait for you rather than prompt.\n\nGive one clause per point, not one paragraph. A checklist gives no extra credit for elaboration, and elaboration is what runs the clock out before the last three points.\n\nReserve the final thirty seconds for records, patient instructions and review. They sit at the end of nearly every checklist and are the marks most often left unclaimed."},{"sourcePage":17,"text":"The structured oral: four domains, five marks each\n\nThe oral is marked on the four published domains, five marks in each, awarded by each examiner inde-pendently — twenty marks per examiner per case. The pass standard is conventionally set around a mean of 3 across the domains and cases, which is worth understanding precisely: you do not have to be brilliant anywhere, but a 1 in one domain has to be bought back by a 5 somewhere else, and 5s are rare.\n\nMark What it describes\n\n5 Comprehensive, fluent and prioritised. Volunteers the material risks, the limits of their own competence and what they would record, without prompting. Cites evidence or guidance where it genuinely bears on the decision. 4 Sound and safe throughout, well organised, with only minor omissions that do not affect patient safety. 3 The pass. Safe and defensible. Reaches the right decision, though prompting is needed for some elements. Knows when to refer. 2 Incomplete or poorly prioritised. Gets to a workable answer only with substantial prompting, or omits an important safety element. 1 Serious gaps. Unsafe reasoning, or fails to recognise a case beyond their competence. 0 No useful response, or actively dangerous advice.\n\nRead the difference between 3 and 5 again: it is not knowledge, it is who raised the point. The same content volunteered scores where the same content extracted does not. That is the whole argument for the habits this notebook keeps repeating — say the risk before you are asked for it, state your competence limit before the examiner probes it, and finish every answer with what you would record and what you would say to the patient.\n\nWhat the examiner is doing while you talk\n\nExaminers in a component of this kind work to a recognisable pattern, and each element of it is an oppor-tunity if you see it coming.\n\nThey let you open uninterrupted for about a minute, then question briskly. The opening is the only monologue you get. After that, being moved on is normal and is not a sign you were wrong — a candidate allowed to talk at length on their strongest ground has not been tested, so an examiner who cuts you off is running the examination properly.\n\nThey prompt — and they record that they prompted. A prompted correct answer is a 3, not a 5. The neutral prompts are recognisable: “What else?”, “Why that one?”, “What would change your mind?”, “What would you record?”, “Where does that sit on the SAC classification?” When you hear one, you have just been told that something obvious is missing from your answer — usually risk, records, the patient’s words, or the referral threshold.\n\nThey release information deliberately. A new radiograph, a result, a change in the history part-way through a case is a test of whether you will revise your position out loud. Say what it changes: “That changes my differential.” Silence there scores nothing.\n\nThey take you to the edge of your competence and check that you notice. This is the most important sentence in this section. Failing to recognise a case that should be referred is the commonest reason a candidate does not pass, and it outweighs any single factual gap. The examiner is not trying to catch you out by pushing towards block grafting or full-arch work; they are checking that you stop, name the complexity, and refer. Stopping is the answer.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nRehearse against the instrument, not just the syllabus. For the OSCE, work the run sheets in Part 6 out loud against a five-minute timer. For the oral, have a colleague mark you on the four domains using the descriptors above — and ask them to note, for every point you made, whether you volunteered it or they had to ask. The count of prompts is a better predictor of your result than the content of your answers."}]},{"id":"core-contents","title":"PART 2 · Core revision notes (contents and overview)","start":18,"end":20,"pages":[{"sourcePage":18,"text":"PART 2 • NOTES\nPART 2\nCore revision notes\nTwelve sections mapped to Appendix A — the learning outcomes and blueprint. Read these\nfirst, and return to them between question blocks.\nIN THIS PART\n§1 Patient assessment, history and risk\n18\n1.1 The structure of an implant history\n18\n1.2 Medical risk — absolute vs relative\n18\n1.3 Local risk factors\n19\n1.4 Psychological, social and cultural factors (blueprint-explicit)\n20\n1.5 Structuring your answer when asked ”what in the history is\nrelevant?”\n20\n§2 Examination, occlusion and space\n21\n2.1 Extra-oral examination\n21\n2.2 Intra-oral examination\n21\n2.3 Occlusal assessment — the examinable framework\n21\n2.4 Space analysis — the numbers you must know\n22\n2.5 Aesthetic assessment and aesthetic risk\n23\n§3 Investigations\n24\n3.1 Justifying imaging — IR(ME)R language\n24\n3.2 Which image, when\n24\n3.3 The diagnostic wax-up — the highest-yield single topic\n25\n3.4 Radiographic and surgical guides\n25\n§4 Surgical anatomy relevant to implant placement\n27\n4.1 Mandible\n27\n4.2 Maxilla\n28\n4.3 Ridge changes after extraction (learn the numbers)\n28\n4.4 Anatomy of the peri-implant tissues\n28\n§5 Osseointegration, bone biology and healing\n29\n5.1 Definitions you must be able to quote\n29\n5.2 Albrektsson’s six factors for osseointegration (a classic\nSBA)\n29\n5.3 The healing cascade at the implant interface\n29\n5.4 Bone quality and quantity classifications\n30\n5.5 Success and survival criteria\n30\n§6 Implant materials, design and surfaces\n31\n6.1 Implant body materials\n31\n6.2 Surface topography and treatments\n31\n6.3 Macro-design\n32\n6.4 Implant–abutment connection\n32\n6.5 Prosthetic component materials\n32\n6.6 Differences between implant systems — how to answer\n33\n§7 Treatment planning, case complexity and consent\n34\n7.1 The treatment planning sequence\n34\n7.2 Options for tooth replacement — always give all of them\n34\n7.3 The SAC classification — the language of the examination\n35\n7.4 Referral — when and how\n35\n7.5 Consent — the second-biggest source of SOE marks\n36\n7.6 Drug therapy and safe prescribing\n36\n§8 Surgical placement — decision making and\nprocedural knowledge\n39\n8.1 Infection control and aseptic technique\n39\n8.2 Flap design\n39\n8.3 Site preparation — the principles\n40\n8.4 Three-dimensional implant position — the rules that\ngenerate SBAs\n40\n8.5 One-stage vs two-stage; and the timing protocols\n41\n8.6 Immediate placement — the details that separate\ncandidates\n42\n8.7 Suturing — materials and techniques (blueprinted W +\nOS-P + SCE)\n42\n8.8 Post-operative care\n43\n§9 Minor hard and soft tissue augmentation\n44\n9.1 What counts as ”minor” at GDP level\n44\n9.2 The biology: GBR and the PASS principle\n44\n9.3 Bone graft materials — the comparison table\n45\n9.4 Barrier membranes\n45\n9.5 Bone defect classification and management\n46\n9.6 Transcrestal (osteotome) sinus floor elevation — the one\nyou may do\n46\n9.7 Soft tissue augmentation\n47\n9.8 Ridge split / expansion\n47\n9.9 The limitations to state whenever asked about\naugmentation\n47\n§10 Restoring implants — prosthodontics and occlusion\n48\n10.1 The prosthetic options\n48\n10.2 Impression techniques\n48\n10.3 Jaw registration\n49\n10.4 Abutment selection (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE)\n49\n10.5 Screw-retained vs cement-retained (blueprinted W +\nOS-P + SCE — expect it)\n49\n10.6 If you must cement — how to avoid residual cement\n50\n10.7 Implant occlusion — ”implant-protective occlusion”\n50\n"},{"sourcePage":19,"text":"PART 2 • NOTES\n10.8 Screw mechanics — preload, torque, loosening\n50\n10.9 Laboratory communication (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE)\n51\n10.10 Managing a mis-fitting prosthesis or framework (a\nnamed blueprint line, marked OS-P)\n51\n10.11 Digital workflows (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE)\n51\n§11 Complications and their management\n52\n11.1 A framework for any complication question\n52\n11.2 Intra-operative complications\n52\n11.3 Early post-operative complications\n53\n11.4 Late biological complications\n53\n11.5 Mechanical/technical complications\n54\n11.6 Aesthetic complications\n54\n11.7 Managing complaints and dissatisfaction (blueprinted to\nSCE)\n54\n§12 Peri-implant health and disease, maintenance and\noutcomes\n55\n12.1 The 2017 World Workshop definitions — learn them\nverbatim-ish\n55\n12.2 Risk factors and risk indicators\n55\n12.3 Diagnosis in practice\n55\n12.4 Treatment — follow the ITI decision tree structure\n55\n12.5 Maintenance, records and outcome monitoring\n57\n12.6 Quality improvement and audit (entrance requirement\nand blueprint content)\n57\n12.7 Professionalism, confidentiality and the team\n58\nM Imp Dent RCSEd | Revision Notebook\n16\n"},{"sourcePage":20,"text":"PART 2 • NOTES\nPART 2 — CORE REVISION NOTES\nM Imp Dent RCSEd | Revision Notebook\n17\n"}]},{"id":"assessment","title":"§1 · Patient assessment, history and risk","start":21,"end":23,"pages":[{"sourcePage":21,"text":"§1 Patient assessment, history and risk\n\nBlueprint: Clinical Interpretation & Reasoning — History taking. Tested W + SCE. Key reading: Misch ch. “Medical Evaluation of the Dental Implant Patient”; Lindhe (systemic risk); SAC ch. 2; CCID ch. 1 Case 2.\n\n1.1 The structure of an implant history The examiners want to hear a structured, comprehensive history — not a list of questions but a reasoned sequence in which each element changes what you do next.\n\nElement What you ask Why it changes the plan\n\nAbsolute vs relative contraindication; MRONJ risk; healing capacity\n\nPresenting complaint In the patient’s own words; how long; what triggered attendance now Distinguishes function-driven from aesthetics-driven from pain-driven cases; sets the outcome you will be judged against History of presenting complaint Why the tooth was lost, when, previous treatment, previous failures Loss from periodontitis, endodontic failure, fracture or trauma predicts residual bone and infection risk Medical history Systematic; including drugs, allergies, previous radiotherapy, bisphosphonates/antiresorptives, smoking, alcohol, recreational drugs\n\nCompliance is the strongest modifiable predictor of long-term implant success\n\nDental history Attendance pattern, previous perio treatment, compliance with hygiene, previous restorative failures, orthodontics, parafunction\n\nUnrealistic expectation is a contraindication in its own right\n\nSocial history Occupation, smoking (pack-years), alcohol, stress, ability to attend, funding Cost and time commitment are part of valid consent Psychological / expectation What does “success” mean to them? Have they seen images? Any previous dissatisfaction?\n\nBlueprint explicitly lists “psychological, social and cultural factors”\n\nFamily/cultural Cultural attitudes to tooth loss, dietary practices, religious observance affecting appointments/fasting\n\nExam phrase to have ready: “I take a structured history under those headings, but I interpret it in the light of the patient’s own concerns — my first question is always what they want the treatment to achieve, because that becomes the outcome measure I audit myself against.”\n\n1.2 Medical risk — absolute vs relative There are very few absolute contraindications. Learn the short list; everything else is relative (i.e. it modifies risk, timing, and consent, but does not by itself forbid treatment).\n\nAbsolute / near-absolute (defer or do not treat)\n\n• Recent myocardial infarction or CVA (conventionally defer elective surgery ~6 months; take cardiol-ogy advice) • Uncontrolled/severe cardiac disease — unstable angina, uncontrolled arrhythmia, severe valvular disease • Profound immunosuppression or active haematological malignancy; neutropenia; uncontrolled bleeding disorder • Active intravenous bisphosphonate / antiresorptive therapy for malignancy, or established MRONJ • Head and neck radiotherapy to the implant site, particularly ≥50–60 Gy, and especially within the first year (risk of osteoradionecrosis); requires specialist/hospital care • Uncontrolled diabetes (see below) — defer until controlled • Active substance misuse / non-engagement, or psychiatric illness precluding consent • Incomplete skeletal growth — implants behave as ankylosed teeth and become infra-occluded as the alveolus develops. Do not place in growing patients; wait for cessation of growth (typically ~late teens"},{"sourcePage":22,"text":"for females, later for males) confirmed by serial cephalometrics/hand-wrist radiographs or by clinical stability over 6–12 months.\n\nRelative — the ones examiners love\n\nCondition Effect Practical management\n\nSmoking Roughly 2–2.5× increased implant failure; markedly increased risk of peri-implantitis, marginal bone loss and graft/GBR failure; impairs perfusion and neutrophil function\n\nLiaise with GP; obtain a recent HbA1c; morning appointments; ensure the patient has eaten and taken medication; consider antibiotic cover in poorly controlled cases and delay loading\n\nCessation advice with very brief intervention; ideally stop ≥1 week pre-op and ≥8 weeks post-op; document the discussion and that success rates are lower Diabetes mellitus Well-controlled (HbA1c generally < 7%/53 mmol/mol, and certainly < 8%/64) implants perform close to normal; poorly controlled impairs healing, increases infection and peri-implantitis\n\nOral bisphosphonates (e.g. alendronate) Low MRONJ risk; SDCEP: manage as “at risk”, no drug holiday required routinely\n\nExplain risk, atraumatic technique, primary closure, review healing, document IV bisphosphonates / denosumab / antiangiogenics for malignancy High MRONJ risk Elective implants contraindicated; refer/liaise with oncology Anticoagulants / antiplatelets Bleeding risk Follow SDCEP: for warfarin, check INR within 24 h (72 h if stable) and treat if INR < 4.0; for DOACs, consider delaying/omitting the morning dose for higher-bleeding-risk procedures after liaison; do not stop antiplatelets; use local haemostasis (sutures, packing, oxidised cellulose, tranexamic mouthwash where indicated) Bleeding disorders Haemophilia, von Willebrand’s Haemophilia centre liaison; factor cover Corticosteroids / immunosuppressants Impaired healing, infection, adrenal suppression Liaise with physician; consider steroid cover for major procedures; meticulous asepsis Osteoporosis Bone quality affected but osseointegration generally achievable Main issue is the drug, not the disease\n\nPrevious radiotherapy Hypovascular, hypocellular, hypoxic bone Specialist referral; hyperbaric oxygen is controversial and not a GDP decision Sjögren’s / xerostomia Mucosal fragility, caries in remaining teeth Implants may actually be favourable vs a mucosa-borne denture Bruxism/parafunction Mechanical, not biological, risk: screw loosening, fracture of screws/abutments/ceramics, overload\n\nSplint, occlusal design, avoid cantilevers, wider/more implants, monolithic materials Periodontitis (history of) Strongest patient-level predictor of peri-implantitis Must be treated and stable first; enrolled in supportive periodontal therapy Pregnancy Elective — defer Second trimester if urgent; avoid radiographs unless justified\n\nWritten-paper trap: “The most significant risk factor for peri-implantitis is…” →a history of periodontitis (with smoking and poor plaque control close behind). “The most significant risk factor for early implant failure is…” → smoking / poor bone quality / lack of primary stability, depending on the stem — read carefully whether it asks about early (pre-osseointegration) or late (biological) failure.\n\n1.3 Local risk factors\n\n• Bone volume — height, width, and the relationship to vital structures (§4) • Bone quality — Lekholm & Zarb types I–IV; Misch D1–D4 (§5.4) • Soft tissue phenotype — thin vs thick; width of keratinised mucosa • Infection at the site — periapical, periodontal, residual root, retained cyst • Adjacent teeth — prognosis, angulation, root proximity, restorative status • Occlusion and parafunction — over-eruption, drifting, loss of space, wear facets • Smile line and lip dynamics • Access and mouth opening — a frequently forgotten OSCE/SOE point for second and third molars; you need vertical access for drills and handpieces"},{"sourcePage":23,"text":"• Oral hygiene and dexterity — including cleansability of the planned prosthesis\n\n1.4 Psychological, social and cultural factors (blueprint-explicit)\n\n• Identify the “difficult” aesthetic patient: brings photographs of others’ smiles; multiple previous dissatisfactions; focus on a defect invisible to you; unwillingness to accept any compromise; demands guarantees. • Consider body dysmorphic features and, where suspected, decline elective aesthetic treatment and seek advice. • Cultural factors are examinable: dietary and fasting practices affecting appointment timing and post-operative analgesia/antibiotic timing; attitudes to gold/metal display; the meaning of tooth loss; family involvement in decision-making; language and the use of a professional interpreter (not a family member) for consent. • Capacity: assess decision-specific capacity; where absent, act in the patient’s best interests under the relevant Mental Capacity legislation — but remember that elective implant treatment for a patient lacking capacity is rarely justifiable.\n\n1.5 Structuring your answer when asked “what in the history is relevant?” Use this four-step and you will never be caught blank:\n\n1. What increases the risk of failure of osseointegration? (smoking, uncontrolled diabetes, irradiated bone, poor bone quality, infection) 2. What increases the risk of biological complications later? (periodontitis history, plaque control, smoking, diabetes, no maintenance, non-cleansable prosthesis, residual cement) 3. What increases the risk of mechanical complications? (parafunction, cantilevers, limited restorative space, opposing dentition, previous fractures) 4. What affects consent and expectation? (aesthetics, cost, time, occupation, previous experience, psy-chological factors)"}]},{"id":"exam","title":"§2 · Examination, occlusion and space","start":24,"end":26,"pages":[{"sourcePage":24,"text":"§2 Examination, occlusion and space\n\nBlueprint: Clinical Interpretation & Reasoning — Patient examination; the words occlusion, interocclusal and intra-occlusal space, and aesthetic concerns appear repeatedly and are marked in both W and SCE. Key reading: Misch (treatment planning and occlusion chapters); CCID ch. 1 Case 4; SAC (prosthodontic classification); NID (occlusion).\n\n2.1 Extra-oral examination\n\n• Facial form, symmetry, profile and skeletal classification (I / II / III) — a Class III skeletal base changes implant angulation, prosthetic space and the achievable occlusal scheme, and is a common SOE prompt in edentulous cases • Lip support — is it currently provided by a denture flange? If so, a fixed implant prosthesis cannot replicate it, and this must be discussed before treatment • Smile line / lip dynamics — low, medium, high; high smile line is the single biggest aesthetic risk modifier • TMJ and muscles of mastication — clicking, crepitus, deviation, tenderness; masseteric hypertrophy suggests parafunction • Maximal mouth opening and inter-incisal access • Lymph nodes, salivary glands, skin, neurological function (baseline lip/chin sensation must be recorded before any mandibular surgery — a medico-legal essential)\n\n2.2 Intra-oral examination\n\n• Soft tissues — mucosal health, oral mucosal lesions, frenal attachments, vestibular depth, floor-of-mouth height • Periodontal assessment — BPE for screening, then full 6-point pocket charting where BPE ≥3, plaque and bleeding scores, mobility, furcations, recession • Peri-implant assessment where implants exist — probing depths, BOP, suppuration, mobility of pros-thesis vs implant, radiographic bone level • Caries, endodontic status, tooth wear, restorative prognosis of every tooth • Edentulous ridge assessment — width, height, contour, undercuts, knife-edge morphology, buccal concavity (palpate!), keratinised tissue width, ridge relationship • Occlusion — see below\n\nCoordinated approach (blueprint wording). Explain that you “target the detailed examination from the symptoms and history”: a patient complaining of a loose crown gets a detailed prosthetic/occlusal assessment; one com-plaining of discharge gets a detailed peri-implant and radiographic assessment. Don’t recite a generic list — say what you are looking for and why.\n\n2.3 Occlusal assessment — the examinable framework Static: - Incisal relationship and Angle classification; skeletal base - Overjet and overbite — deep overbite restricts restorative space and loads anterior implants adversely - Crossbite (a crossbite in Q2/Q3 is a classic SOE complication) — determines the buccolingual position of implants and whether the occlusal scheme can be conventional - Centric relation vs maximum intercuspation; presence of a slide - Over-eruption and drifting of unopposed/adjacent teeth — the classic 46-over-erupted-into-the-space scenario - Wear facets, tooth surface loss, cracks, abfractions\n\nDynamic: - Canine guidance vs group function; working and non-working interferences - Protrusive guidance, anterior guidance, presence of an “envelope of function” - Parafunction — masseteric hypertro-phy, scalloped tongue, linea alba, cervical abfraction, fractured restorations, wear patterns not explained by diet\n\nVertical dimension: - OVD (occlusal vertical dimension) and RVD (rest vertical dimension); freeway space = RVD −OVD, normally 2–4 mm - Methods to measure freeway space (frequently asked, see OSCE 15): Willis gauge or two marks (nose/chin) with dividers or a ruler; phonetics (“Emma”, “Mississippi” — closest speaking space); swallowing then relaxing; patient at rest, unsupported head, relaxed lips"},{"sourcePage":25,"text":"2.4 Space analysis — the numbers you must know\n\nMesiodistal space\n\nRequirement Value\n\nImplant to adjacent tooth ≥1.5 mm at the bone crest Implant to adjacent implant ≥3 mm Minimum mesiodistal space for a standard (Ø4.1 mm) implant ≈7 mm Minimum for a narrow (Ø3.3 mm) implant ≈5.5–6 mm Two adjacent implants (Ø4.1) ≈14 mm\n\nWhy: the saucerisation/lateral bone remodelling around the implant neck (~1.0–1.5 mm) will consume the interproximal bone; if two remodelling craters overlap, the interproximal crest drops and the papilla is lost.\n\nTarnow’s papilla rules (learn these; classic SBA material): - Distance from contact point to bone crest: ≤5 mm →papilla present ~100%; 6 mm →~56%; 7 mm →~27% - Inter-implant horizontal distance <3 mm →mean crestal bone loss ~1.04 mm; ≥3 mm →~0.45 mm\n\nBuccolingual space\n\nRequirement Value\n\nFacial bone thickness after placement ≥1.5–2 mm (aesthetic zone: ≥2 mm) Palatal/lingual bone ≥1 mm Total ridge width for a Ø4.1 mm implant ≈6.5–7 mm\n\nVertical / restorative space\n\nRestoration Minimum interocclusal (restorative) space\n\nSingle screw-retained crown (from implant platform to opposing) ≈5–6 mm (4 mm abutment/interface + ~2 mm ceramic occlusal thickness) Cement-retained crown ≈7–8 mm (needs ≥4–5 mm abutment height for retention) Bar-retained overdenture ≈12–15 mm Locator/stud overdenture ≈8–10 mm Fixed full-arch (hybrid) prosthesis ≈12–15 mm\n\nIf there is insufficient space: options are enameloplasty/occlusal adjustment or restoration of the over-erupted opposing tooth, orthodontic intrusion, elective devitalisation and crown-lengthening/shortening of the antagonist, increasing the OVD (only within the freeway space and with a Dahl/appliance trial), or alveoloplasty/crest reduction at surgery to create prosthetic space. In the edentulous case, alveoloplasty is often the correct answer.\n\nBone height relative to vital structures\n\nStructure Safety margin\n\nInferior alveolar canal ≥2 mm above the superior border of the canal Mental foramen / anterior loop ≥2 mm distal, and beware an anterior loop of up to 3–4 mm (sometimes more) — plan ≥5 mm anterior to the radiographic foramen if you have no CBCT Maxillary sinus floor Leave intact, or elevate; transcrestal SFE where residual height >6 mm and a flat sinus floor Nasal floor ≥1 mm Incisive (nasopalatine) canal Do not engage; may require enucleation and grafting (a specialist decision) Adjacent root ≥1.5 mm"},{"sourcePage":26,"text":"2.5 Aesthetic assessment and aesthetic risk Aesthetic risk assessment (ERA — ITI): low / medium / high across: medical status; smoking; patient aesthetic expectations; lip line; gingival biotype/phenotype; shape of tooth crowns; infection at the site; bone level at adjacent teeth; restorative status of neighbouring teeth; width of the edentulous span; soft tissue anatomy; bone anatomy of the alveolar crest.\n\nHigh-risk features to name aloud in an SOE: high lip line, thin scalloped phenotype, triangular crown form, bone loss on the adjacent tooth, multiple adjacent missing teeth, active infection, and high patient expectation.\n\nA classic anterior scenario — a young patient in a public-facing occupation missing a 22: the triangular crown form of 23 vs rectangular 21, narrow ridge, and the patient’s occupation are all aesthetic risk elevators — say them explicitly. Triangular crowns have a more coronal contact point and a longer papilla to fill, so black-triangle risk is high."}]},{"id":"investigations","title":"§3 · Investigations","start":27,"end":29,"pages":[{"sourcePage":27,"text":"§3 Investigations\n\nBlueprint: “Assess the need and justification for additional and appropriate investigations… radiographs, study casts, blood tests… in planning treatment” and “the role and importance of diagnostic wax-ups” — the latter tested in all three components. Key reading: Misch (radiographic evaluation); FGDP/RCR Selection Criteria; ITI TG vol 11 (digital).\n\n3.1 Justifying imaging — IR(ME)R language Every radiographic answer should contain three words: justification, optimisation, limitation.\n\n• Justification — the exposure must do more good than harm, for this patient, answering a specific ques-tion you cannot answer another way. • Optimisation (ALARP) — smallest field of view, appropriate resolution, rectangular collimation, cor-rect kV/mA, thyroid protection where appropriate. • Limitation — dose constraints; do not repeat what already exists.\n\nRoles under IR(ME)R: the referrer (supplies clinical data), the IR(ME)R practitioner (justifies), the operator (carries out any practical aspect). In practice a GDP may be all three — say so, and say you are entitled and trained (adequate training under the regulations) to act in those roles.\n\nEvery image must be reported — a written radiological report of the whole image, not just the region of interest, entered in the notes. This is an easy checklist mark in an OSCE.\n\n3.2 Which image, when\n\nImage Use in implant dentistry Limitations\n\n2-D only; no buccolingual information; geometric distortion if not parallel\n\nPeriapical (paralleling, with holder) Baseline and follow-up bone levels; adjacent tooth pathology; verifying component seating; assessing residual cement\n\nBitewings Caries, restorative status, crestal bone in posterior sextants Not for implant planning\n\nMagnification (≈1.2–1.3×, not uniform), no buccolingual dimension, ghosting, superimposition\n\nPanoramic (OPT/DPT) Screening the whole jaw; gross vertical bone height; sinus and canal position; pathology; over-eruption; also used to identify bruxism-related wear and condylar changes\n\nHigher dose than 2-D; artefact from metal (beam hardening/streak); must be justified case-by-case — never “routine”; the whole volume must be reported\n\nCBCT Buccolingual width, ridge morphology, buccal concavity, proximity to IAN/mental loop/sinus, bone density approximation, planning guided surgery, assessing existing implants when 2-D is inconclusive\n\nNeeds facebow + interocclusal record for anything beyond simple cases\n\nStudy casts, mounted Space analysis, occlusal analysis, arch relationship, diagnostic wax-up, surgical/radiographic guide fabrication\n\nScan-body accuracy across long spans; needs training\n\nIntraoral scan / digital workflow Casts, wax-up, guide design, restoration design; superimposition with CBCT for planning\n\nClinical photographs Consent, aesthetics, records, medico-legal, monitoring Consent for images required\n\nNot routine\n\nBlood tests HbA1c (diabetes control), FBC, INR, vitamin D, bone profile where indicated; liaise with the GP rather than requesting blindly\n\nInvasive, crude — CBCT has largely replaced it\n\nBone mapping / ridge mapping Old-fashioned but valid: LA + calibrated probe through the mucosa to plot ridge width\n\nWhen is CBCT justified? When the answer will change what you do: suspected proximity to the IAN or sinus, suspected buccal concavity (especially the posterior mandible with a lingual undercut), assessing the volume for augmentation, planning guided surgery, atypical anatomy, and assessing an implant with unexplained symptoms where periapicals are inconclusive."},{"sourcePage":28,"text":"Radiographic interpretation drill for the SOE. Report in this order and you will not miss anything: (1) type of image, date, quality/diagnostic acceptability; (2) the whole image — teeth, restorations, caries, periapical status, bone levels, sinus, canal, condyles, any incidental finding; (3) the region of interest — vertical height, mesiodis-tal width, crestal morphology, angulation, relationship to structures; (4) what I still cannot tell (buccolingual width, soft tissue) and what further imaging or investigation I would justify.\n\n3.3 The diagnostic wax-up — the highest-yield single topic\n\nBlueprinted to* W + OS-P + SCE *and used as RCSEd’s own published sample OSCE station (Appendix D).\n\nPurpose (say all six): 1. Determines whether the desired tooth position is achievable — i.e. is the case prosthetically driven? 2. Assesses space in three dimensions, including interocclusal space and the restorative space at the planned platform level 3. Evaluates the occlusal scheme and vertical dimension before committing 4. Communicates with the patient — converted to a trial/mock-up or provisional to test appearance and phonetics and to support valid consent 5. Becomes the blueprint for the radio-graphic guide and then the surgical guide — the mechanism by which planning is transferred to surgery 6. Communicates with the laboratory and the rest of the team\n\nPractical steps for requesting one (this is the OSCE checklist): 1. Consent, and take clinical pho-tographs 2. Primary impressions in stock trays / intraoral scan; special tray if needed →definitive impressions in an appropriate material 3. Interocclusal record at the correct vertical dimension; facebow transfer where the occlusal scheme is being altered 4. Mount on a semi-adjustable articulator 5. Record shade, midline, incisal plane, smile line, lip support, occlusal plane 6. Written laboratory prescription — patient identifier, your details, date required, exactly what you want: teeth to be waxed, desired tooth form/size/position, whether to alter the vertical dimension and by how much, duplicate in clear acrylic for a radiographic guide with radiopaque markers (gutta-percha/barium sulphate 20–30%), request a vacuum-formed matrix/putty index for chairside mock-up and for the provisional 7. Specify return date, materials, and that the case be disinfected; comply with the Medical Devices Regulations (custom-made device statement) and record the lab’s details 8. On return: verify the wax-up in the mouth (mock-up), check with the patient, adjust, re-photograph, then convert to the guide\n\nModel sentence: “The wax-up is not a laboratory nicety — it is how I decide whether this is a restoratively driven case that I can treat, and it is the physical link between my plan and my drill.”\n\n3.4 Radiographic and surgical guides\n\nGuide type Description Use\n\nWorn during CBCT/OPT to relate planned tooth position to bone\n\nRadiographic guide Duplicate of the wax-up in clear acrylic with radiopaque markers (gutta-percha, barium sulphate, metal spheres of known diameter for calibration)\n\nNon-limiting (tooth-position) guide Shows planned tooth position only Simple cases; keeps you restoratively driven Partially limiting Indicates entry point and angle (e.g. a drilled channel) Most GDP-level cases\n\nPartially vs fully edentulous\n\nCompletely limiting (fully guided) Sleeve-guided drills; static guided surgery from merged CBCT + scan Accuracy at the cost of flexibility; needs sufficient mouth opening; you must know the concept, not the software detail Support Tooth-supported (most accurate) > mucosa-supported (needs fixation pins) > bone-supported\n\nAccuracy expectations for static guided surgery (approximate, from systematic reviews): mean angular deviation ~2–4°, entry point ~1 mm, apex ~1.3–1.6 mm. Therefore always keep a safety margin of ≈2 mm from vital structures even with a guide.\n\nLimitations to state: cost; laboratory/scan time; error accumulates through impression, scan, merge, man-ufacture and seating; cannot compensate for a poorly fitting or unstable guide; restricted irrigation and heat generation within sleeves; limited access posteriorly; and a guide does not remove the need to know the anatomy."},{"sourcePage":29,"text":"PART 2 • NOTES\nM Imp Dent RCSEd | Revision Notebook\n26\n"}]},{"id":"anatomy","title":"§4 · Surgical anatomy relevant to implant placement","start":30,"end":31,"pages":[{"sourcePage":30,"text":"§4 Surgical anatomy relevant to implant placement\n\nBlueprint: “Apply knowledge of the anatomy of the mandible, maxilla and related structures and how this may influence implant treatment” (W). Key reading: Misch (anatomy and avoidance of complications); The Mental Foramen and Nerve: Clinical and Anatomic Factors.\n\n4.1 Mandible Inferior alveolar nerve (IAN) and canal - Runs from the mandibular foramen (lingula) forward, usually lingually then centrally then buccally, to divide into the mental and incisive branches. - Safety margin ≥2 mm above the canal roof. Add the imaging error: OPT magnification and the 2 mm rule together are why CBCT is justified when the planned length approaches the canal. - Bifid canals occur in a small percentage — a specific reason to look at the CBCT rather than assume.\n\nMental foramen and the anterior loop - Typically located apical to, or between, the premolars, most often at/near the second premolar apex. - The anterior loop — the portion of the nerve that runs anterior to the foramen before turning back — is present in a substantial proportion of patients and commonly measures 1–3 mm, occasionally up to ~5 mm or more. - Practical rule: without CBCT, keep ≥5 mm anterior to the anterior border of the radiographic mental foramen; with CBCT, measure it. - Incisive canal branch continues anteriorly and can cause bleeding and, occasionally, altered sensation — the anterior mandible is not automatically “safe”.\n\nLingual anatomy — the genuinely dangerous part - Submandibular fossa (lingual undercut) at the posterior mandible: the ridge crest can be lingually inclined over a concavity, so a drill following the apparent long axis perforates the lingual plate. Always palpate the lingual concavity and consider CBCT. - Sublingual artery (from the lingual artery) and submental artery (from the facial artery) anastomose in the anterior floor of mouth and enter through the lingual foramina of the genial region. Perforation of the lingual plate anteriorly can cause a rapidly expanding floor-of-mouth haematoma with airway obstruction — a genuine emergency (see §11.2). - Lingual nerve — at risk from lingual flap reflection and from lingually placed incisions/releasing incisions in the third-molar/retromolar region. - Mylohyoid ridge and muscle attachment, genial tubercles, mental spines.\n\nOther mandibular considerations - Cortical thickness: the anterior mandible has dense cortical bone (D1/D2) — excellent primary stability, but a higher risk of overheating; use sharp drills, copious ir-rigation, intermittent pressure, and consider cortical bone tapping/counter-sinking. - Retromolar and external oblique ridge — the usual intraoral donor site for particulate autogenous bone at GDP level (a block graft from the ramus is a complex procedure and out of the examination’s scope for planning). - Buccal shelf, mandibular canal in the atrophic mandible — in severe atrophy the canal may be at/near the crest, and the mandible is at risk of iatrogenic fracture."},{"sourcePage":31,"text":"4.2 Maxilla\n\n• Maxillary sinus — pneumatisation increases after posterior tooth loss; the floor may scallop between roots. Septa (Underwood’s septa) occur in a significant minority and increase the risk of membrane perforation during sinus elevation. • Schneiderian membrane — normal thickness ~0.8–1 mm; thickened >2 mm suggests sinus pathology; pre-existing sinus disease and ostium obstruction are contraindications to elevation until treated (ENT referral). • Ostiomeatal complex — drainage is superior/medial; graft material or blood obstructing the ostium causes sinusitis. • Posterior superior alveolar artery and its intraosseous anastomosis with the infraorbital artery — runs in the lateral wall, typically 15–20 mm from the alveolar crest; the main bleeding risk in a lateral window (out of scope to perform, in scope to know). • Nasopalatine (incisive) canal — behind the central incisors; enlarges with age and resorption; im-plants must avoid it (fibrous tissue →no integration). • Greater palatine foramen/artery — the donor site limit for connective tissue and free gingival grafts; the greater palatine neurovascular bundle lies ~7–17 mm from the CEJ of the palatal teeth depending on vault height; stay within ~2 mm of the CEJ-adjacent zone and within 5–7 mm apical to the gingival margin to harvest safely. • Nasal floor and anterior nasal spine. • Bone quality — the posterior maxilla is typically D3/D4 (type III/IV): thin cortex, sparse trabeculae →lowest primary stability, longest healing, highest failure rate of any site. • Buccal plate in the anterior maxilla — thin (<1 mm) in the large majority of sites, and it is bundle bone dependent on the periodontal ligament, so it resorbs after extraction. This single fact underlies ridge preservation, immediate placement risk, and mid-facial recession.\n\n4.3 Ridge changes after extraction (learn the numbers)\n\n• Post-extraction resorption is greater horizontally than vertically, and greater on the buccal than the palatal/lingual. • Classical figures (Schropp; Tan et al. systematic review): ~50% of ridge width lost at 12 months, of which two-thirds occurs in the first 3 months; horizontal loss ≈3.8 mm, vertical loss ≈1.2 mm at 6 months. • Bundle bone (the ligament-dependent inner socket wall) is lost regardless of technique — ridge preservation reduces but does not eliminate resorption (typically halves horizontal loss). • Consequence: the ridge migrates palatally/lingually and apically, which is why late-placed anterior implants often need GBR and why the emergence profile must be built prosthetically.\n\n4.4 Anatomy of the peri-implant tissues (Cross-reference §5.3.)\n\n• Junctional/barrier epithelium ~2 mm + connective tissue ~1–1.5 mm = biologic width / supracrestal tissue attachment ≈3–4 mm around implants (slightly greater than around teeth). • No cementum, no periodontal ligament, no Sharpey’s fibre insertion: connective tissue fibres run parallel to the implant surface (circular and longitudinal), not perpendicular. • Vascular supply is reduced — derived from the periosteum/supraperiosteal vessels only, with no PDL plexus. Hence reduced defence, faster progression of inflammation, and peri-implantitis lesions ex-tend closer to the bone crest than periodontitis lesions. • Keratinised mucosa ≥2 mm is associated with less plaque, less inflammation, less recession and greater patient comfort during brushing — an increasingly examinable point, and the rationale for a free gingival graft or an apically positioned flap."}]},{"id":"biology","title":"§5 · Osseointegration, bone biology and healing","start":32,"end":33,"pages":[{"sourcePage":32,"text":"§5 Osseointegration, bone biology and healing\n\nBlueprint: “Demonstrate understanding of the phenomenon of osseointegration and the differences between implant systems” (W). Key reading: Lindhe ch. on osseointegration and peri-implant tissues; Misch (bone density, biomechanics); NID.\n\n5.1 Definitions you must be able to quote Osseointegration (Brånemark, 1977/1985): “a direct structural and functional connection between ordered living bone and the surface of a load-carrying implant.”\n\nOsseointegration (Albrektsson & Zarb, histological/clinical): “a process whereby clinically asymptomatic rigid fixation of alloplastic materials is achieved, and maintained, in bone during functional loading.”\n\nPractical clinical definition: an implant that is immobile, has no peri-implant radiolucency, no persis-tent pain, infection, neuropathy or paraesthesia, and stable bone levels.\n\nFibrous encapsulation = failure of osseointegration; occurs with excessive micromotion, overheating, infection, or an implant placed into fibrous tissue (e.g. into the incisive canal).\n\n5.2 Albrektsson’s six factors for osseointegration (a classic SBA)\n\n1. Implant material (biocompatibility — commercially pure titanium/Ti alloy, zirconia) 2. Implant design (macro-geometry: shape, thread form) 3. Implant surface (micro/nano-topography, chemistry) 4. Status of the host bed (quality, quantity, health, previous irradiation) 5. Surgical technique (atraumatic, minimal heat, adequate irrigation, primary stability) 6. Loading conditions (timing and magnitude; avoidance of excessive micromotion)\n\nCritical thresholds: - Heat: bone necrosis at ~47 °C for 1 minute (Eriksson & Albrektsson). Hence graded drilling, sharp single-use/limited-use drills, copious external ± internal irrigation with sterile saline at ≤room temperature, intermittent pressure, low speed for dense bone. - Micromotion: >50–150 𝜇m at the interface prevents osseointegration and produces fibrous repair. This is the biological basis of the immediate-loading criteria (insertion torque >35 Ncm and/or ISQ >70, no direct occlusal contact, no parafunction — see ITI Decision Tree).\n\n5.3 The healing cascade at the implant interface\n\n1. Haemostasis and protein adsorption (seconds–minutes): plasma proteins adsorb onto the surface; the surface chemistry determines which proteins and in what conformation — this is why hydrophilic surfaces (e.g. SLActive) accelerate the early phase. 2. Inflammatory phase (days 1–3): platelet degranulation →growth factors (PDGF, TGF-𝛽, VEGF); neutrophils then macrophages. 3. Proliferative phase / osteoconduction (days 3–14): migration of osteogenic cells along the surface; woven bone formation. Contact osteogenesis (bone forms on the implant surface, de novo, from cells that migrated to it — favoured by roughened surfaces) vs distance osteogenesis (bone forms from the old bone surface toward the implant — typical of machined surfaces). 4. Remodelling / maturation (weeks–months): woven bone replaced by lamellar bone, then load-adapted remodelling.\n\nThe stability dip. Total stability = primary (mechanical) + secondary (biological). Primary stability falls as the bone at the interface is resorbed during remodelling; secondary stability rises as new bone forms. The trough is at approximately 2–4 weeks — the period of greatest vulnerability, and the reason “early loading” is defined as ≥1 week but <2 months, and the reason a wobble at 3 weeks is ominous.\n\nPeri-implant soft tissue seal (see also §4.4): a hemidesmosomal attachment of the barrier epithelium plus a parallel-fibred connective tissue cuff. It is a weaker seal than the periodontium and is disrupted by repeated abutment disconnection — the biological argument for the “one abutment, one time” concept."},{"sourcePage":33,"text":"5.4 Bone quality and quantity classifications\n\nLekholm & Zarb (1985) — bone quality\n\nType Description\n\nI Almost entirely homogeneous compact bone II Thick cortical layer surrounding a core of dense trabecular bone III Thin cortical layer surrounding dense trabecular bone of favourable strength IV Thin cortical layer surrounding a core of low-density trabecular bone\n\nLekholm & Zarb — bone quantity (resorption of the edentulous ridge)\n\nA minimal resorption · B moderate residual ridge resorption · C advanced resorption, only basal bone remains · D some resorption of basal bone · E extreme resorption of basal bone\n\nMisch bone density (with approximate Hounsfield units)\n\nMisch Description Typical site HU (approx.)\n\nD1 Dense cortical Anterior mandible, atrophic >1250 D2 Porous cortical + coarse trabecular Anterior mandible, posterior mandible 850–1250\n\nD3 Thin porous cortical + fine trabecular Anterior maxilla, posterior mandible 350–850\n\nD4 Fine trabecular, minimal cortex Posterior maxilla 150–350\n\n(D5) Immature, non-mineralised (grafted) — <150\n\nClinical consequences: - D1/D2 — excellent primary stability; heat is the enemy; consider counter-sinking/tapping, slow speed, sharp drills; earlier loading possible. - D3/D4 — poor primary stabil-ity; under-prepare (osteotomy narrower than the implant), use tapered/aggressive-thread implants, osteotomes/osseodensification, longer healing (posterior maxilla conventionally 6 months, or 3 months for modern surfaces in D3), consider more/wider implants, avoid immediate loading. - D4 has the highest reported failure rate of any bone type.\n\n5.5 Success and survival criteria Albrektsson et al. (1986) — success criteria (still the standard exam answer): 1. The individual, unattached implant is immobile when tested clinically 2. No peri-implant radiolucency on an undistorted radiograph 3. Vertical bone loss <0.2 mm annually after the first year of loading 4. No persistent pain, discomfort, infection, neuropathies, paraesthesia or violation of the mandibular canal 5. Success rate of 85% at 5 years and 80% at 10 years as a minimum\n\nMarginal bone remodelling in the first year: classically ≤1.5 mm in year one, then ≤0.2 mm/year. Mod-ern platform-switched, bone-level implants often show considerably less.\n\nDistinguish carefully: - Survival = the implant is still in the mouth (says nothing about health) - Success = survival + defined bone/soft tissue/prosthetic criteria met - Failure — early (before or at abutment connection; failure to integrate: micromotion, overheating, infection, poor bone, smoking) vs late (after successful integration: peri-implantitis, overload, fracture)\n\nTypical figures for the written paper: implant survival ~95–97% at 5 years and ~90–95% at 10 years; but technical/biological complication rates are high — roughly one-third of patients experience some complication over 5 years; ceramic chipping, screw loosening and mucositis are the commonest."}]},{"id":"materials","title":"§6 · Implant materials, design and surfaces","start":34,"end":36,"pages":[{"sourcePage":34,"text":"§6 Implant materials, design and surfaces\n\nBlueprint: “Different implant designs and surfaces and how they influence provision of treatment”; “different dental materials and their role in implant dentistry, e.g. impression materials, cements, implant component materials” (W). Key reading: DSMT (materials MCQ book); Misch; CCID ch. 2.\n\n6.1 Implant body materials Commercially pure titanium (cpTi), grades 1–4 - Graded by oxygen, iron, carbon and nitrogen content: increasing grade = increasing oxygen = increasing strength but decreasing ductility. Grade 4 is the strongest cpTi and the most common for implant bodies. - Biocompatibility comes from the spontaneous titanium dioxide (TiO2) passivation layer, a few nanometres thick, which reforms in milliseconds if scratched. Damage to this layer (e.g. by a steel curette) impairs biocompatibility — hence titanium, plastic or carbon-fibre instruments for debriding implants. - True titanium allergy is very rare; where suspected, zirconia is the alternative.\n\nTitanium alloy — Ti-6Al-4V (grade 5) - ~4× the tensile strength of grade 1; used for narrow implants, abutments and screws. Concerns about vanadium and aluminium ion release led to Ti-6Al-7Nb and to titanium–zirconium alloys (Roxolid®, TiZr ~13–17% Zr), which allow reliably strong narrow-diameter (≈3.3 mm) implants.\n\nZirconia (yttria-stabilised tetragonal zirconia polycrystal, Y-TZP) - White, so avoids grey show-through with thin phenotypes; low plaque affinity; good soft tissue response. - Transformation toughening: a crack triggers tetragonal→monoclinic transformation with ~4% volume expansion that closes the crack tip. - Low-temperature degradation (ageing) in moist environments is the trade-off; cannot be bent, welded or easily adjusted; predominantly one-piece designs, which limits prosthetic flexibility and pre-vents submerged healing. - Evidence base is shorter-term than titanium — be cautious in the SOE: “an option with promising but less mature evidence.”\n\nHistoric/obsolete (know the names only): subperiosteal frames, blade/plate-form implants, transman-dibular (staple) implants, ceramic (alumina) implants, hydroxyapatite-coated implants (coating delami-nation/dissolution).\n\n6.2 Surface topography and treatments Roughness classification (Albrektsson & Wennerberg), by Sa:\n\nClass Sa Examples\n\nSmooth <0.5 𝜇m polished Minimally rough 0.5–1.0 𝜇m machined/turned Moderately rough 1.0–2.0 𝜇m SLA, TiUnite, OsseoSpeed, most modern implants Rough >2.0 𝜇m plasma-sprayed (TPS), HA-coated\n\nModerately rough (1–2 𝜇m) is optimal: best balance of bone-to-implant contact and rate of integration against plaque accumulation and peri-implantitis risk. (Note the RCSEd sample SBA: “micro-textured” sur-faces have a roughness of 1–5 micrometres — read the units carefully; the examiners deliberately offer nanometre distractors.)\n\nHow surfaces are made: - Subtractive: acid-etching (HCl/H2SO4), sandblasting/grit-blasting (Al2O3, TiO2), SLA = sandblasted, large-grit, acid-etched, anodisation (TiUnite), laser ablation - Additive: ti-tanium plasma spray (TPS), hydroxyapatite coating, calcium-phosphate nanoparticle deposition - Hy-drophilic/chemically modified: SLActive (stored in isotonic saline), OsseoSpeed (fluoride-modified) — faster early integration, useful in D3/D4 and where early loading is intended\n\nBone-to-implant contact (BIC) is greater on moderately rough surfaces; do not confuse BIC (histological) with ISQ (clinical stiffness measure)."},{"sourcePage":35,"text":"6.3 Macro-design\n\nFeature Options and consequences\n\nShape Parallel-walled (cylindrical) — simple osteotomy, good in D1/D2; tapered (root-form) — better primary stability in soft bone and immediate sockets, allows placement between converging roots and away from concavities Thread V-thread (mainly compressive), square/power thread (best for compressive load transfer), buttress/reverse buttress (tensile). Deeper, wider-pitched, more aggressive threads improve stability in soft bone; finer threads in dense bone Thread pitch Smaller pitch = more threads per unit length = greater surface area, slower insertion Diameter Narrow ≈3.0–3.5 mm (lateral incisors, lower incisors, narrow ridges); standard ≈4.0–4.5 mm; wide ≈5.0–6.0 mm (molars, immediate molar sockets, poor bone). Diameter increases surface area more than length does, and resists bending moments far better Length Standard ≈8–13 mm; short implants ≤8 mm; extra-short ≈4–6 mm. Modern evidence: short implants in the posterior jaws perform comparably to longer implants with augmentation at 3–5 years, with fewer biological complications — an important, defensible, GDP-level answer Neck Machined/polished collar (soft-tissue level, e.g. tissue-level implants) vs roughened to the top (bone level). Microthreads at the neck may reduce crestal bone loss Platform switching Abutment narrower than the implant platform →the microgap and inflammatory infiltrate are moved medially away from the crest →reduced crestal bone loss (typically ~0.3–0.5 mm less)\n\n6.4 Implant–abutment connection\n\nConnection Features\n\nExternal hex (original Brånemark) Long track record; low anti-rotational height; higher rates of screw loosening; micro-movement at the joint Internal hex / internal octagon Better load distribution, deeper engagement Internal conical (“Morse taper”, 8–16°) Friction-fit; best seal, least micro-leakage, least screw loosening, best load distribution; may exhibit abutment “settling”/sinking Conical + indexed (conical connection with anti-rotation) Current standard for single units Tissue-level vs bone-level Tissue-level places the microgap supracrestally (advantage: less crestal remodelling, easier cementation control); bone-level allows better aesthetic emergence and is more flexible in the aesthetic zone\n\nThe microgap and micro-leakage. Any two-piece implant has a microgap that harbours bacteria; the closer it is to bone, the more crestal remodelling. This drives: platform switching, conical connections, tissue-level designs in non-aesthetic sites, and “one abutment, one time”.\n\n6.5 Prosthetic component materials\n\n• Abutments: titanium (strongest, versatile), titanium base + zirconia/lithium disilicate superstruc-ture (“Ti-base hybrid”) — current mainstream, gold (cast/UCLA, largely historic), zirconia (aesthetic; avoid a direct zirconia-on-titanium implant interface without a Ti-base because of wear and titanium particle release). • Screws: titanium alloy or gold alloy; gold-coated/DLC-coated screws reduce friction and improve preload. Preload = the clamping force generated by tightening; torque applied is largely lost to friction. Settling/embedment relaxation loses ~2–10% of preload in the first minutes — hence re-torque after 10 minutes. • Frameworks: cast cobalt-chromium, milled titanium, milled zirconia, PEEK (provisional/hybrid), fibre-reinforced. • Veneering ceramics: feldspathic, leucite-reinforced, lithium disilicate (e-max), zirconia (monolithic — least chipping), composite/acrylic (repairable, shock-absorbing, but wears)."},{"sourcePage":36,"text":"• Impression materials: polyvinyl siloxane (addition silicone) — best elastic recovery and dimensional stability; polyether — hydrophilic, very rigid (excellent for splinted open-tray impressions, but difficult to remove with undercuts); avoid alginate and impression compound for definitive implant impressions. Do not use latex gloves with addition silicone (sulphur inhibits the set). • Cements: see §10.6 — the safest are radiopaque ones you can see and remove.\n\n6.6 Differences between implant systems — how to answer The blueprint asks you to understand “the differences between implant systems”. A strong answer covers:\n\n1. Body: material (Ti grade, TiZr, zirconia), shape, thread design, surface treatment 2. Connection: external/internal/conical, indexing, platform switching 3. Level: bone-level vs tissue-level 4. Prosthetic portfolio: range of abutments, angulated screw channels, availability of narrow/short op-tions, digital library availability 5. Evidence and support: is there long-term peer-reviewed data (≥5 years)? Is the manufacturer stable and likely to supply components in 10 years? (The FGDP standards explicitly require you to satisfy yourself the manufacturer “is sufficiently stable and of adequate stature to be able to provide component parts for the foreseeable future.”) 6. Practical: cost, training and support, compatibility with your laboratory, availability of spare/rescue components\n\nSOE answer to “why do you use your system?” — “Because it has long-term published data, a full prosthetic range including narrow and short implants, a stable manufacturer who will still supply parts in ten years, and my laboratory and my mentor both work with it. If a patient of mine moves practice, another clinician can service it.” That answer scores in domain 2 and domain 4 simultaneously."}]},{"id":"planning","title":"§7 · Treatment planning, complexity and consent","start":37,"end":41,"pages":[{"sourcePage":37,"text":"§7 Treatment planning, case complexity and consent\n\nBlueprint: the whole of Clinical decision making and treatment planning including consent (W + SCE, some OS-P). This is the largest single block of marks in the SOE. Key reading: SAC Classification (learn thoroughly); Misch (treatment planning); ITI TG vol 1 & 3; CCID.\n\n7.1 The treatment planning sequence Present it as a phased plan — examiners mark for sequencing:\n\nPhase Content\n\n1. Emergency/relief of pain Extirpation, drainage, extraction of hopeless teeth, temporary restoration 2. Disease control (initial/hygiene phase) OHI, smoking cessation, diet, caries control, non-surgical periodontal therapy, endodontics, provisional restorations, removal of hopeless teeth 3. Re-evaluation Re-assess periodontal response, plaque and bleeding scores, prognosis of each tooth. Do not proceed to implants until disease is controlled and the patient has demonstrated compliance 4. Definitive/reconstructive Implant placement ± augmentation; definitive restorations; orthodontics where needed 5. Maintenance Individualised recall, peri-implant monitoring, radiographic baseline and follow-up\n\nPrognosis of the remaining dentition must be settled before implants are planned — the blueprint says: “arrive at a prognosis of the remaining teeth and the impact of these on the treatment options.” A questionable tooth adjacent to a planned implant is a planning decision, not an afterthought: either treat it to a good prognosis, or extract it and plan the space as a unit.\n\n7.2 Options for tooth replacement — always give all of them For any missing tooth, be able to give the full option list with pros and cons in 60 seconds:\n\nOption Advantages Disadvantages\n\nDebonding, metal show-through, limited in heavy occlusion/parafunction\n\nDo nothing / accept the space (with monitoring) No cost, no biological price; often appropriate for a shortened dental arch Drifting, over-eruption, aesthetics, function Resin-bonded bridge (RBB), cantilever Minimally invasive, no LA often needed, reversible-ish, excellent survival for single anterior units (~80–90% at 10 yrs for cantilever designs), cheap, quick\n\nConventional bridge Fixed, immediate, restores contour Destructive to abutments (biological cost), risk of abutment pulp necrosis/caries; needs sound abutments Removable partial denture Cheap, replaces soft tissue, non-surgical, can add teeth later Plaque retention, comfort, function, patient acceptance, caries/perio risk Implant-supported crown/bridge Preserves adjacent teeth, maintains bone, high survival, independent Surgery, cost, time, biological/mechanical complications, maintenance, contraindications Orthodontic space closure Biological, no prosthesis Time, cost, may not be feasible; changes occlusion Autotransplantation Biological, maintains alveolus, grows with the patient Case-specific (typically premolar to incisor site in a young patient); technique-sensitive\n\nExaminer’s favourite: “Why not just place an implant?” — the marks are in the reasoning: age and growth status, cost, medical status, the patient’s own priorities, the condition of adjacent teeth (an unrestored virgin tooth strongly favours an implant or a minimal-prep RBB), the number of adjacent missing teeth, and the risk profile."},{"sourcePage":38,"text":"7.3 The SAC classification — the language of the examination SAC = Straightforward / Advanced / Complex, applied separately to the surgical and prosthodontic aspects of a case (ITI/Dawson & Chen).\n\nHow it works: start from the normative classification for the generic case type, then apply modifying factors which can raise (or occasionally lower) the classification.\n\nGeneral modifiers (apply to all cases): - Contraindications (medical/absolute) - Smoking - Growth considerations - Patient’s aesthetic expectations - Compliance/oral hygiene - Adjacent teeth periodontal status\n\nSite/surgical modifiers: bone volume (adequate / deficient horizontally / deficient vertically), anatom-ical risk (proximity to IAN, sinus, adjacent roots), aesthetic risk zone, soft tissue quality and quantity, complexity of the augmentation required, need for provisionalisation, extraction socket status, infection.\n\nProsthodontic modifiers: occlusal scheme and parafunction, interarch space, span, need to alter vertical dimension, aesthetic demands, provisionalisation, loading protocol, need for hard/soft tissue augmenta-tion for prosthetic reasons.\n\nTypical normative classifications (learn these examples):\n\nSituation Surgical Prosthodontic\n\nSingle posterior tooth, adequate bone, adequate space S S\n\nSingle anterior tooth, adequate bone, low lip line, thick phenotype S–A S–A\n\nSingle anterior tooth, high lip line, thin phenotype, deficient bone C C\n\nImmediate placement + immediate provisional in the anterior maxilla A–C A–C\n\nExtended edentulous space in the aesthetic zone A–C A–C\n\nEdentulous mandible, 2-implant overdenture S–A S–A\n\nC —\n\nEdentulous maxilla, fixed full-arch C C Any case requiring major augmentation (block graft, lateral sinus lift, vertical augmentation)\n\nUse it out loud: “On the SAC classification I would grade this as surgically Advanced-to-Complex and prosthodontically Advanced, because of the high lip line, the thin phenotype and the need for simultaneous contour augmentation. That places it beyond what I would undertake as a general dentist with an interest in implants without mentoring, so I would either refer or co-manage.” — that single sentence hits blueprint items on complexity, referral and self-limitation.\n\n7.4 Referral — when and how Refer (or co-manage) when: - Major augmentation is needed (block graft, lateral window sinus el-evation, vertical ridge augmentation, distraction) - Full-arch fixed rehabilitation, or an altered vertical dimension across the whole arch - High aesthetic risk you cannot mitigate (high lip line + thin phenotype + bone loss on adjacents) - Complex medical history (antiresorptives for malignancy, irradiated jaws, bleeding disorders, immunosuppression) - Nerve proximity that cannot be managed with a safe margin - Management of a failing implant beyond your competence, or nerve injury - Patient factors: severe anxi-ety needing sedation/GA you cannot provide; psychological concerns - Any time the case exceeds your training, experience or equipment (GDC Principle 7.2)\n\nA good referral letter contains: patient identifiers; your details; the presenting complaint and the patient’s expectation; relevant medical, dental and social history; your clinical findings and diagnoses; investiga-tions already done (with the radiographs/CBCT attached so they are not repeated — an IR(ME)R point); what you have already discussed and consented; precisely what you are asking for (opinion / treatment / shared care); who will provide the restorative phase and the maintenance; and urgency."},{"sourcePage":39,"text":"7.5 Consent — the second-biggest source of SOE marks Valid consent requires: capacity + information + voluntariness, and it is a process, not a signature.\n\nMontgomery v Lanarkshire (2015) replaced the Bolam test for information disclosure. The clinician must take reasonable care to ensure the patient is aware of any material risks involved in the recommended treatment and of any reasonable alternative or variant treatments. A risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient’s position would attach significance to it, or if the clinician is or should reasonably be aware that this particular patient would.\n\nApplication: a 1% risk of altered lip sensation may be immaterial to one patient and decisive to a saxophonist or a professional actor. You must ask what matters to them.\n\nContent of implant consent (checklist — an easy OSCE/SOE structure): 1. Diagnosis and why treat-ment is being proposed 2. All reasonable options including no treatment, with their risks, benefits, prognosis and costs 3. The proposed procedure, stage by stage, including number of visits, healing times and provisional arrangements 4. Risks — general surgical: pain, swelling, bruising, bleeding, infection, wound dehiscence, need for further surgery 5. Risks — site-specific: altered/lost sensation of lip, chin, tongue (temporary and permanent, quote your figures), damage to adjacent teeth/roots, sinus perforation and sinusitis, oro-antral communication, mandibular fracture (atrophic mandible), floor-of-mouth haem-orrhage 6. Risks — failure: failure to integrate (~2–5%), late failure, need for removal and replacement, possibility that the site cannot be re-implanted 7. Risks — aesthetic: recession, grey show-through, black triangles, mismatch of shade/contour, papilla loss, and that aesthetics can deteriorate over years as the face changes 8. Risks — biological: peri-implant mucositis and peri-implantitis, the requirement for lifelong maintenance 9. Risks — mechanical: screw loosening, ceramic chipping, screw or abutment fracture, implant fracture, wear of overdenture attachments; the need for replacement of components at intervals 10. Grafting materials — the blueprint explicitly requires consent for “the use of grafting materials and barriers”: name the origin (autogenous, allograft — human donor, xenograft — usually bovine or porcine, alloplast — synthetic) and discuss religious, cultural and personal objections, and the theoretical (extremely low) risk of disease transmission 11. Costs, including maintenance and future component replacement, and what happens if it fails 12. Alternatives if it fails, and who is responsible for aftercare 13. Time: total treatment duration, and that they must attend maintenance 14. Written information given, cooling-off period, opportunity to ask questions, and check understanding by asking them to summarise back 15. Record it: what was discussed, what was given, what the patient said, and the date; re-confirm consent at each surgical stage\n\nQuotable: “I check understanding by asking the patient to tell me, in their own words, what we have agreed and what could go wrong. If they can’t, I haven’t consented them — I’ve just talked at them.”\n\n7.6 Drug therapy and safe prescribing\n\nBlueprint: “Prescribe drugs including analgesics, local anaesthetics and antibiotics and justify their use”; “apply national guidelines”; “drug interactions” and “suggest appropriate management for drug interactions” — the last of these is blueprinted to W + OS-P + SCE.\n\nLocal anaesthetics (know the table — an OSCE station in itself)\n\nAgent Typical formulation Max dose (healthy adult) Notes\n\n4.4 mg/kg, max 300 mg (≈7 cartridges for a 70 kg adult) Workhorse; amide\n\nLidocaine (lignocaine) 2% with adrenaline 1:80,000 2.2 mL cartridge = 44 mg lidocaine + 27.5 𝜇g adrenaline\n\nArticaine 4% with adrenaline 1:100,000 or 1:200,000 2.2 mL = 88 mg 7 mg/kg, max ~500 mg Superior bone diffusion → good buccal infiltration in the mandible; the debated association with paraesthesia after IDB means many clinicians avoid it for blocks; not for children <4 Prilocaine 3% with felypressin 0.03 IU/mL 2.2 mL = 66 mg 6 mg/kg, max 400 mg Useful when adrenaline is undesirable; methaemoglobinaemia in high dose; avoid felypressin in pregnancy (oxytocic effect)"},{"sourcePage":40,"text":"Agent Typical formulation Max dose (healthy adult) Notes\n\nMepivacaine 3% plain 2.2 mL = 66 mg 4.4 mg/kg, max 300 mg Short procedures, adrenaline-free Bupivacaine 0.5% with adrenaline long-acting 2 mg/kg, max 150 mg Post-operative analgesia after longer surgery; slow onset\n\nTechniques: infiltration (buccal/palatal/lingual), inferior alveolar nerve block, long buccal, mental/in-cisive block, greater palatine and nasopalatine blocks (for palatal harvesting), intraligamentary and in-traosseous as supplements. For implant surgery in the posterior mandible, some clinicians deliberately avoid an IDB so that the patient can report an electric-shock sensation if the drill approaches the nerve — a genuinely examinable point; the counter-argument is inadequate anaesthesia. Know both sides.\n\nSide effects/complications: failure, intravascular injection (palpitations, tachycardia), haematoma, tris-mus, facial nerve palsy (misplaced IDB into the parotid), needle fracture, prolonged anaesthesia/paraes-thesia, allergy (true amide allergy is rare — most “allergy” is to the preservative or is a vasovagal/a-drenaline response), methaemoglobinaemia (prilocaine, benzocaine), toxicity (perioral tingling, tinnitus, drowsiness, convulsions, cardiorespiratory depression).\n\nDrug interactions relevant to implant dentistry (blueprint requires you to manage them)\n\nInteraction Consequence Management\n\n↑INR, bleeding Avoid; use alternative; check INR\n\nAdrenaline + non-selective 𝛽-blockers (propranolol) Unopposed 𝛼→hypertensive crisis, reflex bradycardia Limit adrenaline dose, aspirate, consider prilocaine/felypressin or plain agent Adrenaline + tricyclic antidepressants Potentiated pressor response Limit dose; avoid adrenaline-containing cord/retraction agents Adrenaline + cocaine/amfetamines Severe hypertension, arrhythmia Do not treat; defer Warfarin + metronidazole, erythromycin/clarithromycin, fluconazole/miconazole (incl. topical gel)\n\nRhabdomyolysis Avoid macrolide/azole, or withhold statin on specialist advice\n\nWarfarin + NSAIDs / aspirin ↑bleeding, GI risk Use paracetamol ± codeine Statins (simvastatin/atorvastatin) + clarithromycin/erythromycin/flucona-zole\n\nGive accurate, current advice\n\nMethotrexate + NSAIDs ↑methotrexate toxicity Avoid NSAIDs Lithium + NSAIDs ↑lithium toxicity Avoid NSAIDs ACE inhibitors / diuretics + NSAIDs Renal impairment, ↑BP Avoid or limit Amoxicillin + allopurinol Rash Note Metronidazole + alcohol Disulfiram-like reaction Warn explicitly and record Combined oral contraceptive + antibiotics (Only enzyme-inducing antibiotics such as rifampicin genuinely reduce efficacy; routine advice changed — but advise about diarrhoea/vomiting)\n\nBisphosphonates/denosumab MRONJ See SDCEP; assess risk, atraumatic surgery, review Warfarin / DOAC Bleeding See SDCEP anticoagulant guidance (§1.2) Clopidogrel/DAPT Bleeding Do not stop; local measures Immunosuppressants, systemic steroids Infection, adrenal crisis Liaise, consider cover\n\nAntibiotics\n\n• Prophylaxis for implant placement: the evidence (Cochrane) supports a single pre-operative dose of amoxicillin 2–3 g orally, 1 hour before surgery to reduce early implant failure; NNT ≈25. Post-operative courses are not supported for straightforward placement. • When to consider a post-operative course: extensive augmentation/GBR with membranes, sinus floor elevation, immunocompromise, established infection. Justify it — do not give it reflexively. • Infective endocarditis: NICE CG64 — antibiotic prophylaxis is not routinely recommended for den-tal procedures. Consult the cardiologist for patients at the highest risk, and record the discussion. • Antimicrobial stewardship: right drug, right dose, right duration, documented indication; penicillin allergy →clindamycin or clarithromycin (check local guidance and SDCEP)."},{"sourcePage":41,"text":"Analgesia\n\n• First line ibuprofen 400 mg qds (with food; avoid in asthma sensitive to NSAIDs, peptic ulceration, renal impairment, anticoagulation, pregnancy 3rd trimester) ± paracetamol 1 g qds — combined is superior to either alone. • Codeine has poor evidence in dental pain and significant side effects; use sparingly. • Pre-emptive analgesia (dose before the LA wears off) and post-operative ice, head elevation and soft diet. • Consider a short course of dexamethasone to reduce swelling after extensive surgery (know it exists; justify it).\n\nSedation (blueprint: “awareness of the different types of sedation available and the associated regulation”)\n\n• Inhalation sedation (nitrous oxide/oxygen) — anxiolysis, rapid recovery, patient can drive home after recovery; needs scavenging. • Intravenous conscious sedation (midazolam) — titrated; flumazenil must be immediately available; escort required; the patient must remain conscious, able to maintain their own airway and respond to verbal command. • Oral/transmucosal sedation — less controllable. • General anaesthesia — hospital setting only. • Regulation: the Standards for Conscious Sedation in the Provision of Dental Care (IACSD) — training, team, equipment, monitoring (pulse oximetry, BP), immediate-life-support training for the whole team, au-dit and clinical governance. GA in primary dental care is not permitted."}]},{"id":"surgery","title":"§8 · Surgical placement & procedural knowledge","start":42,"end":46,"pages":[{"sourcePage":42,"text":"§8 Surgical placement — decision making and procedural knowledge\n\nBlueprint: Decision making in relation to treatment procedures and basic procedural knowledge/skills — surgical placement, handling of tissues and complications. Tested in all three components. Flap design, atraumatic elevation, site preparation, one/two stage, suturing and asepsis all carry the W + OS-P + SCE triple marking. Key reading: Misch (surgical protocols); ITI TG vols 1, 3, 10; Lindhe; CCID chs 5–7.\n\n8.1 Infection control and aseptic technique (Blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE — treat it as guaranteed content.)\n\n• Environment: designated clean surgery, cleaned and disinfected surfaces, minimal traffic, doors closed, no unnecessary equipment on the field. • Sterilisation and decontamination: compliant with HTM 01-05 (or local equivalent) — instruments cleaned (ultrasonic/washer-disinfector), inspected, vacuum autoclave (type B) for hollow/lumened instruments, correctly packaged, dated, stored, and the cycle records retained. • Personnel: surgical scrub (2–5 min for the first case), sterile gown, sterile gloves (double-gloving optional), mask, cap, eye protection. • Patient: pre-operative chlorhexidine 0.2% mouthrinse for 1 minute, perioral skin disinfection, sterile drapes. • Field: sterile handpiece and tubing, sterile saline irrigation, single-use or validated drills, implant components handled with sterile instruments and not touched by gloved fingers or non-titanium instruments (protect the oxide layer). • Waste and sharps handled correctly; needle re-sheathed with a device; instrument count. • Documentation: batch/lot numbers and expiry of the implant, abutment, graft material and mem-brane recorded in the notes and on a patient implant card — this is both a governance requirement and a medical-device traceability requirement.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nSee also Set-piece 3 at the start of Part 7, which gives a full table of incision and flap designs by clinical situation, the atraumatic elevation script, the periosteal releasing incision technique, the matching closure, and the complications attributable to flap design — written as a spoken SOE answer.\n\n8.2 Flap design Principles (say them in this order): 1. Access adequate for the procedure — including for a bur, a membrane, or a graft 2. Base wider than the apex to preserve blood supply 3. Incisions over sound bone, not over the defect or the graft, and closure not over a membrane edge 4. Avoid vital structures (mental nerve, lingual nerve, greater palatine artery) 5. Preserve papillae where aesthetics matter 6. Tension-free closure — the single biggest determinant of graft success 7. Full thickness (mucoperiosteal) where you need bone access; split thickness where you are repositioning soft tissue\n\nDesign options:\n\nDesign Use\n\nCrestal (mid-crestal) incision, no releasing incisions Straightforward placement with adequate keratinised tissue; least morbidity Crestal with 1 or 2 vertical relieving incisions Where more access or coronal advancement is required; place reliefs at the line angles, not over the papilla or over the graft Palatally/lingually displaced crestal incision To move keratinised tissue buccally at second-stage or to gain buccal tissue Buccally displaced Rarely — to bring keratinised tissue palatally Papilla-sparing (papilla-preserving) Aesthetic zone; avoids papilla recession Envelope (sulcular) flap Small elevations; no vertical incisions"},{"sourcePage":43,"text":"Design Use\n\nFlapless Adequate bone width, sufficient keratinised tissue, guided placement; less morbidity, faster healing, but no visualisation of the bone — only for straightforward cases with CBCT/guide Apically positioned flap To increase keratinised tissue at second-stage, or in resective peri-implantitis surgery Roll flap / pouch Small buccal contour augmentation at second stage\n\nAtraumatic elevation (a blueprint phrase — use the exact vocabulary): sharp incision to bone in one firm stroke; a sharp periosteal elevator working against bone, not against tissue; elevate from a corner or from the vertical incision; keep the flap fully thick and undisturbed; retract with a broad retractor resting on bone, never crushing the flap edge; keep the flap moist with saline gauze; minimise the duration of retraction; handle with tissue forceps at the base, not the margin.\n\nPeriosteal releasing incision — to advance the flap for tension-free primary closure over a graft: score the periosteum horizontally at the base of the buccal flap with a sharp blade held nearly parallel to the flap, then blunt-dissect. Risk: bleeding, flap perforation, mental nerve injury if too deep in the premolar region.\n\n8.3 Site preparation — the principles\n\n1. Determine the position first from the guide/wax-up, not from the bone. 2. Sequential (graded) drilling from small to final diameter, at 800–1500 rpm (system-dependent), with copious sterile irrigation, intermittent (“pumping”) pressure, sharp drills with a controlled number of uses. 3. Maintain <47 °C: heat generation increases with drill bluntness, drill diameter, speed, pressure, depth, dense cortical bone and inadequate irrigation. 4. Direction indicators / parallel pins after the pilot drill: check mesiodistal and buccolingual angulation, parallelism with adjacent implants/teeth, and take a periapical radiograph with the pin in situ if the canal or an adjacent root is close. 5. Under-preparation in D3/D4 (skip the final drill, or use osteotomes/osseodensification) to raise inser-tion torque; over-preparation/tapping/counter-sinking in D1 to avoid excessive torque and pressure necrosis. 6. Irrigate and inspect the osteotomy walls — a probe or depth gauge should meet bone in all directions; perforation must be detected before, not after, the implant is placed. 7. Insert the implant at low speed (≈15–30 rpm) with the driver, then finish with a hand ratchet, moni-toring insertion torque.\n\n8.4 Three-dimensional implant position — the rules that generate SBAs Buser’s “comfort and danger zones” for the anterior maxilla:\n\nDimension Rule\n\nMesiodistal ≥1.5 mm from an adjacent tooth; ≥3 mm from an adjacent implant; centre the implant in the space Orofacial (buccopalatal) The implant shoulder should be ≈1–2 mm palatal to the point of emergence of the adjacent teeth (the “comfort zone” is palatal); leaves ≥2 mm of buccal bone Coronoapical Shoulder ≈3 mm apical to the planned mid-facial gingival margin (≈1 mm apical to the CEJ of the adjacent teeth), to allow an emergence profile Angulation Long axis directed through the cingulum of the planned crown (anterior) or the central fossa (posterior); screw access should emerge palatally/occlusally\n\nInsertion torque: typically 25–45 Ncm. >35 Ncm is the conventional threshold for immediate restora-tion/loading. Torque >50 Ncm risks compression necrosis of the crest in dense bone.\n\nImplant stability quotient (ISQ) by resonance frequency analysis (Osstell): scale 1–100; >70 = high stability (immediate loading candidate); 60–69 = medium; <60 = low (do not load early). A falling ISQ over successive measurements is the strongest early warning of failure."},{"sourcePage":44,"text":"8.5 One-stage vs two-stage; and the timing protocols\n\nOne-stage (non-submerged) vs two-stage (submerged) — blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE\n\nOne-stage (healing abutment placed at surgery) Two-stage (cover screw, primary closure, second-stage surgery later)\n\nIndications Good primary stability, adequate bone and keratinised tissue, no simultaneous graft or only minor GBR, non-aesthetic or straightforward aesthetic case, tissue-level implants\n\nPoor primary stability, simultaneous augmentation requiring protection, membrane placed, thin/compromised soft tissues, need to graft soft tissue at second stage, patient wearing a mucosa-borne denture over the site Advantages One surgical episode, no second-stage discomfort, no repeated disruption of the mucosal seal, allows earlier soft tissue maturation, cheaper\n\nProtects the graft/membrane from exposure and infection, protects from micromotion under a denture, allows soft-tissue manipulation at uncovering Disadvantages Exposure risk for the graft; micromotion under a denture Second procedure, longer treatment, extra cost, disrupts the seal\n\nSecond-stage (uncovering) options: simple crestal incision or tissue punch (fastest, but removes kera-tinised tissue — avoid where KM is scarce); apically positioned flap or roll flap to gain buccal keratinised tissue/contour; connective tissue graft simultaneously if the phenotype is thin.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nSee also Set-piece 1 at the start of Part 7, which separates placement timing from loading protocol, gives the advantages and disadvantages of each of Types 1–4, and reproduces the combination grid with its ITI evidence grading.\n\nPlacement timing after extraction (Hämmerle/ITI consensus — memorise)\n\nType Timing Situation\n\nType 1 — Immediate Same day as extraction Socket walls intact, ≥1 mm facial wall, no infection, primary stability achievable apically/palatally, ideal 3-D position achievable, thick phenotype, low aesthetic risk Type 2 — Early with soft tissue healing 4–8 weeks Soft tissue closure achieved, infection resolved; the commonest compromise in the aesthetic zone Type 3 — Early with partial bone healing 12–16 weeks Significant socket healing; useful where more bone fill is needed Type 4 — Late ≥6 months Fully healed ridge; often requires GBR because of resorption\n\nLoading protocols (ITI/Gallucci consensus — memorise)\n\nProtocol Timing\n\nImmediate restoration Restoration fitted within 1 week, out of occlusion Immediate loading (Type A) Restoration in occlusion within 1 week Early loading (Type B) Between 1 week and 2 months Conventional loading (Type C) >2 months (classically 3 months mandible, 6 months maxilla)\n\nCriteria for immediate loading (all must be met): 1. Insertion torque >35 Ncm or ISQ >70 2. No direct occlusal contacts (immediate restoration) / controlled contacts 3. No parafunction 4. Good bone quality and quantity, adequate primary stability, no simultaneous major graft 5. Patient compliance with a soft diet\n\nEvidence grading used in the ITI decision tree — worth quoting: SCV (scientifically and clinically validated) > CWD (clinically well documented) > CD (clinically documented) > CID (clinically insuffi-ciently documented). Conventional loading of a late-placed implant is SCV; immediate placement with immediate"},{"sourcePage":45,"text":"loading is only CD, and immediate placement in a compromised socket with early loading is CID. This is exactly the sort of evidence-grading answer that scores in SOE domain 2.\n\n8.6 Immediate placement — the details that separate candidates Advantages: fewer surgical episodes, shorter overall time, patient satisfaction, uses the socket as a guide, may preserve soft tissue architecture. Disadvantages/risks: mid-facial recession (the principal risk; higher with thin phenotype and a buccally positioned implant), unpredictable bone fill, difficulty achiev-ing ideal position (the drill skids off the palatal wall), reduced primary stability, cannot fully prevent buccal bundle-bone resorption, higher aesthetic complication rate, technique-sensitive.\n\nTechnique points: - Atraumatic extraction — periotomes, luxators, physics forceps, sectioning of multi-rooted teeth; preserve the buccal plate at all costs - Debride the socket thoroughly and confirm the buccal wall is intact by probing - Engage the palatal wall and 3–5 mm of bone apical to the socket for primary stability; start the osteotomy on the palatal wall, not in the socket centre - Position the implant palatally, leaving a ≥2 mm horizontal (jumping) gap buccally - Graft the gap with a slowly resorbing xenograft (deproteinised bovine bone mineral) — reduces horizontal ridge change - Consider a connective tissue graft to thicken the phenotype - Manage the soft tissue: immediate provisional (screw-retained, out of occlusion) shapes the emergence; or a custom healing abutment; or a socket-seal/free gingival “punch” graft - Do not place immediately into a site with acute infection/suppuration; chronic apical granuloma can be managed with thorough debridement but is higher risk\n\n8.7 Suturing — materials and techniques (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE)\n\nMaterials\n\nSuture Resorbable? Filament Notes\n\nSilk (3/0, 4/0) No Braided (multifilament) Cheap, excellent handling and knot security, but wicks bacteria — avoid over grafts; remove at 7 days Polyamide/nylon (Ethilon) No Mono Low tissue reactivity, poor knot security (needs more throws), stiff ePTFE (Gore-Tex, Cytoplast) No Mono Best choice over GBR/membranes — minimal plaque, minimal wicking, excellent tissue response Polypropylene (Prolene) No Mono Inert, slippery Polyglactin 910 (Vicryl) Yes (~56–70 days mass absorption; tensile strength ~2–3 weeks)\n\nBraided Good handling; Vicryl Rapide loses strength in ~7–10 days Polyglycolic acid Yes Braided Similar Poliglecaprone (Monocryl) Yes Mono Low reactivity, ~1–2 weeks strength Catgut / chromic gut Yes — Largely obsolete; high tissue reaction\n\nSizes: implant surgery generally 4/0 or 5/0 (5/0–6/0 in the aesthetic zone and for microsurgical/CTG work); 3/0 for flap closure under tension or in the posterior mandible. Reverse-cutting needle for mucosa; 3/8 circle for accessible sites, 1/2 circle for posterior/limited access.\n\nTechniques\n\nTechnique Use\n\nSimple interrupted Universal; failure of one does not lose the closure Interrupted mattress — horizontal Everts wound edges; distributes tension; good over grafts Interrupted mattress — vertical Everts and adapts deep and superficial layers; used for tension-free primary closure over GBR Continuous (running) Speed over long spans; one failure loses all Continuous locking (blanket) Better tension control over long flaps"},{"sourcePage":46,"text":"Technique Use\n\nSling / suspensory Suspends the flap around a tooth or healing abutment without pulling on the opposite flap — ideal for papilla-preserving and apically positioned flaps Figure-of-eight Over extraction sockets to retain graft material Periosteal suture Anchors and stabilises a repositioned flap\n\nRules to state: pass from mobile to fixed tissue; from thin to thick; enter perpendicular, 3 mm from the margin, taking equal bites of each side; place the knot away from the incision line; approximate, do not strangulate (tension causes ischaemic necrosis and dehiscence — the leading cause of membrane exposure); remove non-resorbable sutures at 7–10 days (or 10–14 days over augmentation).\n\n8.8 Post-operative care Give it verbally and in writing and record that you did.\n\n• Bite on gauze 20–30 minutes; expect some ooze on the first day • Cold compress for the first 24 hours (20 min on/off), head elevated when sleeping • Analgesia — ibuprofen ± paracetamol; take the first dose before the LA wears off • Antibiotics if prescribed — complete the course; metronidazole and alcohol warning • Chlorhexidine 0.2% mouthrinse twice daily from day 1 for 1–2 weeks (do not rinse vigorously on day 1); no brushing of the wound but continue elsewhere • Soft diet, avoid chewing on the site; avoid hot food/drink on day 1 • No smoking, no alcohol, no strenuous exercise for 48–72 hours • Denture wear — remove for 24–48 hours, then relieve generously and reline with a soft liner; do not wear over a graft if possible • Sinus precautions where relevant: no nose-blowing, sneeze with the mouth open, no straws, no smok-ing, no diving/flying if advised • What is normal (swelling peaks 48–72 h, bruising, mild ooze) and what is not — return/contact if: bleeding not controlled by pressure, increasing pain after day 3, fever, spreading swelling, difficulty swallowing or breathing (emergency), numbness persisting beyond the expected LA duration, or the graft/membrane becoming exposed • Emergency contact number and review appointment (suture removal 7–10 days)"}]},{"id":"augmentation","title":"§9 · Minor hard and soft tissue augmentation","start":47,"end":50,"pages":[{"sourcePage":47,"text":"§9 Minor hard and soft tissue augmentation\n\nBlueprint: “Evaluate the appropriateness of minor augmentation procedures and their execution”, “describe the different types of bone defects and suggest appropriate means to manage these”, “differentiate between the different types of soft and hard tissue augmentation materials”, “apply an understanding of the role of barriers”, “limitations… and the timing”. All blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE. Remember the scope: minor augmentation is in; block onlay grafts, lateral-window sinus elevation and vertical augmen-tation are out of scope to plan or perform — in scope to recognise and refer. Key reading: ITI TG vol 7; Lindhe; CCID ch. 4–5; ITI Decision Trees.\n\n9.1 What counts as “minor” at GDP level In scope: alveolar ridge preservation/socket grafting; simultaneous horizontal GBR of a dehiscence or fenestration; grafting the jumping gap at immediate placement; transcrestal (osteotome/Summers) sinus floor elevation with residual height >6 mm and a flat sinus floor; ridge split/expansion of modest degree (arguably advanced); connective tissue and free gingival grafts; roll flaps.\n\nOut of scope (recognise and refer): autogenous block onlay grafts; lateral window sinus elevation; vertical ridge augmentation; guided bone regeneration for large defects requiring titanium-reinforced membranes/mesh; distraction osteogenesis; Le Fort I with interpositional graft; nerve lateralisation.\n\nThe ITI Decision Tree for the atrophic posterior maxilla is a clean way to organise this: favourable interarch relationship + insufficient height →SFE alone; inadequate crest width as well →SFE + horizontal augmenta-tion; severe vertical resorption →vertical ridge augmentation ± SFE; severe vertical resorption + inadequate width →horizontal and vertical augmentation, or Le Fort I with interpositional graft. Only the first branch, and only in its transcrestal form, is GDP territory.\n\n9.2 The biology: GBR and the PASS principle Guided bone regeneration (GBR) uses a barrier membrane to exclude the fast-migrating epithelial and connective tissue cells from the defect, creating and maintaining a space in which slower osteoprogenitor cells can regenerate bone.\n\nPASS principles for predictable regeneration (Wang & Boyapati) — quote them: - Primary wound closure (tension-free) - Angiogenesis — blood supply (decorticate/perforate the cortical plate to open marrow spaces) - Space maintenance/creation - Stability of the wound and the graft (fixation, tacks, screws, or a stable graft/membrane construct)\n\nGraft mechanisms (know the three words): - Osteogenesis — the graft contains living cells that form bone. Only autogenous bone. - Osteoinduction — the graft induces host mesenchymal cells to differen-tiate into osteoblasts (BMPs). Autogenous and (variably) DFDBA. - Osteoconduction — the graft acts as a scaffold along which bone grows from the host. All graft materials.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nSee also Set-piece 2 at the start of Part 7, which expands this into the four categories with their full advantages, disadvantages and consent points, and adds a structured account of the complications of bone grafting by donor site, recipient site, early, late and material-specific."},{"sourcePage":48,"text":"Type Examples Properties Notes\n\n9.3 Bone graft materials — the comparison table\n\nType Examples Properties Notes\n\nOsteogenic + osteoinductive + osteoconductive; no disease transmission; fastest incorporation\n\nAutogenous (“gold standard”) Intraoral: chin, ramus/external oblique ridge, tuberosity, bone from the osteotomy, bone scrapers/collectors, bone traps\n\nDonor site morbidity, limited volume, most rapid and unpredictable resorption; particulate autogenous from a scraper is the realistic GDP source Allograft (human donor, processed) FDBA (freeze-dried bone allograft), DFDBA (demineralised — exposes BMPs, more osteoinductive, less space-maintaining), cortical/cancellous chips\n\nOsteoconductive ± osteoinductive Ethical/religious considerations; consent must state the human origin; theoretical disease transmission (essentially eliminated by processing); tissue-bank regulation Xenograft (another species) Deproteinised bovine bone mineral (DBBM) — Bio-Oss®; porcine; equine; anorganic\n\nOsteoconductive only; very slow/negligible resorption →outstanding volume and contour stability\n\nVariable resorption; generally less well documented than DBBM in implant sites\n\nOsteoconductive; unlimited supply; no disease/ethical issue\n\nBovine/porcine origin — must be disclosed (religious, dietary, vegetarian objections); the standard material for jumping gaps, ridge preservation and contour augmentation Alloplast (synthetic) 𝛽-tricalcium phosphate, hydroxyapatite, biphasic calcium phosphate, bioactive glass, calcium sulphate, polymers\n\nGrowth factors; soft tissue healing benefit best documented for PRF\n\nEvidence variable; know what they are and that the evidence for hard-tissue gain is limited\n\nBiologics/adjuncts Autologous platelet concentrates (PRF/PRP), enamel matrix derivative, rhBMP-2, rhPDGF\n\nComposite grafting (autogenous + DBBM, typically 1:1) is the common clinical compromise: autogenous supplies osteogenic cells, xenograft supplies volume stability.\n\n9.4 Barrier membranes\n\nMembrane Examples Pros Cons\n\nResorbable — natural collagen Porcine/bovine type I & III collagen (Bio-Gide®) No second surgery, excellent tissue integration, exposure is better tolerated (may still heal), haemostatic\n\nRequires removal; ePTFE exposure →infection and graft loss\n\nPoor space maintenance (collapses), unpredictable resorption (~4 weeks–6 months), animal origin — consent Resorbable — synthetic PLA/PGA copolymers No second surgery, predictable degradation Acidic degradation products may cause inflammation; stiffness variable Non-resorbable — dPTFE / ePTFE Cytoplast™, Gore-Tex® Excellent space maintenance; high-density PTFE can be left exposed intentionally over sockets\n\n— Best space maintenance for large/vertical defects Advanced–complex; refer\n\nNon-resorbable — titanium-reinforced PTFE / titanium mesh\n\nThe commonest complication is membrane exposure, caused by tension at closure, a suture line over the membrane, denture pressure, or smoking. Manage exposure by: chlorhexidine, no brushing, close review; if a collagen membrane and no infection, it often heals; if ePTFE or infected, remove the membrane early to save what bone has formed.\n\n“Ridge preservation” vs “socket seal”: a graft placed in the socket plus a barrier/seal (membrane, col-lagen sponge/plug, free gingival punch graft, or a customised healing abutment). It reduces but does not abolish ridge resorption — typically halving horizontal loss (≈1.5–2 mm saved). Indications: thin or"},{"sourcePage":49,"text":"damaged buccal plate, aesthetic zone, a site to be implanted later, and where the patient cannot proceed immediately. Not indicated routinely where immediate placement is planned and achievable, or where the site will be left edentulous with no prosthesis.\n\n9.5 Bone defect classification and management Around an implant at placement:\n\nDefect Description Management\n\nDehiscence Bone missing from the crest down the implant face, communicating with the crest\n\nSimultaneous GBR: decorticate, particulate graft (composite), collagen membrane, tension-free closure, usually two-stage/submerged; expect ~4–6 months Fenestration A “window” of exposed implant surface with intact bone at the crest GBR as above; often more predictable than a dehiscence Horizontal (buccal) deficiency Insufficient ridge width If ≥1–2 mm implant coverage achievable →simultaneous GBR; if not →staged GBR first, or ridge split, or refer Vertical deficiency Insufficient height Refer — vertical augmentation is complex and unpredictable; consider short implants as the GDP-level alternative Combined — Refer\n\nPeri-implantitis defects (Schwarz classification) — used in the ITI peri-implantitis decision tree: - Class I — intrabony (with sub-types Ia dehiscence; Ib dehiscence + semicircular; Ic dehiscence + circumferential; Id semicircular buccal + lingual; Ie circumferential) - Class II — supracrestal/horizontal bone loss Also used clinically: CID (circumferential intrabony defect), DD (dehiscence defect), CD (combined defect), CSD (combined semicircular defect), ID (intrabony defect) — as in the ITI decision tree, where recon-structive therapy is reserved for defects with an intrabony component (Types 1 CID, 3 CD, 5 ID) and resective therapy for non-aesthetic sites with Types 4 CSD and 5 ID.\n\nExtraction socket classification (Elian): - Type 1 — intact facial soft tissue and intact facial plate (best for immediate placement) - Type 2 — soft tissue present but the facial plate is damaged/missing - Type 3 — both soft tissue and facial plate deficient (worst; ridge preservation or staged approach)\n\n9.6 Transcrestal (osteotome) sinus floor elevation — the one you may do Indications: posterior maxilla, residual bone height >6 mm (some say ≥5 mm), flat sinus floor, need for only 3–4 mm of gain, adequate ridge width, healthy sinus. Contraindications: height ≤6 mm, oblique/s-teep sinus floor, septa, sinus pathology, ostium obstruction, previous sinus surgery, heavy smoker → lateral window instead — which means refer.\n\nSequence (Summers): flap →osteotomy to 1–2 mm short of the sinus floor →concave osteotomes of increasing diameter with gentle malleting to green-stick fracture and elevate the floor with a plug of graft/bone →confirm membrane integrity with the Valsalva manoeuvre (no air movement/bubbling; the membrane should not move) →place the implant →close. Modern alternatives: hydraulic elevation, sequential drills with a stopper, piezo-surgery.\n\nComplications: Schneiderian membrane perforation (commonest; if small, a collagen membrane may seal it, otherwise abandon and re-plan), benign paroxysmal positional vertigo from malleting, graft mi-gration into the sinus, sinusitis, oro-antral communication, loss of the implant into the sinus.\n\nPost-operative sinus precautions and a course of antibiotics ± decongestant are usually appropriate.\n\nAlternative to sinus grafting for the GDP: short implants. Say this in the SOE — “Given a residual height of 6 mm with a flat floor I would consider either a transcrestal elevation with simultaneous placement, or a short (6 mm) implant with a favourable crown-to-implant ratio, which has comparable medium-term survival with fewer complications and would keep the case within my competence.”"},{"sourcePage":50,"text":"9.7 Soft tissue augmentation\n\nProcedure Purpose Notes\n\nFree gingival graft (FGG) Increase the width of keratinised mucosa (target ≥2 mm) Epithelialised graft from the palate; heals by secondary intention at the donor site; colour mismatch (“patch”); most predictable for KM gain Subepithelial connective tissue graft (SCTG) Increase soft tissue thickness/volume, improve colour and contour, treat recession\n\nHarvested from the palate (single/double incision, trapdoor); gold standard for phenotype modification; can be combined with a coronally advanced flap Soft tissue substitutes Xenogeneic collagen matrix, acellular dermal matrix Avoid a second surgical site; slightly less volume gain than autogenous Roll flap / pouch technique Small buccal contour gain at second stage Uses the palatal tissue over the implant Apically positioned flap Increase KM at uncovering; also resective peri-implantitis surgery Preserves keratinised tissue rather than excising it Vestibuloplasty / frenectomy Deepen the sulcus, remove a pull on the margin Often before overdenture provision\n\nTiming: phenotype modification is easiest and most predictable at implant placement or at second stage, not after the prosthesis is fitted. Donor site care: palatal stent/plate, haemostatic dressing, avoid the greater palatine bundle, warn about discomfort and bleeding — the palate is the most painful part of the whole procedure for most patients.\n\n9.8 Ridge split / expansion Indication: ridge width ≥3 mm with cancellous bone between two cortical plates, adequate height, maxilla far more predictable than mandible (the mandible’s dense cortex fractures rather than expands). Technique: crestal incision, careful (often partial-thickness) flap to preserve the buccal periosteal blood supply, mid-crestal corticotomy with a thin disc/piezo, vertical releases, expansion with chisels/osteotomes/expanders, implant placement, graft the gap, membrane, close. Risks: fracture or loss of the buccal segment, malposition, dehiscence. A recurring SOE line of questioning — “short implant risks; would you split?” — — a good answer compares ridge split, GBR (staged or simultaneous), a narrow-diameter implant, a short implant, and the non-implant options, then states which you would do and why, referencing your own competence.\n\n9.9 The limitations to state whenever asked about augmentation\n\n1. Vertical augmentation is far less predictable than horizontal. 2. Autogenous grafts resorb, sometimes substantially and unpredictably. 3. Regenerated bone is not native bone — lower density, slower to integrate, longer healing (typically 4–6 months for simultaneous GBR, 6–9 months for staged). 4. Membrane exposure is the commonest complication and reduces the gain. 5. Smoking markedly reduces success. 6. Every graft adds cost, time, morbidity and risk, and must be consented for specifically, including the material’s origin. 7. Sometimes the right answer is not to graft: a shorter or narrower implant, a different implant position, a different prosthetic design, or a non-implant option."}]},{"id":"prosthodontics","title":"§10 · Restoring implants: prosthodontics and occlusion","start":51,"end":54,"pages":[{"sourcePage":51,"text":"§10 Restoring implants — prosthodontics and occlusion\n\nBlueprint: Decision making in relation to treatment procedures — restoring implants and managing complications (the majority of these lines are marked W + OS-P + SCE). Key reading: Misch (prosthetic sections); CCID ch. 3; ITI TG vols 2, 4, 11; DSMT.\n\n10.1 The prosthetic options\n\nProsthesis Indication Notes\n\nSingle crown Single tooth Screw- or cement-retained; anti-rotational connection essential Implant-supported FDP (bridge) Multiple adjacent units Never connect an implant rigidly to a natural tooth for routine cases (differential mobility →intrusion of the tooth, screw loosening, cement failure) Cantilever Where an implant cannot be placed One unit only, out of the aesthetic/heavy-load zone, avoid in parafunction; increases mechanical complications Implant-retained overdenture Edentulous, especially mandible Two implants in the mandibular canine regions is the accepted minimum standard of care for the edentulous mandible (McGill and York consensus statements) Implant-supported fixed full arch Edentulous Out of scope for planning in this examination Implant-assisted RPD Selected partially dentate cases Uncommon; know it exists\n\nAttachment systems for overdentures:\n\nAttachment Features\n\nBall/stud Simple, cheap, resilient; wear of the matrix; needs parallelism Locator® Low profile (needs less vertical space), various retention inserts, tolerates divergence up to ~20–40°, easy to change nylon inserts Bar (Dolder, milled) Splints the implants, distributes load, good for divergent implants and poor bone; needs more vertical space (12–15 mm), harder to clean, more expensive, more laboratory steps Magnets Least retentive, easy to place/clean, useful with poor dexterity; corrosion/wear Telescopic/conus Rigid, excellent but costly\n\n10.2 Impression techniques\n\nTechnique How When\n\nMultiple implants, non-parallel implants, deep implants — the most accurate for splinted/multiple units\n\nOpen tray (direct, pick-up) Long guide screws protrude through a window in a custom tray; screws unscrewed before removal; the coping is picked up in the impression\n\nSingle units, limited mouth opening; risk of re-seating error\n\nClosed tray (indirect, transfer) Transfer copings remain in the mouth; the impression is removed and the copings re-seated into the impression with analogues\n\n≥3 implants, full arch — improves accuracy\n\nSplinted Copings joined with acrylic (sectioned and re-joined to compensate polymerisation shrinkage), pattern resin, or floss + composite\n\nDigital / intraoral scan with scan bodies No physical impression; direct to CAD Excellent for single units and short spans; accuracy across long edentulous spans remains the limitation"},{"sourcePage":52,"text":"Technique How When\n\nChoose implant-level in the aesthetic zone\n\nAbutment-level vs implant(fixture)-level Abutment-level simplifies the lab work and preserves the seal; implant-level allows full customisation of the emergence profile\n\nMaterial choice: polyether or addition silicone (PVS) — high rigidity, dimensional stability, elastic recovery. Custom tray, correct adhesive, uniform thickness, verify the coping seating radiographically where deep. Also record: shade, jaw registration, opposing arch, facebow where needed, and photographs.\n\n10.3 Jaw registration\n\n• Needed whenever there is no stable, reproducible intercuspal position, when the vertical dimension is being altered, and for all extensive/edentulous cases. • Use rigid record bases with an accurate fit (ideally screwed to the implants for edentulous cases), wax rims adjusted for lip support, incisal level, occlusal plane and midline, and a low-distortion registra-tion material (e.g. addition silicone bite registration, or a rim locked with pattern resin). • Facebow to relate the maxilla to the hinge axis for anything more than a single unit in an established occlusion. • Common error: displacing a resilient mucosa or a flexible base and building in an error you then blame on the laboratory.\n\n10.4 Abutment selection (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE) Decide in this order: 1. Retention type — screw or cement (see §10.6) 2. Level — implant-level (cus-tomisable emergence) vs abutment-level (multi-unit abutments for full-arch/angled cases) 3. Angulation — straight vs pre-angled; or an angulated screw channel (ASC) to keep a screw-retained design when the implant is buccally inclined 4. Height — sufficient for retention (cement) and to bring the margin to the right level; the subgingival margin depth is the key aesthetic and cement-related decision — keep it ≤1 mm subgingival, ideally at or just below the margin, and follow the scalloped gingival contour 5. Material — titanium (posterior, high load), Ti-base + zirconia/lithium disilicate (aesthetic), full zirconia (avoid direct zirconia-titanium interfaces at the implant connection), gold (historic) 6. Stock vs custom (CAD-CAM) — custom gives a correct emergence profile, correct margin level and better soft tissue support; stock is cheaper and faster 7. Emergence profile — developed with the provisional and then copied by a customised impression coping\n\n10.5 Screw-retained vs cement-retained (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE — expect it)\n\nScrew-retained Cement-retained\n\nBetter where angulation is unfavourable\n\nRetrievability Excellent — the decisive advantage Difficult/destructive Cement risk None Residual cement is a major cause of peri-implantitis Minimum space Less (~4–5 mm) More (needs abutment height ~5 mm) Aesthetics Access hole may show if the implant is buccally angulated (solved by ASC abutments)\n\nPassive fit Demands a passive framework; misfit is transmitted Cement layer compensates for minor misfit Occlusion Access hole in the occlusal table can weaken porcelain/cause chipping Uninterrupted occlusal surface\n\nCost/complexity Fewer components Extra abutment step\n\nRules of thumb: screw-retained wherever the angulation allows, and especially where the margin would be >1–2 mm subgingival; cement-retained only where the emergence is unfavourable and the margin can be kept at or above the mucosal margin."},{"sourcePage":53,"text":"10.6 If you must cement — how to avoid residual cement\n\n1. Keep the margin at or above the mucosal margin wherever possible (≤1 mm subgingival). 2. Use a radiopaque cement so you can see what you have left behind. 3. Prefer weak/temporary cements (e.g. zinc oxide eugenol-free temporary cement, or resin-modified glass ionomer) — retrievability matters more than maximal retention. 4. Minimise the volume: apply cement only to the coronal half of the intaglio; use an extra-oral repli-ca/analogue technique (seat the crown on a duplicate abutment first to extrude excess), or a Teflon/sil-icone spacer. 5. Remove excess meticulously with floss, scalers and air/water; take a radiograph to confirm. 6. Do not use eugenol-containing cement with resin-based restorations.\n\n10.7 Implant occlusion — “implant-protective occlusion” Because there is no periodontal ligament, implants have: - Axial movement of only ~3–5 𝜇m (teeth: ~25–100 𝜇m) - Lateral movement of ~10–50 𝜇m (teeth: ~56–108 𝜇m) - No proprioception from a PDL →higher tactile threshold, reduced protective reflex, and slower/absent detection of overload - Move-ment pattern: teeth show an immediate, non-linear, two-phase movement; implants show gradual, linear movement with no initial phase\n\nTherefore the occlusal scheme is designed to protect the implant:\n\nRule Detail\n\nLight contacts in ICP Implant restorations should contact lightly — the classic instruction is that shimstock (8–12 𝜇m) holds under heavy clenching but not under light tapping; i.e. contact ~25–30 𝜇m lighter than the adjacent teeth Contacts directed along the long axis Centralise contacts over the implant; narrow the occlusal table and reduce cusp inclines No lateral/excursive contacts on implants where teeth can guide Use canine guidance on natural teeth; if the canine is an implant, use group function to distribute load Eliminate working and non-working interferences Especially non-working (balancing) interferences Reduce cantilevers and offsets Cantilever length and buccolingual offset generate the largest bending moments Avoid connecting implants to teeth Differential mobility Cusp height/angle Flatter cusps reduce lateral force In parafunction Splint (hard acrylic full-coverage), more/wider implants, no cantilevers, monolithic zirconia rather than layered ceramic, accept a higher complication risk and consent for it In an overdenture Bilateral balanced or lingualised occlusion for a complete overdenture, as for conventional dentures\n\nProgressive loading (Misch): in soft bone (D3/D4), gradually increase load — provisional in acrylic, out of/light occlusion, then progressive contacts over weeks — to allow bone to mature and remodel. Know the concept; the evidence is modest.\n\n10.8 Screw mechanics — preload, torque, loosening\n\n• Preload is the tensile clamping force in the screw that holds the joint together. It is generated by torque, but ~90% of applied torque is consumed by friction. • Settling (embedment relaxation): micro-roughness flattens under load, losing ~2–10% of preload → always re-torque after ~10 minutes. • Causes of screw loosening: inadequate torque (the most likely cause of a loose single crown, per the RCSEd sample SBA the answer given is excessive occlusal loads — read the stem carefully: the published sample question with a 12-month-old, symptomless, gradually loosening screw-retained crown is marked B, excessive occlusal loads), excessive/eccentric occlusal load, cantilever, non-passive fit, worn or reused screws, absent anti-rotational feature (single unit on an external hex), lack of re-torque. • Management of a loose screw: remove the restoration, inspect and replace the screw with a new one, check for component damage and for implant fracture, identify and correct the cause (occlusion, cantilever, fit), re-torque to the manufacturer’s value, re-torque after 10 minutes, seal the access with PTFE tape + composite, review. Never simply retighten and send the patient away. • Fractured screw retrieval: ultrasonic scaler used counter-clockwise on the fragment, an explor-er/probe, a proprietary screw-removal kit, or a purpose-made reverse-thread drill; protect the internal threads; if the threads are destroyed the implant may be lost."},{"sourcePage":54,"text":"10.9 Laboratory communication (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE) Every prescription must contain: - Patient identifier and your name, address and GDC number; date sent and date required - Precise description of the work — units, tooth notation, implant system, connec-tion, platform diameter, abutment type (stock/custom, angulation, material), retention (screw/cement), framework material, veneering material, occlusal material - Shade (with photographs, ideally with a shade tab in shot), characterisation, translucency, stump shade - Occlusal scheme required, and the verti-cal dimension - Emergence profile instructions, margin position, contact points, contour for cleansability - Whether a try-in is required (framework/verification jig/bisque) - Statement of disinfection on despatch and on return - Custom-made device statement under the medical devices regulations, retained in the notes; and record the laboratory’s details and the batch/lot numbers of the components returned\n\nVerification jig / verification of fit: for multi-unit frameworks, use a sectioned-and-rejoined jig or a screw-test (Sheffield/one-screw test: tighten only the most distal screw and check the fit at the other end — a gap means the framework is not passive).\n\n10.10 Managing a mis-fitting prosthesis or framework (a named blueprint line, marked OS-P)\n\n1. Confirm the misfit objectively: visual gap, the one-screw (Sheffield) test, tactile check with a probe, radiograph (a periapical taken with a parallel technique — the classic sign is a wedge-shaped gap between abutment and implant), patient symptoms (pressure, pain on tightening). 2. Identify the cause: interference from soft tissue or bone over the platform; a component not fully seated; incorrect analogue orientation; impression distortion; inaccurate cast; laboratory error; frame-work distortion during firing; the wrong component. 3. Do not force it. Never torque a non-seating restoration — this causes screw fracture, bone loss and pain. 4. Remedy according to cause: remove interference; re-seat; section the framework and re-solder/laser weld; take a new impression with a splinted open-tray technique or a verification jig; remake. 5. Explain to the patient, apologise for the delay, and record everything. Do not blame the technician in front of the patient.\n\n10.11 Digital workflows (blueprinted W + OS-P + SCE) Know the chain and its error sources: intraoral scan →(merge with CBCT via DICOM/STL superim-position) →virtual wax-up →guide design →3-D printed/milled guide →guided placement →scan body →CAD abutment/crown →milled/printed restoration.\n\nAdvantages: patient comfort, no impression material, immediate assessment and re-scan, digital archiv-ing, communication with the laboratory and patient, restoratively driven planning, potential accuracy for single units. Limitations: cost, learning curve, accuracy over long edentulous spans, scan-body wear/ac-curacy, metal artefact in CBCT merging, deep subgingival margins scan poorly, and the guide cannot compensate for surgical judgement."}]},{"id":"complications","title":"§11 · Complications and their management","start":55,"end":57,"pages":[{"sourcePage":55,"text":"§11 Complications and their management\n\nBlueprint: Interpretation and problem solving of clinical outcomes — Patient safety; “Suggest appropriate strategies for managing complications that may occur during a surgical procedure”; “Recognize clinical situations which are unsafe or could lead to an adverse event”. Key reading: Misch (complications — the most complete single source); ITI TG vol 8; CCID (complications chapter).\n\n11.1 A framework for any complication question Answer every complication question in the same five steps. Examiners are marking a process, not a fact.\n\n1. Recognise — what are the signs and symptoms, and what is the differential? 2. Immediate management — stop, protect the airway/haemostasis/patient safety, relieve pain 3. Definitive management — what will I do, and over what timescale? 4. Refer? — to whom, how urgently, and what will I say? 5. Prevent, record and learn — how would I stop this happening again; what do I record; do I need to report it (significant event analysis, duty of candour, indemnity)?\n\nSay “duty of candour” out loud. When something goes wrong you must tell the patient promptly, apologise, explain what happened and what you will do, and record it. An apology is not an admission of liability. This is explicitly in GDC Standards and is a domain-4 marking point.\n\n11.2 Intra-operative complications\n\nComplication Recognition Management Prevention\n\nFlap design avoiding named vessels\n\nHaemorrhage — soft tissue Brisk bleeding from flap/vessel Pressure, ligation/diathermy, suture, local haemostatics (oxidised cellulose, collagen, tranexamic acid)\n\nRapidly expanding swelling of the floor of mouth, tongue elevation, dysphagia, dyspnoea, stridor\n\nCBCT, respect the lingual concavity, angle the drill lingually-aware, palpate the lingual plate\n\nFloor-of-mouth haemorrhage (lingual plate perforation anteriorly; sublingual/submental arteries)\n\nAIRWAY EMERGENCY: sit the patient up, call 999, secure the airway, direct pressure/bimanual compression, transfer immediately; do not send home\n\nNerve injury — IAN/mental Bleeding from the osteotomy, patient reports electric shock, post-op anaesthesia/paraes-thesia/dysaesthesia\n\n2 mm margin, CBCT, direction indicators + radiograph, drill stops, avoid IDB in selected cases\n\nIf recognised during surgery: remove or shorten the implant immediately. If post-op: review within 24 h, map and document the area with a diagram, prescribe a short course of steroids ± NSAID (evidence limited), remove/back out the implant within 24–36 h if it is impinging, refer urgently to oral/maxillofacial surgery within days if not resolving — do not “wait and see” for months\n\nRidge assessment, CBCT\n\nLingual nerve injury Altered tongue/taste sensation As above; usually from flap/incision, not the drill Avoid lingual releasing incisions; careful retraction Perforation of the lingual/buccal plate Loss of tactile resistance, probe passes through If small and the implant is stable →graft ± membrane; if the implant is unstable or malpositioned →remove, graft, re-plan\n\nHeight >6 mm and flat floor for transcrestal; check for septa\n\nSinus membrane perforation Valsalva positive, bubbling, visible membrane tear Small (<5 mm): collagen membrane patch, continue cautiously; large: abandon, close, re-plan in 3–6 months"},{"sourcePage":56,"text":"Complication Recognition Management Prevention\n\nAs above\n\nOro-antral communication Air/fluid passage <2 mm: suture and sinus precautions; larger: buccal advancement flap ± referral; antibiotics + decongestant\n\nAssess bone height; do not force\n\nImplant displaced into the sinus Implant not retrievable at the crest Do not probe blindly; refer for retrieval (endoscopic/Caldwell-Luc) — leaving it risks sinusitis/fungal ball\n\nGuide, direction indicator + radiograph, 1.5 mm margin\n\nDamage to an adjacent root Drill deviates; radiograph confirms Reposition the osteotomy; monitor the tooth (vitality, radiograph); may need endodontics; inform the patient\n\nUnder-prepare in soft bone; assess D-classification\n\nLack of primary stability Implant spins in the osteotomy Place a wider/longer implant if bone allows; graft and abandon the site (submerge and re-enter in 4–6 months); never load\n\nThroat pack/gauze screen, floss ligature on small components, high-volume suction, four-handed dentistry\n\nOverheating / bone necrosis Smoking/charring, bone smell, later loss Abandon that osteotomy; re-site or delay Sharp drills, irrigation, intermittent pressure, low speed in dense bone Mandibular fracture (severely atrophic mandible) Crack, mobility, malocclusion, pain Stabilise, urgent maxillofacial referral Do not place standard implants in a mandible <7–8 mm high — refer Instrument/component aspiration or ingestion Coughing, choking, or a lost component Airway first: back blows/abdominal thrusts if obstructing; if swallowed and asymptomatic →chest and abdominal radiograph; if aspirated →urgent hospital referral for bronchoscopy\n\nEmphysema Sudden crepitant swelling with air-driven handpiece Stop, antibiotics, observe; usually self-limiting Never use an air-turbine/air-driven handpiece for implant surgery — use an electric surgical motor\n\n11.3 Early post-operative complications\n\n• Pain, swelling, bruising — expected; peak at 48–72 h. Increasing pain after day 3 suggests infection or dry socket-type problem. • Bleeding — pressure, review anticoagulation and local haemostasis. • Infection / abscess — drain, irrigate, antibiotics, review; consider removing an implant that is mobile. • Wound dehiscence / membrane exposure — chlorhexidine, no brushing, close review; remove ePTFE early; a collagen membrane may still heal. • Failure to integrate (early failure) — mobile implant, pain on percussion/loading, peri-implant radi-olucency. Remove the implant, curette and irrigate the site, allow 2–3 months of healing (± graft), then re-implant — usually with a wider implant. Warn the patient that a second failure at the same site is more likely. • Prolonged altered sensation — see §11.2; document, map, review at 1 week, 1 month, and refer if not improving.\n\n11.4 Late biological complications Covered in §12 (peri-implant mucositis and peri-implantitis). Others:\n\n• Retrograde peri-implantitis — apical radiolucency around an otherwise integrated implant, typically from residual pathology in the site or an adjacent endodontically involved tooth. Treat the source; apical surgery/decontamination or removal. • Soft tissue hyperplasia / peri-implant polyp — usually from an ill-fitting or over-contoured prosthe-sis, or a poorly seated abutment. • Mucosal recession and grey show-through — thin phenotype, buccal implant position, titanium abut-ment. Manage with a connective tissue graft, a zirconia or pink-ceramic abutment, prosthetic recon-touring, or accept and monitor; in a badly malpositioned implant the definitive answer is removal."},{"sourcePage":57,"text":"• Sinusitis after sinus procedures.\n\n11.5 Mechanical/technical complications\n\nComplication Cause Management\n\nScrew loosening (commonest technical complication) Inadequate preload, overload, cantilever, non-passive fit, no anti-rotation See §10.8 — new screw, correct the cause, re-torque, re-torque again at 10 min Screw fracture Fatigue from repeated loosening, overload Retrieve the fragment (ultrasonic, screw-removal kit); replace; correct the cause Abutment fracture Overload, parafunction, narrow abutment Replace; consider a stronger material/wider implant/splinting Ceramic chipping/fracture (commonest complication of implant FDPs) Layering porcelain, parafunction, framework support, thermal stresses Polish/repair, or remake in monolithic zirconia; adjust occlusion; splint Framework fracture Inadequate bulk, misfit Remake Implant fracture (rare, ~0.2–1%) Fatigue, narrow implant under heavy load, bone loss increasing the lever arm, untreated screw loosening\n\nImplant must be removed (trephine) or the fractured portion left submerged (“sleeper”) if removal would be destructive Wear/loss of retention of overdenture attachments Normal wear Replace inserts/matrices — consent for this maintenance cost from the outset Denture base fracture Insufficient bulk over attachments Reinforce with a metal framework Loss of cement retention Weak cement, short abutment, taper Re-cement after checking fit and occlusion — or convert to screw-retained\n\n11.6 Aesthetic complications Causes are almost always planning and positioning, not the ceramic. Recognise:\n\n• Implant too buccal →recession, grey show-through, thin tissue • Implant too shallow →visible metal collar, poor emergence • Implant too deep →deep pocket, difficult hygiene, cement risk, “ridge lap” contour • Implants too close together →papilla loss, black triangles • Contact point too far from the crest (>5 mm) →black triangle • Failure to graft the buccal contour →concavity beneath the crown\n\nManagement: soft tissue grafting, prosthetic camouflage (pink ceramic, modified contours, a longer crown), orthodontic adjunct, or explantation and re-treatment. A candidate who recognises that “this implant is in the wrong place and no restoration will fix it” is scoring highly, provided they also explain the conversation with the patient, the costs, and who should do the re-treatment.\n\n11.7 Managing complaints and dissatisfaction (blueprinted to SCE)\n\n1. Listen — uninterrupted, in a private setting, with time set aside. 2. Acknowledge and apologise for the patient’s experience (duty of candour). 3. Establish the facts — examine, take records/radiographs, review the notes and the consent. 4. Explain in plain language what has happened and what the options are; separate what is a complication from what is an error. 5. Offer a remedy — repair, remake, refund, referral for a second opinion. 6. Document everything contemporaneously; inform your indemnity provider early. 7. Follow the practice complaints procedure, respond within the stated timescales, and tell the patient about the relevant ombudsman/regulator if unresolved. 8. Learn — significant event analysis, change of protocol, and re-audit.\n\nNever: become defensive, blame the laboratory or the previous dentist in front of the patient, alter the records, or promise an outcome you cannot deliver."}]},{"id":"maintenance","title":"§12 · Peri-implant health, disease and maintenance","start":58,"end":62,"pages":[{"sourcePage":58,"text":"§12 Peri-implant health and disease, maintenance and outcomes\n\nBlueprint: Quality and maintenance; Complications and Clinical Outcomes. Marked W + SCE throughout. Key reading: Lindhe; ITI TG vol 13 and the ITI Decision Tree “Treating Peri-Implant Mucositis and Peri-Implantitis”; 2017 World Workshop (Berglundh et al.); EFP S3 guideline; Termeie; PCS.\n\n12.1 The 2017 World Workshop definitions — learn them verbatim-ish Peri-implant health - No clinical signs of inflammation: no erythema, no swelling, no bleeding on gentle probing (≤0.25 N), no suppuration - No increase in probing depth compared with previous examinations - No bone loss beyond the initial crestal remodelling - Health can exist around implants with reduced bone support.\n\nPeri-implant mucositis - Bleeding on gentle probing (the primary diagnostic criterion), ± erythema, swelling, suppuration - Probing depth may be increased because of swelling or reduced resistance - No bone loss beyond initial crestal remodelling - Caused by plaque; reversible if the biofilm is controlled (the key message)\n\nPeri-implantitis - Bleeding and/or suppuration on gentle probing - Increased probing depth compared with previous examinations - Bone loss beyond crestal bone level changes resulting from initial bone remodelling\n\nIn the absence of previous examination data (the usual clinical reality), peri-implantitis is diagnosed when all of: 1. Bleeding and/or suppuration on gentle probing 2. Probing depth ≥6 mm 3. Bone level ≥3 mm apical to the most coronal portion of the intraosseous part of the implant\n\nPeri-implant soft and hard tissue deficiencies — the fourth category: recession, lack of keratinised mu-cosa, ridge deficiency, papilla loss.\n\nPrevalence (weighted means, be able to quote approximate figures): peri-implant mucositis ≈43% of patients, peri-implantitis ≈22% of patients (with wide reported ranges, ~20–47% and ~9–47%, depending on case definition — say that the case definition drives the number, which is an insightful examiner-pleasing point).\n\n12.2 Risk factors and risk indicators Strong evidence: history of periodontitis, poor plaque control / no supportive maintenance, smoking (dose-dependent). Moderate/emerging: poorly controlled diabetes, residual submucosal cement, ab-sence of keratinised mucosa (<2 mm), prosthesis design preventing access for cleaning (over-contoured, ridge-lap, deep margins), occlusal overload (contested as a primary cause but a co-factor), implant surface characteristics, genetic factors, alcohol.\n\n12.3 Diagnosis in practice\n\n• Probe gently (0.2–0.25 N) with a plastic or metal probe — probing is safe and essential; the “do not probe implants” idea is obsolete • Record probing depths at 6 sites, BOP, suppuration, mucosal recession, width of keratinised mucosa, plaque score • Mobility = terminal (loss of integration) — check the implant, not the prosthesis, by removing the restoration if in doubt • Radiograph: standardised, paralleling technique, compared with a baseline taken at prosthesis fitting (this is why the baseline radiograph matters so much) • Assess the prosthesis: fit, contour, cleansability, residual cement, screw integrity, occlusion • Assess the patient: periodontal status of remaining teeth, plaque control, smoking, diabetes, dexterity, motivation\n\n12.4 Treatment — follow the ITI decision tree structure"},{"sourcePage":59,"text":"Step 0 — Examination and cause-related therapy\n\nAssess periodontal status; assess and modify potential risk factors (smoking, diabetes); assess the pros-thesis for cleansability and modify or remake its contours if required; assess the patient’s ability to perform plaque control; assess patient expectations; obtain previous records and radiographs.\n\nStep 1 — Diagnosis and explanation\n\nExplain the disease and the treatment; discuss costs and possible adverse effects, e.g. soft tissue reces-sion (this exact wording is in the ITI tree — quote it). For peri-implantitis, decide explicitly whether it is rational to keep the implant or irrational (in which case plan removal from the outset).\n\nStep 2 — Non-surgical therapy (for both mucositis and peri-implantitis)\n\n• Mechanical debridement — removal of biofilm and calculus with titanium, PEEK/plastic or carbon-fibre curettes, ultrasonics with plastic-sheathed tips, glycine/erythritol air-polishing (better access to threads than curettes) • Modify prosthesis contours where they prevent access • Individualised oral hygiene instruction — interdental brushes sized to the site, single-tufted brushes, superfloss, powered brushes • Adjunctive measures (optional, modest evidence): air-polishing, local antimicrobials (chlorhexidine chip/gel, minocycline microspheres), antimicrobial photodynamic therapy, probiotics, systemic antibi-otics only in selected peri-implantitis cases • Re-assess at approximately 1 month with peri-implant probing\n\nStep 3 — Re-assessment outcomes\n\n• Resolution (no BOP/suppuration, no increased PD) →supportive therapy • Persistent mucositis (persistent BOP) →further non-surgical therapy, re-motivate, re-examine the prosthesis • Peri-implantitis with BOP and PD ≤5 mm →further non-surgical therapy and close monitoring • Persistent peri-implantitis — suppuration and/or BOP with PD ≥6 mm →surgical treatment\n\nStep 4 — Surgical treatment\n\nCommon surgical steps: mucoperiosteal flap; removal of inflamed granulation tissue; decontamination of the implant surface (chemical — hydrogen peroxide, chlorhexidine, saline; mechanical — ultrasonic, titanium brush; laser — Er:YAG); adequate flap adaptation; adequate post-operative care — antiseptics ± peri-operative systemic antimicrobials.\n\nThen choose the approach by defect morphology:\n\nApproach Indication (ITI tree)\n\nOpen-flap debridement Types 1 CID, 2 DD, 3 CD, 4 CSD, 5 ID — i.e. any morphology, where the aim is access and decontamination only Reconstructive therapy (bone graft/substitute ± membrane ± soft-tissue graft) Defects with an intrabony component: Type 1 CID, Type 3 CD, Type 5 ID Resective therapy (bone removal ± implantoplasty, apically positioned flap) Non-aesthetic areas: Type 4 CSD, Type 5 ID\n\nImplant removal Extent of the defect too great, or the aesthetic outcome would be severely compromised. Then either spontaneous healing or hard and/or soft tissue grafting\n\nImplantoplasty = smoothing the exposed rough implant surface with burs/polishers to make it plaque-resistant. Use high suction and protect the tissues from titanium particles.\n\nStep 5 — Supportive therapy\n\nRegular monitoring every 3–5 months: professional biofilm removal and oral-hygiene reinforcement. (That interval is straight from the ITI tree — quote it.)"},{"sourcePage":60,"text":"When to remove an implant\n\nMobility (loss of integration), fracture of the implant, progressive bone loss unresponsive to treatment, bone loss beyond ~50–60% of the implant length, uncontrollable infection, severe malposition making a satisfactory restoration or hygiene impossible, or a patient who cannot maintain it. Methods: reverse-torque (counter-torque ratchet) — least destructive; piezo-surgery; trephine — most destructive.\n\n12.5 Maintenance, records and outcome monitoring A written maintenance programme is part of the treatment plan, not an afterthought — and this is precisely the sort of thing a candidate forgets when the examiner asks “and then what?”\n\nRecall interval: risk-based; commonly 3–6 months for implant patients, 3–4 months where there is a history of periodontitis, smoking, or previous peri-implant disease. The ITI tree specifies 3–5 monthly after treating peri-implant disease.\n\nAt every implant recall: - Update medical history, smoking status, symptoms - Plaque and bleeding scores; peri-implant probing at 6 sites; BOP and suppuration; recession; keratinised mucosa - Check prosthesis: mobility, screw integrity, wear, chipping, contour, cleansability, occlusion - Professional biofilm removal with implant-safe instruments; polish with a non-abrasive paste - Re-motivate and re-instruct with site-specific aids - Radiographs: baseline at prosthesis fitting, then at 1 year, then only when clinically indicated (change in probing depth, BOP/suppuration, symptoms) — an IR(ME)R justification point - Record everything, including the date, findings, advice given and the next review\n\nParameters used to monitor outcomes (a blueprint phrase): implant survival; marginal bone level change against baseline; probing depths and BOP; suppuration; soft tissue level/recession; papilla in-dex; prosthetic complication events; patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs: OHIP-14, satisfaction, aesthetics, function, comfort); and function/aesthetics assessed against the pre-operative plan and the patient’s stated expectations.\n\nRecords — what must be in the notes for every implant case: medical history and updates; consent dis-cussion and written information given; radiographs and reports; the treatment plan and any modifications with reasons; the surgical record — implant system, diameter, length, site, batch/lot number, insertion torque, graft and membrane products with lot numbers, closure, sutures; post-operative instructions given; the prosthetic record — abutment type and torque, cement type, occlusion; the maintenance plan; every review; and any complication with what you did and said. Give the patient an implant record/pa-tient card.\n\n12.6 Quality improvement and audit (entrance requirement and blueprint content) Audit cycle: identify the topic →set the standard (with a source) →collect data →compare with the standard →implement change →re-audit to close the loop. Examples of implant-relevant audits: completeness of implant surgical records; radiographic justification and reporting; consent documenta-tion; peri-implant probing at recall; rate of early implant failure; antibiotic prescribing against guidance; sterilisation records; patient-reported outcomes. Significant event analysis for adverse events; clinical governance components: audit, risk management, education and training, evidence-based practice, pa-tient involvement, information governance, staffing.\n\nHave ready for the SOE: your own QI project in 60 seconds — problem, standard, method, result, change, re-audit."},{"sourcePage":61,"text":"12.7 Professionalism, confidentiality and the team\n\n• Confidentiality and data protection — UK GDPR/Data Protection Act; records retained (commonly 11 years for adults, or until age 25 for children, per NHS guidance — know that a defined retention period exists); patients may request access; images require specific consent; be careful with messaging apps and lab communications. • Communicating the plan to the wider team (blueprint line): written treatment plan shared with the hygienist/therapist for maintenance; a clear laboratory prescription; the referring/receiving clinician; the GMP where medical liaison is needed; and the patient’s own copy. • Working within competence (GDC Principle 7), mentoring, indemnity for implant dentistry specifi-cally, verifiable CPD with reflection. • Evidence-based practice — be able to describe the hierarchy (systematic review/meta-analysis of RCTs > RCT > cohort > case-control > case series > expert opinion), and to say why a particular claim is or is not well supported. “Defend a treatment plan derived using an evidence-based approach” is a literal blueprint line."},{"sourcePage":62,"text":"High-yield numbers, classifications and lists\n\nEverything in the notebook that has to be known as a number, a name or a list. Read this section daily in the last ten days.\n\nIN THIS PART\n\n2.1 Distances and dimensions 60 2.2 Physiological and biomechanical numbers 60\n\n2.3 Classifications to have at your fingertips 61 2.4 Drugs — quick recall 61"}]},{"id":"numbers","title":"High-yield numbers, classifications and lists","start":63,"end":65,"pages":[{"sourcePage":63,"text":"PART 3 — HIGH-YIELD NUMBERS, CLASSIFICATIONS AND LISTS\n\nRevise this part daily in the last ten days. Almost every number here has appeared in a written paper somewhere.\n\n2.1 Distances and dimensions\n\nParameter Value\n\nImplant–tooth (crestal) ≥1.5 mm Implant–implant ≥3 mm Buccal bone thickness (aesthetic) ≥2 mm (minimum 1.5 mm) Palatal/lingual bone ≥1 mm Ridge width for a Ø4.1 mm implant ≈6.5–7 mm Mesiodistal space, standard implant ≈7 mm Mesiodistal space, narrow implant ≈5.5–6 mm Safety margin from the IAN ≥2 mm Distance anterior to the mental foramen (no CBCT) ≥5 mm Anterior loop length commonly 1–3 mm, up to ~5 mm Implant shoulder below the planned mid-facial gingival margin ≈3 mm Implant shoulder palatal to the emergence of adjacent teeth ≈1–2 mm Contact point to crest for a papilla ≤5 mm = ~100%; 6 mm = 56%; 7 mm = 27% Restorative space, screw-retained crown ≈5–6 mm Restorative space, cement-retained crown ≈7–8 mm Restorative space, stud/Locator overdenture ≈8–10 mm Restorative space, bar overdenture / fixed hybrid ≈12–15 mm Freeway space (normal) 2–4 mm Biologic width / supracrestal tissue attachment around implants ≈3–4 mm\n\nKeratinised mucosa target ≥2 mm PSA artery in the lateral sinus wall ≈15–20 mm from the crest Transcrestal SFE indication residual height >6 mm, flat floor\n\n2.2 Physiological and biomechanical numbers\n\nParameter Value\n\nBone necrosis threshold 47 °C for 1 minute Micromotion threshold preventing osseointegration 50–150 𝜇m Insertion torque, typical 25–45 Ncm Insertion torque for immediate loading >35 Ncm ISQ high stability >70 (medium 60–69; low <60) Implant axial movement 3–5 𝜇m (tooth 25–100 𝜇m) Implant lateral movement 10–50 𝜇m (tooth 56–108 𝜇m) Optimal implant surface roughness (Sa) 1.0–2.0 𝜇m (moderately rough) TiO2 passivation layer a few nanometres, reforms in milliseconds Marginal bone loss, year 1 ≤1.5 mm (Albrektsson); thereafter ≤0.2 mm/year Loss of preload from settling ~2–10% →re-torque at 10 minutes Ridge width lost at 12 months post-extraction ~50% (two-thirds in the first 3 months) Horizontal / vertical ridge loss at 6 months ≈3.8 mm / ≈1.2 mm Probing force for peri-implant probing 0.2–0.25 N (gentle) Peri-implantitis (no baseline) criteria BOP/suppuration + PD ≥6 mm + bone loss ≥3 mm Prevalence, mucositis / peri-implantitis (patient level) ≈43% / ≈22% Implant survival, 5 yr / 10 yr ≈95–97% / ≈90–95% Smoking effect on failure ≈2–2.5×"},{"sourcePage":64,"text":"2.3 Classifications to have at your fingertips Bone quality: Lekholm & Zarb I–IV · Misch D1–D4 (+HU) Bone quantity: Lekholm & Zarb A–E Place-ment timing: Type 1 immediate · Type 2 early soft-tissue (4–8 wk) · Type 3 early partial bone (12–16 wk) · Type 4 late (≥6 mo) Loading: Immediate restoration/loading (Type A, <1 wk) · Early (Type B, 1 wk–2 mo) · Conventional (Type C, >2 mo) Evidence grading (ITI): SCV > CWD > CD > CID Case complex-ity: SAC — Straightforward / Advanced / Complex, applied separately to surgical and prosthodontic Extraction socket: Elian Type 1 / 2 / 3 Peri-implantitis defects: Schwarz Class I (a–e) intrabony, Class II supracrestal; ITI tree types CID / DD / CD / CSD / ID Surface roughness: smooth <0.5 · minimally rough 0.5–1.0 · moderately rough 1.0–2.0 · rough >2.0 𝜇m Periodontal (2017 World Workshop): Stage I–IV (severity/complexity) and Grade A/B/C (rate of progression); e.g. “generalised periodontitis stage IV grade C” — a failing dentition loosely described as “grade 4C” is, in current language, Stage IV, Grade C generalised periodontitis Aesthetic risk (ITI ERA): 12 factors, low/medium/high Albrektsson’s 6 factors for osseointegration; Albrektsson’s 5 success criteria; PASS principles; osteogenesis/osteoin-duction/osteoconduction\n\n2.4 Drugs — quick recall\n\nLidocaine 2% + adr 1:80,000 44 mg/cartridge; max 4.4 mg/kg (300 mg) Articaine 4% 88 mg/cartridge; max 7 mg/kg (~500 mg) Prilocaine 3% + felypressin 66 mg/cartridge; max 6 mg/kg (400 mg) Antibiotic prophylaxis for placement Amoxicillin 2–3 g single dose, 1 h pre-op (NNT ≈25) Infective endocarditis NICE CG64 — not routinely recommended Warfarin Treat if INR <4.0, checked within 24 h (72 h if stable) Analgesia Ibuprofen 400 mg qds ± paracetamol 1 g qds Chlorhexidine 0.2% rinse, 1 minute pre-op; bd post-op 1–2 weeks Metronidazole Alcohol warning; interacts with warfarin Midazolam reversal Flumazenil"},{"sourcePage":65,"text":"The literature: books, papers and numbers\n\nA topic-by-topic map of the standard texts, the papers and names worth quoting, and the survival and complication figures — so that you always know where to read next, and what to cite.\n\nIN THIS PART\n\n4.4 The numbers: survival, success, longevity and prognosis 68\n\n4.5 One-line summaries: the papers worth carrying into the room 73\n\n4.1 Topic →source map 63 4.2 A realistic reading strategy 64 4.3 The papers behind the techniques: who to name, and for what 64"}]},{"id":"literature","title":"Literature, reference books and key papers","start":66,"end":82,"pages":[{"sourcePage":66,"text":"PART 4 — THE LITERATURE: WHICH BOOK, WHICH PAPER, WHAT NUMBERS\n\nThe commonest mistake in preparing for this examination is reading too widely. You do not need to read every book on the list. Use this table to go straight to the right source for the topic you are weak on.\n\n4.1 Topic →source map\n\nIf you are weak on… Read this first Then this\n\nFGDP(UK) Training Standards in Implant Dentistry\n\nThe scope and level of the exam RCSEd Appendix A — Learning Outcomes & Blueprint; Regulations §2 and §18\n\nMedical assessment and risk Misch — “Medical Evaluation of the Dental Implant Patient” CCID ch. 1 Case 2 (Medical Considerations); SDCEP guidance Anticoagulants, MRONJ, prescribing SDCEP — Anticoagulants, MRONJ, Drug Prescribing for Dentistry BNF; NICE CG64\n\nNID (condensed summaries); OIR\n\nExamination, occlusion, space analysis Misch — treatment planning and “Available Bone / Force Factors” chapters CCID ch. 1 Case 4 (Occlusal/Anatomical Considerations) Radiography and IR(ME)R FGDP(UK)/RCR Selection Criteria for Dental Radiography CCID ch. 1 Case 5 (Radiographic Interpretation); Misch radiographic chapters The diagnostic wax-up and guides RCSEd Appendix D (the sample OSCE station) Misch — prosthetic planning; ITI TG vol 11 (digital) Surgical anatomy Misch — anatomy and complications chapters The Mental Foramen and Nerve: Clinical and Anatomic Factors (the paper supplied) Osseointegration and peri-implant biology Lindhe — Clinical Periodontology and Implant Dentistry, the implant biology section\n\nBone density and biomechanics Misch — bone density (D1–D4), force factors, implant design DSMT\n\nRidge changes after extraction Lindhe — Araújo & Lindhe on socket healing and bundle bone ITI TG vol 10 (extraction sockets)\n\nImplant materials, surfaces, metallurgy DSMT — Dental Science, Materials and Technology: MCQ in Implant Dentistry CCID ch. 2 (Implant Design); Misch\n\nCase complexity and risk assessment SAC Classification, 2nd edn — read it cover to cover; it is short ITI TG vol 1 (aesthetic risk assessment)\n\nAesthetic zone planning ITI TG vol 1 SAC; CCID aesthetic cases Loading protocols ITI TG vol 2; the ITI Decision Tree on immediate placement Gallucci et al. consensus\n\nPosterior sites, short implants ITI TG vol 3 CCID ch. 2 Case 5 (Short Implants) Extraction sockets and immediate placement ITI TG vol 10; ITI Decision Tree: Immediate Placement Lindhe\n\nRidge preservation and GBR ITI TG vol 7; CCID ch. 5 (Ridge Site Preparation — 8 cases) Lindhe; Termeie (regeneration Q&A)\n\nMisch\n\nSinus floor elevation ITI TG vol 5; the two ITI Decision Trees (sinus technique; atrophic posterior maxilla)\n\nSoft tissue management CCID ch. 4 (FGG, SCTG, vestibuloplasty/frenectomy) Lindhe; PCS\n\nProsthetic design, abutments, retention CCID ch. 3 (Abutment Design; Screw-Retained; Restorative Materials) ITI TG vol 11; Misch\n\nImplant occlusion Misch — implant-protective occlusion NID Overdentures and the edentulous patient ITI TG vol 4 Misch\n\nComplications — biological and technical ITI TG vol 8 Misch — complications chapters\n\nPeri-implant disease ITI TG vol 13; the ITI Decision Tree on mucositis/peri-implantitis Lindhe; 2017 World Workshop (Berglundh et al.); EFP S3 guideline Periodontal underpinning Termeie — Periodontal Review Q&A PCS — Periodontics: The Complete Summary; Lindhe Written-paper practice Mosea — MCQs in Implant Dentistry DSMT; Termeie; this notebook Parts 5 and 8 Case-based rehearsal for the SOE CCID — Clinical Cases in Implant Dentistry This notebook Part 7"},{"sourcePage":67,"text":"If you are weak on… Read this first Then this\n\nConsent, records, professionalism GDC Standards for the Dental Team (Principles 1, 3, 4, 7) Montgomery v Lanarkshire summary; FGDP standards\n\n4.2 A realistic reading strategy If you have three months: - Read SAC in full (a weekend). It is the single highest-return book for the SOE. - Read the four ITI Decision Trees until you can reproduce them from memory. They are one page each and they structure four whole SOE answers. - Work through CCID chapters 1–5 as SOE rehearsal — read the case, close the book, answer the four domains aloud, then read the discussion. - Use Misch as a reference, not a read-through. Look up what you don’t know. - Do Mosea and DSMT MCQs for the written paper, plus the 800 in this notebook. - Read the 2017 World Workshop peri-implant definitions until you can state them verbatim.\n\nBefore anything else, whatever time you have: read “Where the marks are” at the start of Parts 6 and 7, and the three set-pieces at the start of Part 7. They will stop you revising the wrong component.\n\nIf you have three weeks: - Part 3 of this notebook (numbers and classifications) every day. - The four ITI Decision Trees. - OSCE stations 51–58, rehearsed out loud against the clock (2 minutes reading, 5 minutes speaking). - All 800 SBAs here — a block a day, marked against Part 8. - As many of the 50 SOE cases as you can, spoken aloud, timed at 15 minutes, ideally with a colleague playing examiner. - The blueprint (Appendix A) — read the actual wording; the examiners write questions from those sentences.\n\nIf you have three days: - Part 3 (numbers and classifications). - The SAC classification summary in §7.3. - The peri-implant disease definitions and the ITI peri-implantitis decision tree. - The four-domain SOE preparation framework, and the nine phrases in Part 7. - The calibration analyses at the head of Parts 6A and 7A. - Skim the complete answer key in Part 8 and re-read every explanation for a question you got wrong.\n\n4.3 The papers behind the techniques: who to name, and for what There is a mark most candidates never claim. It is given when you attach a name to a technique or a classification and say, in one sentence, what that person or group actually contributed. It costs four extra words in a sentence you were going to say anyway, and it converts a competent answer into an evidence-based one.\n\nThe tables below are grouped by territory: the name, the contribution, and the sentence to say. Learn the third column — the first two exist so that it makes sense.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nName the contribution, not the citation. “Following Araújo and Lindhe’s work on bundle bone…” scores. “There was a paper in 2005…” does not, and invites a question you cannot answer. If you are unsure of a year, leave it out — the contribution is what is examined, and a wrong year undermines everything else you say.\n\nOsseointegration and its definition\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nBrånemark and the Gothenburg group Discovered and named osseointegration (first patient 1965); the machined, submerged, two-stage protocol\n\n“Osseointegration is a direct structural and functional connection between ordered living bone and the surface of a load-carrying implant. That definition excludes fibrous encapsulation, and my irrigation, drill speed, primary stability and control of micromotion all exist to achieve it rather than repair.” Schroeder and the Swiss/Bern line (origin of the ITI) Independently showed direct bone apposition, naming it functional ankylosis; the one-stage transmucosal implant\n\n“Two groups reached the same biology independently. Schroeder’s contribution to how I work is the one-stage transmucosal protocol — which is why a single-stage healing abutment is a legitimate default, not a shortcut.”"},{"sourcePage":68,"text":"Name(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nAlbrektsson, with Brånemark, Hansson and Lindström (1981) The six factors influencing osseointegration: material, design, surface, host bed, surgical technique, loading\n\n“I would distinguish survival from success — an implant can survive and still fail against Albrektsson and Zarb’s criteria.”\n\n“Three of the six are chosen at planning, one is the patient’s biology, and two — surgical technique and loading — are entirely mine. When an implant fails, that tells me where to look.” Albrektsson & Zarb (with Worthington and Eriksson, 1986) The success criteria: immobility, no radiolucency, under 0.2 mm annual bone loss after year one, no pain or neuropathy, 85% at five years and 80% at ten\n\nBuser on surface topography Moderately rough sandblasted, acid-etched surfaces increase bone-to-implant contact and removal torque; later, hydrophilic surfaces\n\n“Moderately rough surfaces are why healing times have shortened from Brånemark’s three and six months — but roughness is a trade-off, because the same topography is harder to decontaminate if peri-implantitis develops.”\n\nBone quality and quantity\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nLekholm & Zarb (1985) Bone quantity A–E; bone quality 1–4 “I would call this quantity C, quality 3 — a thin cortex around dense trabecular bone. That drives my drilling protocol, whether I under-prepare, and whether immediate loading is even on the table.” Misch Bone density D1–D4, tied to drilling protocol, healing time and force management\n\n“I use D1 to D4 because it comes with instructions: D1 needs heat control and tapping, D4 needs under-preparation, bone condensing and a longer healing period — and it has the highest failure rate.” Cawood & Howell (1988) Classification of the edentulous jaw, Classes I–VI “Class IV is a knife-edge ridge — a horizontal problem. Class V and VI are flat or depressed — vertical problems, far less predictable, and I would refer them.”\n\nExtraction socket biology and ridge change\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nAraújo & Lindhe (2005) The coronal buccal wall is largely bundle bone, is ligament-dependent, and resorbs when the tooth is removed\n\n“The buccal plate depends on the periodontal ligament, so it resorbs when the tooth comes out — which is why an immediate implant does not preserve it.” Schropp and colleagues (2003) Dimensional change over 12 months: roughly 50% of width lost, about two-thirds in the first three months\n\n“Half the ridge width goes in the first year, two-thirds of that in the first three months. A patient who wants a year to think about it needs to be told what the year costs them.” Chappuis and colleagues (2013) Buccal bone thickness in the anterior maxilla — most sites have a facial wall of 1 mm or less, and thin-wall sites lose markedly more vertical bone\n\n“I cannot assume the facial wall is thick; in most anterior sites it is a millimetre or less, so I need a CBCT. That measurement decides between immediate and early placement here.” Cardaropoli, Botticelli and the Lindhe group The stages of socket healing, and that the marginal gap around an immediate implant fills with bone while the outer contour still resorbs\n\n“The inside of the socket is not the problem — it fills. The outside is. That is why I graft the jumping gap for contour, not for osseointegration.”\n\nPlacement timing and loading"},{"sourcePage":69,"text":"Name(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nHämmerle, Chen & Wilson (ITI consensus, 2004) The Type 1–4 placement classification, defined by the state of socket healing, not elapsed days\n\n“Type 2, early placement at four to eight weeks. The classification is by the state of the socket, not the calendar — a socket still inflamed at eight weeks is not a Type 2 site.” Gallucci and colleagues (ITI consensus on loading) Immediate / early / conventional loading definitions, the restoration versus loading distinction, and the evidence-graded combination grid\n\n“Placement timing and loading are separate decisions. Conventional loading is scientifically and clinically validated with any placement type; immediate placement with immediate loading is only clinically documented, and I would say so rather than present them as equivalent.” Buser and colleagues on early placement Established Type 2 early placement with simultaneous contour GBR in the aesthetic zone\n\n“Early placement with simultaneous contour augmentation is the workhorse aesthetic-zone protocol — infection resolved, soft tissue healed enough for tension-free closure, most horizontal resorption not yet complete.” The ITI consensus line on immediate placement Immediate placement is not bone-preserving; site-selected; highest aesthetic complication rate\n\n“Intact socket, facial wall at least a millimetre, no acute infection, thick phenotype, achievable primary stability, correct three-dimensional position. If any one is missing I place early instead.”\n\nGuided bone regeneration\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\n“I would think about this graft through Wang’s PASS principles, and then say which of the four is at risk in this case. Here it is primary closure, because the flap will be under tension — so I would perform a periosteal releasing incision and test the flap passively before placing the membrane. Naming the threatened principle is worth more than reciting all four.”\n\nHom-Lay Wang (with Boyapati, 2006) — the PASS principles The four requirements for predictable regeneration. Primary wound closure protects the graft from the oral environment while it is most vulnerable; Angiogenesis supplies blood, growth factors and osteoprogenitor cells, which is exactly why the buccal plate is decorticated; Space maintenance preserves a volume for bone to occupy, via a membrane that will not collapse, a slowly resorbing graft, or tenting screws; Stability of the clot prevents micromotion, achieved by fixation, tacks, or containment within bony walls\n\n“The membrane excludes the cells that would otherwise win the race. It is not there to hold the graft in.”\n\nNyman, Karring, Gottlow and colleagues The barrier principle — excluding faster-growing epithelium and connective tissue lets the slower cell population repopulate the wound\n\nDahlin, with Nyman, Linde and colleagues (1988) Showed experimentally that a barrier membrane over a bone defect produces bone regeneration\n\n“This is why a membrane is not optional over a dehiscence — without a barrier the defect fills with soft tissue and the graft becomes an encapsulated scaffold rather than bone.” Buser on the staged approach Graft first and place later where simultaneous placement cannot achieve correct position or stability; augmentation is prosthetically driven\n\n“Exposed collagen without infection I manage conservatively with chlorhexidine and no brushing; exposed or infected PTFE I remove early to salvage the bone already formed.”\n\n“If I cannot place in the correct prosthetic position with the bone that will be there, I stage — graft, wait six to nine months, place into a healed ridge. You augment to the position the restoration needs.” The biology of membrane exposure Exposure converts a closed regenerative wound into a contaminated one and substantially reduces bone gain; the commonest complication of the procedure\n\nSoft tissue and aesthetics"},{"sourcePage":70,"text":"Name(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nTarnow, with Magner and Fletcher (1992) Contact point to bone crest and papilla fill: at 5 mm or less the papilla was almost always present, falling substantially at 6 mm and further at 7 mm\n\n“When a patient asks about the black triangle, my answer is a measurement, not a reassurance — the papilla is nearly always present at 5 mm or less and unpredictable beyond that.” Tarnow, with Cho and Wallace (2000), and later work on adjacent implants Inter-implant distance under 3 mm gives significantly more crestal bone loss; soft tissue height between two implants is lower than between an implant and a tooth\n\n“At least 3 mm between implants, 1.5 to 2 mm to a tooth. The papilla between two adjacent implants is the hardest tissue to create, so I would ask whether one implant with a pontic gives a better result than two.” Grunder and colleagues The buccal bone requirement — approximately 2 mm of facial bone to maintain the facial soft tissue level\n\n“I would graft the buccal aspect even when the implant is stable. That is a prosthetic and aesthetic requirement, not a stability one.” Kan & Rungcharassaeng Phenotype as the determinant of mid-facial recession, especially after immediate placement, continuing over years\n\n“Thin phenotypes recede more after immediate placement and go on doing so, so here I would place early and consider a connective tissue graft at the same visit.” Fürhauser and colleagues (2005) The Pink Esthetic Score — a reproducible index for the peri-implant soft tissue\n\n“I would assess the result with an objective index rather than an opinion — papillae, level, contour, colour, texture, alveolar process — so I can record and compare.” Belser and colleagues (2009) The modified PES and White Esthetic Score, adding objective assessment of the crown\n\n“The white component scores the crown itself — form, outline, colour, texture, translucency. Together they are the vocabulary for documenting a result, or explaining why I am not satisfied with one.”\n\nPeri-implant disease\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nBerglundh and colleagues, 2017 World Workshop The case definitions of peri-implant health, mucositis and peri-implantitis, and of tissue deficiencies\n\n“Mucositis is bleeding on gentle probing without bone loss beyond initial remodelling; peri-implantitis adds bleeding or suppuration, increased probing depth and progressive bone loss. Without baseline records, 3 mm or more of bone loss with bleeding and probing of 6 mm or more supports the diagnosis.” Papapanou, Tonetti and Sanz (same workshop) Staging and grading of periodontitis — stage by severity and complexity, grade by rate of progression and risk factors\n\n“I would stage and grade the periodontitis before discussing implants at all — the grade gives me the rate of progression and the risk profile.” Derks & Tomasi (2015) Meta-analysis of prevalence, showing figures depend heavily on the case definition and threshold\n\n“Mucositis is the reversible stage and the point at which I can still change the outcome, so it is what I screen for at every review.”\n\n“Mucositis in roughly 20 to 65% of patients and peri-implantitis in roughly 1 to 47%, weighted means near 43% and 22%. The spread reflects different thresholds between studies, and that is the limitation I would state.” Lang & Berglundh The mucositis-to-peri-implantitis model — mucositis is the precursor and is reversible; the peri-implant lesion is less well contained than the periodontal one\n\nWilson, and Linkevicius and colleagues Residual excess cement as a cause of peri-implant disease, far more likely and less detectable with deep subgingival margins\n\n“This is why I use screw retention where the access channel allows. If I must cement, the margin goes no deeper than about 1 mm subgingivally, I extrude the excess on a copy abutment extra-orally, and I radiograph afterwards.” Heitz-Mayfield and Renvert The evidence for treatment: non-surgical decontamination first, its limited predictability in established disease, surgical access, and maintenance\n\n“Non-surgical therapy resolves mucositis reliably but is much less predictable in established peri-implantitis, so I would treat, reassess at six to eight weeks, and refer for surgery if it persists.” The EFP S3-level guideline (2023) A stepwise, graded guideline for prevention and treatment of peri-implant diseases\n\n“Risk factor control, non-surgical decontamination, reassessment, surgery if needed, supportive care throughout. Saying I follow a stepwise protocol matters more than naming any one instrument.”"},{"sourcePage":71,"text":"The edentulous mandible\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nThe McGill consensus (2002) — Feine and colleagues The two-implant overdenture should be the first-choice standard of care for the edentulous mandible\n\n“Not offering the option, or not recording that it was discussed, is a consent failing rather than a clinical preference.”\n\n“The evidence for satisfaction and function over a conventional lower denture is strong and consistent, and I would open with that.” The York consensus (2009) — Thomason and colleagues Reaffirmed McGill, and added that patients should be informed the option exists\n\nSinus floor elevation and augmentation\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nBoyne & James (1980) First published the lateral window elevation with autogenous marrow grafting\n\n“What has changed since is the graft material, not the principle — the Schneiderian membrane is elevated and the space beneath it becomes bone.” Tatum Developed and popularised the lateral window clinically, and the crestal approach\n\n“I choose on morbidity, cost and consent rather than a claim of superiority. What changes the outcome is residual bone height, membrane integrity and implant stability.”\n\n“Membrane integrity is the whole procedure, and perforation is the commonest intra-operative complication.” Summers (1994) The osteotome transcrestal technique through the implant osteotomy “Where residual height is adequate and the gain needed is modest I would consider a transcrestal approach — less invasive, but blind. I would refer a lateral window and would not attempt an atrophic posterior maxilla.” The ITI and consensus position on graft materials Survival in the grafted sinus is comparable across materials, including bone substitute alone; the graft is a space-maintaining scaffold\n\nConsent and the law\n\nName(s) Contribution What to say in the oral\n\nMontgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board (2015, UK Supreme Court) Replaced the professional standard for disclosure with a patient-centred test of material risk — any risk a reasonable person in the patient’s position, or this patient, would attach significance to, plus reasonable alternatives\n\n“The test is not what a body of dentists would disclose but what this patient would consider material, including reasonable alternatives and no treatment. Here that means the origin of graft materials, the lifetime maintenance burden and cost, the risk of peri-implant disease, and that the aesthetic result may not be perfect — and I must record the discussion, because a consent form is not consent.” GDC, Standards for the Dental Team Principle 1 patients’ interests first; Principle 3 valid consent; Principle 4 records; Principle 7 work within your knowledge, skills and experience\n\n“Principle 7 governs the referral decision. Working within my competence is a professional obligation, not a personal preference, so referring a vertical augmentation is the standard being applied — and I would record the reasoning.”\n\n4.4 The numbers: survival, success, longevity and prognosis\n\nWATCH OUT\n\nRead this before you memorise anything below. These are indicative figures for oral examination purposes. Every one varies with the case definition, the population, the threshold applied and the length of follow-up, and reported values across systematic reviews often differ by more than the figure itself. Quote them as ranges with a time frame, and be ready to name the limitation. “Around 95 to 98% at five years in systematic reviews,"},{"sourcePage":72,"text":"though survival is not success and these are largely specialist-treated cohorts” scores. “96.4%” invites a question you will not survive.\n\nImplant and prosthesis survival\n\nRestoration Implant survival, 5 years 10 years Source type How to say it\n\nSingle crown approx. 95–98% approx. 90–95% Systematic reviews of prospective cohorts “Around 95 to 98% at five years, 90 to 95% at ten. The crown survives slightly less well than the implant, and most cohorts are specialist-treated — so a good prognosis, not a guarantee.” Implant-supported fixed partial denture approx. 95% implants and prostheses approx. 90–93% implants; 85–90% prostheses\n\nSystematic reviews of prospective and retrospective cohorts\n\n“At ten years roughly nine in ten survive — but only about half the patients have been free of any complication over that period, which is the figure the patient actually needs.” Full-arch fixed prosthesis approx. 95–98% approx. 90–95% Systematic reviews, largely specialist cohorts\n\n“High implant survival with a substantial technical maintenance burden — and losing one implant in a full-arch reconstruction is a far larger problem than losing a single implant.” Implant overdenture (two implants, mandible)\n\napprox. 95–98% approx. 90–95% Systematic reviews, long-term cohorts, consensus statements\n\n“Implant survival is comparable to fixed work, but the prosthesis needs regular maintenance. I would consent for maintenance rather than for survival.”\n\nKEY POINT\n\nThree distinctions the examiners listen for. Implant survival is the implant still being in the mouth. Prosthesis survival is the restoration still in service — always the lower number over time, and the same implants may be carrying a second prosthesis. Success adds criteria: no pain or infection, no radiolucency, bone loss within limits, and an acceptable aesthetic and functional result. An implant can survive, fail against the success criteria, and be carrying its third crown. Say which one you mean.\n\nTechnical and biological complications over five years\n\nComplication Indicative 5-year rate Source type How to say it\n\nScrew loosening approx. 5–10%, higher on single molars and cantilevers Systematic reviews of prosthodontic complications “One of the commonest technical complications, higher with a wide occlusal table or a cantilever. Modern connections and correct torque have reduced it, not abolished it.” Ceramic or veneer chipping approx. 3–6% metal-ceramic; higher for veneered zirconia Systematic reviews; varies markedly with material “Highly material-dependent, which is why monolithic materials have become popular posteriorly.” Abutment or screw fracture around 1% or less Systematic reviews “Uncommon — but the complication with the worst consequence, because a fractured screw within the implant may end the restoration.”"},{"sourcePage":73,"text":"Complication Indicative 5-year rate Source type How to say it\n\nLoss of retention / decementation approx. 5%, higher with short or tapered abutments Systematic reviews of cemented restorations “Around 5% over five years, and one argument for screw retention where the access channel allows — the others being retrievability and avoiding residual cement.” Overdenture attachment maintenance — insert or matrix replacement, activation, relines, denture repair\n\nThe highest of any complication in implant dentistry. Most patients need some attachment maintenance within five years; figures vary very widely by attachment type\n\nSystematic reviews and long-term prospective cohorts “Attachment maintenance is the commonest complication in the whole of implant dentistry. The implants are very likely to last; the attachments, denture teeth and fit surface are consumables. That is planned maintenance, not failure — and rates vary too widely between systems for me to quote one figure.”\n\nPeri-implant disease prevalence\n\nWATCH OUT\n\nDo not quote a single number here. Reported prevalence depends almost entirely on the case definition and bone loss threshold used, and on whether the figure is per patient or per implant. Pooling studies with different thresholds produces very wide ranges — that spread is the finding, and saying so is the marked answer.\n\nCondition Reported range Weighted mean where reported How to say it\n\nPeri-implant mucositis roughly 20–65% of patients around 40–45% “Common, reversible, and what I am screening for at review.” Peri-implantitis roughly 1–47% of patients around 20–22% “Anywhere from about 1% to nearly 50%, weighted mean around 20%. That range is not sloppiness in the literature — it reflects genuinely different case definitions and thresholds, which is why the 2017 definitions matter. I would give the range and the reason for it.”\n\nMarginal bone loss\n\nMeasure Figure Where it came from How to say it\n\nFirst year after loading up to about 1.5 mm traditionally accepted; modern platform-switched connections commonly well under 1 mm\n\nThe Albrektsson and Zarb criteria, derived from machined, externally connected, two-stage implants\n\n“The classical figure comes from machined implants with an external connection — modern conical connections with platform switching typically lose considerably less.” Annually thereafter under 0.2 mm per year Same source “What matters clinically is whether the loss is progressive on serial radiographs taken reproducibly. One radiograph gives me a level; two give me a direction.” The practical rule Compare against a baseline radiograph at prosthesis fitting\n\n2017 World Workshop position on diagnosis “Without a baseline I cannot separate initial remodelling from disease, and I am reduced to the 3 mm fallback threshold.”\n\nSuccess of the procedures themselves"},{"sourcePage":74,"text":"Procedure Indicative outcome Source type How to say it\n\nGuided bone regeneration (horizontal) Survival in augmented sites comparable to native bone, around 95% or better at five years; membrane exposure the commonest complication\n\nSystematic reviews of cohorts and controlled studies “Comparable to native bone — with the caveat that these are mostly experienced operators and selected defects, and the complication that changes the result is exposure.” Vertical augmentation Less predictable, markedly higher complication rate than horizontal\n\nSystematic reviews; consistent direction of finding “The least predictable augmentation procedure. Outside what I would undertake, and I would refer.” Ridge preservation Reduces dimensional change, does not abolish it — typically 1–2 mm less horizontal loss and around 1 mm or more less vertical loss than unassisted healing\n\nSystematic reviews of cohorts “Good medium-term survival, but the technique is blind — a perforation may go unrecognised. That is why residual height and a modest required gain are the selection criteria.”\n\nMeta-analyses of randomised trials “It limits resorption, it does not prevent it. I consent the patient that the ridge will be better than it would otherwise have been, not that it stays as it is, and that a proportion of sites still need augmentation later.” Transcrestal (osteotome) sinus elevation approx. 95–98% at three to five years; gain of 3–4 mm; generally needs residual height around 5 mm or more; perforation less frequent than lateral but undetected when it occurs\n\nSystematic reviews and consensus reports “Highly operator- and anatomy-dependent, particularly with antral septa. I would refer it.”\n\nLateral window sinus elevation approx. 95% or better at three to five years; membrane perforation the commonest complication, reported over a wide range, commonly cited 10–25% and higher in some series\n\nImmediate vs early vs conventional placement, aesthetic zone\n\nSurvival is similar; the difference is aesthetic — immediate placement carries the highest risk of mid-facial recession in thin phenotypes and thin buccal walls\n\nSystematic reviews and consensus statements “Survival is the wrong outcome to compare them on. The difference is the aesthetic complication rate, which is why I select on phenotype and buccal wall thickness rather than on the patient’s preference for fewer visits.”\n\nRisk factors, and how much they change the numbers\n\nRisk factor Effect on the numbers How to say it\n\nSmoking Roughly doubles failure risk; greater effect in the maxilla and in grafted sites; also raises peri-implantitis and dehiscence\n\n“I would treat a smoker, but after a documented cessation discussion, and I would record that they were told their risk is higher.” Uncontrolled diabetes Control matters more than the diagnosis — well-controlled shows little or no increase in failure; poorly controlled means delayed healing and more peri-implant disease\n\n“I would ask for a recent HbA1c and liaise with the general medical practitioner. Well-controlled diabetes is not a contraindication; poorly controlled, I would defer until control improves.” History of periodontitis Roughly a two- to fivefold increase in peri-implantitis risk, greatest with severe or unstable disease\n\n“Not a contraindication, but among the strongest risk factors. I would establish periodontal stability first, consent specifically to the raised risk, and set a shorter maintenance interval from the outset.” Parafunction Increases technical complications — screw loosening, chipping, component fracture — rather than biological failure\n\n“A technical risk, so I design for it: screw retention, a narrower occlusal table, shallow cuspal inclines, no interferences, a nightguard — and consent to a higher maintenance rate.” Poor plaque control A consistent risk indicator for mucositis and peri-implantitis; the restoration must also be cleansable\n\n“If the patient cannot clean the restoration I designed, that is my failure — so plaque control before placing, and an emergence profile that can actually be cleaned.”"},{"sourcePage":75,"text":"Risk factor Effect on the numbers How to say it\n\n“Maintenance is not an add-on I offer; it is part of the treatment, and I make that explicit at consent, including the cost.”\n\nLack of supportive maintenance Absence of supportive therapy is associated with a substantially higher rate of peri-implantitis — commonly reported as two- to threefold\n\nHow to quote a number in the oral\n\nA quoted figure should contain four things:\n\n1. The range, not a point estimate — unless the number is a definition or a threshold, in which case be exact. 2. The follow-up period — “at five years”, “at ten years”. A survival figure without a time frame is meaningless. 3. What was actually measured — implant survival, prosthesis survival, patient-level prevalence, mil-limetres of horizontal change. 4. One limitation — the case definition, the population, the operator experience, the follow-up, or the heterogeneity between studies.\n\nAnd one prohibition: never invent a precise figure. If you do not know, say what you do know. “I am not certain of the exact figure, but the principle is…” is a scoring answer. A confident wrong decimal is not.\n\nDIRECTION OF THOUGHT\n\nThree good answers, and one bad one.\n\nGood — survival. “Systematic reviews of single implant crowns report implant survival of around 95 to 98% at five years and roughly 90 to 95% at ten. That is implant survival — crown survival is a little lower, because the crown may be remade over the same implant. The main limitation is that most cohorts are specialist-treated and selected, so I would present this as a good prognosis rather than a guarantee.”\n\nGood — peri-implantitis prevalence. “I would give a range. Peri-implantitis is reported in about 1% to nearly 50% of patients, with meta-analysed means around 20%, and mucositis in roughly 20 to 65%. That spread is because studies use different case definitions and bone loss thresholds, and report per patient or per implant — which is exactly why the 2017 World Workshop definitions were produced.”\n\nGood — ridge preservation. “Meta-analyses of randomised trials show ridge preservation reduces horizontal ridge loss by roughly one to two millimetres compared with unassisted healing, over healing periods of four to six months. Crucially it limits change rather than preventing it, so I would consent the patient that the ridge will still change and that a proportion of sites still need augmentation at placement.”\n\nBad. “Implants have a 98.2% success rate.” Three failures in one sentence: no time frame, no source, and success used where survival was meant — and the false precision invites the examiner to ask where the figure came from. There is no recovery from that answer once it has been given."},{"sourcePage":76,"text":"4.5 One-line summaries: the papers worth carrying into the room Section 4.3 gave you the names. This section gives you the papers themselves, compressed to one line each, in the form an examiner will accept: who, what they showed, and the number that came out of it.\n\nRead this section the way it is written — not as a reading list but as a spoken resource. In an oral you will almost never be asked “what did the paper say?”; you will be asked “how do you know?”, and the answer that scores is one clause of attribution followed by the clinical consequence. “Chen and Buser’s review of post-extraction sites found recession of more than a millimetre at the mid-facial mucosa is substantially more frequent after immediate placement, which is why I would place this one at four to eight weeks.” That is the whole technique.\n\nKEY POINT\n\nThree rules for using this section. 1. Learn the consequence column; the citation is only there to license it. 2. If you are not certain of a year, give the author and the finding and leave the year out — a wrong year invites a question you will lose. 3. Where a figure is a range across studies, say the range and say why it is a range. Precision you do not have is the fastest way to be caught.\n\nOn the source of this section. These summaries were consolidated with the help of Notes in Implant Dentistry (Chu and Tang, 1st edn, 2021), a freely distributed postgraduate study note that condenses each paper it covers into a single statement, alongside the standard texts and consensus documents already listed in §4.1. The citation is always the underlying paper, not the note. Papers dealing with work outside this examination’s scope — full-arch immediate-function protocols, block grafting, lateral window sinus surgery, ridge splitting, biologics — have been left out deliberately; where they appear below, they appear as the boundary at which a general dentist with an interest in implants refers.\n\nDiagnosis, prognosis and the language you are marked on\n\nPaper What it established What it means at the chair\n\nTonetti, Greenwell and Kornman (2018) — staging and grading The stage (I–IV) describes severity and complexity of management; the grade (A–C) describes the rate of progression and the risk of further loss\n\nThe single most examinable definition set in the component. Learn the three, in order.\n\n“I would stage from interproximal attachment loss at the worst site and from tooth loss due to periodontitis, and grade from bone loss as a percentage of age, smoking and glycaemic control.” Berglundh and colleagues (2018) — 2017 World Workshop peri-implant case definitions\n\nPeri-implant health, mucositis and peri-implantitis defined; bleeding on gentle probing is the pivot, progressive bone loss beyond initial remodelling makes the diagnosis\n\nGives you defensible language for the extraction decision instead of “hopeless”.\n\nLindhe and Lang — prognosis of the individual tooth Separates “irrational to treat” (recurrent perio-endo lesion, attachment loss to the apex, vertical root fracture, caries into the canal) from “doubtful” (class II–III furcation, angular defect, bone loss beyond two-thirds of the root)\n\n“An implant is not simply in or out — I would place this one in the compromised-survival group and say why.”\n\nMisch and colleagues (2008) — ICOI Pisa consensus health scale Four categories rather than two: success, satisfactory survival, compromised survival, failure, with bone loss thresholds attached\n\nThe Glossary of Prosthodontic Terms The formal definitions of implant, abutment and the prosthesis types Where the wording of a definition question comes from. Worth reading once.\n\nHealing, the peri-implant tissues, and why they are not periodontal tissues\n\nPaper What it showed Consequence\n\nAraújo and Lindhe (2005) — dimensional ridge alterations after extraction\n\nThe reason immediate placement does not preserve the buccal plate. Say it in those words.\n\nThe buccal bundle bone is ligament-dependent and resorbs once the tooth is removed; the buccal wall loses more height than the lingual\n\nThe number behind the ridge-preservation decision.\n\nAraújo and colleagues (2005) — implants into fresh sockets, dog model Placing an implant into the socket did not prevent the buccal wall resorbing Kills the “immediate placement holds the bone” answer outright. Schropp and colleagues (2003) About half the ridge width is lost in twelve months — of the order of 5–7 mm horizontally — most of it in the first three months, with the crest moving palatally"},{"sourcePage":77,"text":"Paper What it showed Consequence\n\nExplains why surface topography and clot stability matter, and why you do not disturb a site at two to four weeks.\n\nDavies (1998) — mechanisms of endosseous integration Three phases: osteoconduction (fibrin retention and cell migration to the surface), de novo bone formation, then remodelling\n\nWhy peri-implantitis progresses faster and why probing behaves differently.\n\nIvanovski and Lee (2017–2018) Peri-implant connective tissue fibres run parallel to the surface rather than inserting; the seal is adaptive, less vascular, and a weaker barrier\n\nUnderpins the whole maintenance argument.\n\nBerglundh, Bosshardt and Lang on the junctional epithelium The epithelial attachment sits at a bacterial interface and is the first line of defence\n\nThe microgap has a biological price; relevant to platform choice and depth.\n\nJudgar and colleagues (2014) Two-piece implants showed a thicker connective tissue component and a greater biologic width than one-piece\n\nWennström and Derks (2012) Less than 2 mm of keratinised mucosa was associated with higher plaque and bleeding scores, though evidence on bone loss was weaker\n\nThe augmentation-timing question: soft tissue is easier before, not after, the problem.\n\n“I would say the width matters for comfort and plaque control, and that the evidence for it preventing bone loss is weaker than people claim.” Boynueğri and colleagues (2013); Lin and colleagues (2018) Sites with adequate keratinised tissue showed lower inflammatory markers; a width of at least 2 mm is the usual threshold quoted\n\nMedical risk — the figures examiners ask for by name\n\nPaper The figure How to use it\n\nLets you rank risks honestly rather than listing them flatly.\n\nChen and colleagues (2013) — meta-analysis Smoking and radiotherapy significantly increase failure; diabetes and osteoporosis did not reach significance in that analysis\n\nChrcanovic and colleagues (2017) Failures cluster in individuals — a minority of patients carry a large share of all failures\n\nTwo drug classes that are easy to miss in an ordinary history.\n\n“Failure is patient-level as much as site-level, so a previous failure changes my consent conversation.” Chappuis and colleagues (2018) — medication-related failure Failure rates reported around 4.3% with proton pump inhibitors and 7.5% with SSRIs\n\nLets you separate the low-risk osteoporosis patient from the oncology patient.\n\nAAOMS position paper (2014) MRONJ in patients on long-term oral bisphosphonates ~0.1%, rising to about 0.21% with longer exposure; risk far higher with intravenous antiresorptives for oncology\n\nThe number that makes the referral threshold defensible.\n\nGaudin and colleagues (2015) MRONJ after extraction about 3.2% where the drug was given for oncological indications, and very much lower for osteoporosis\n\n“I would not stop the warfarin; I would check a recent INR and use local measures.”\n\nMadrid and Sanz (2009) Patients on oral anticoagulants with INR 2–4 who continued their medication did not bleed significantly more\n\nMost “contraindications” are relative. Saying so, and then managing the risk, is the scoring answer.\n\nHwang and Wang (2006) Named the small list of genuine absolute contraindications — recent infarct or stroke, valve surgery, profound immunosuppression, active malignancy, uncontrolled bleeding disorder\n\nThe reason “stability before implants” is a hard rule, not a preference.\n\nLee and colleagues (2012) Residual pockets of 5 mm or more with bleeding after periodontal treatment were a risk indicator for peri-implantitis, with a markedly higher rate at implant level in treated periodontitis patients\n\nMorris and colleagues (2000) Type 2 diabetes showed more failures, but the effect was marginal Control matters more than the diagnosis.\n\nPlacement timing and the immediate-placement argument"},{"sourcePage":78,"text":"Paper What it showed Consequence\n\nThe spine of any placement-timing answer.\n\nBuser, Chappuis, Belser and Chen (2017) — when immediate, when early, when late\n\nSets out Type 1 immediate / Type 2 early with soft tissue healing (4–8 weeks) / Type 3 early with partial bone healing (12–16 weeks) / Type 4 late, and argues Type 2 as the workhorse of the aesthetic zone\n\nSurvival is the wrong outcome measure in the aesthetic zone. Say that.\n\nChen and Buser (2009, 2014) — post-extraction outcomes Survival is similar across types, but recession of the mid-facial mucosa of more than 1 mm is more frequent, and outcomes more variable, after Type 1\n\nGives you the pre-requisite list in a form you can recite.\n\nCosyn and colleagues (2012, 2016, 2019) Advanced recession after immediate placement is predictable only when all of the favourable factors are present — intact buccal wall, thick phenotype, flapless surgery, correct 3-D position, immediate provisional\n\nThe honest answer to “why not place it immediately?” — you often cannot know until the tooth is out.\n\nChen and Darby (2017) At extraction in the anterior maxilla, fewer than half the sites had an intact facial wall; the remainder had fenestrations or dehiscences\n\nA shape you can assess intra-operatively and abandon on.\n\nKan and colleagues (2007) Facial defect morphology predicts the soft tissue result: V-shaped defects behaved best, U-shaped worse, and the wide “ultra-U” worst\n\nIf you place immediately, do both.\n\nTarnow and colleagues (2014) Grafting the gap and placing an immediate provisional preserved facial-palatal ridge contour better than either alone\n\nTurns “graft the jumping gap” into a measurement.\n\nCapelli and colleagues (2013) The implant-to-buccal-plate distance is a usable diagnostic parameter for deciding whether and how to graft the gap\n\nA technique-sensitive extension, not a default.\n\nRagucci and colleagues (2020) Immediate placement into molar sockets showed high survival with limited marginal bone loss in selected cases\n\n“Chronic apical infection is not an absolute bar if the socket is thoroughly debrided; acute suppuration is.”\n\nZhao (2016), Lee (2018), Chen (2018), Blus (2015) — infected sockets With debridement and antibiotic cover, immediate placement into chronically infected sockets showed no significant difference in failure or bone loss; reported survival around 98.8% non-infected, 100% chronically infected, and lower (about 94%) in acute infection\n\nThree-dimensional position — the numbers that decide aesthetics\n\nPaper The number Consequence\n\nGives you a structured way to answer “where exactly would you place it?”\n\nGrunder, Gracis and Capelli (2005) About 2 mm of buccal bone is needed at the shoulder for stable facial tissue The single most quoted figure in aesthetic-zone planning. Buser, Martin and Belser (2004, 2007) Comfort and danger zones in three planes; the shoulder placed palatal to the point of emergence and about 3 mm apical to the future gingival margin\n\nTarnow, Magner and Fletcher (1992) Contact point to bone crest 5 mm → papilla present almost always; 6 mm → about half; 7 mm →about a quarter\n\nWhy two adjacent implants are a different problem from two single ones.\n\nThe papilla answer, and the reason you look at the neighbouring bone, not the implant. Tarnow and colleagues (2000) Less than 3 mm between adjacent implants increased crestal bone loss laterally, of the order of 1.3–1.4 mm from each shoulder\n\nSets the honest expectation for the papilla between two implants.\n\nTarnow and colleagues (2003) Only about 3.4 mm of soft tissue on average forms over the inter-implant crest\n\nThe measurement behind “place it palatally, and graft the buccal contour”.\n\nSpray and colleagues (2000) Facial bone loss fell sharply once the buccal plate reached roughly 1.8–2 mm in thickness at placement"},{"sourcePage":79,"text":"Paper The number Consequence\n\nScutellà and colleagues (2015) Buccolingual position and the abutment finish-line geometry together determine emergence and therefore cleansability\n\nLinks the surgical decision to the restorative one, which is exactly what a domain 3 question is testing.\n\nThe edentulous mandible and the overdenture\n\nPaper What it showed Consequence\n\nLearn one sentence of this verbatim; it appears in almost every edentulous case.\n\nMcGill consensus (2002–2003) and the York statement (2009) A two-implant mandibular overdenture should be the first-choice standard of care for the edentulous mandible, rather than a conventional complete denture\n\nLets you answer the “how many implants?” question honestly.\n\nRoccuzzo and colleagues (2012) For the mandible, two implants are sufficient for a removable reconstruction; for the maxilla the evidence to recommend a number is far weaker\n\nThe safe recommendation, with a reason.\n\nSchimmel and colleagues (2014) All three loading protocols give high survival, but early and conventional loading are better documented than immediate\n\nThe counter-argument you should be able to state before rejecting it.\n\nSanda and colleagues (2019) Marginal bone loss around immediately loaded mandibular overdenture implants was comparable with conventional loading\n\nUseful when the examiner pushes on immediate loading.\n\nRignon-Bret and colleagues (2019) Immediate loading of two non-splinted mandibular implants remained satisfactory at 10 years\n\nThe maintenance argument for bar over solitary attachments.\n\nStoker and colleagues (2007) Over eight years, a bar on two implants was the most efficient design in terms of aftercare and cost\n\nA legitimate option for the frail or financially constrained patient.\n\nHarder and colleagues (2011) A single midline implant overdenture improved quality of life and chewing in older edentulous patients\n\nThe lip-support answer for the maxillary case.\n\nZitzmann and Marinello (1999) In the maxilla, a fixed design suits only minimal resorption with a favourable maxillomandibular relationship; otherwise an overdenture restores lip support better\n\nMore implants is not automatically better.\n\nSlot and colleagues (2019) Four versus six implants supporting a maxillary overdenture gave comparable five-year outcomes\n\nThe patient-centred outcome, which domain 2 rewards.\n\nAllen and McMillan (2003) Implant-retained prostheses produced significant improvement in satisfaction and oral health-related quality of life\n\nGrafting, ridge preservation and the materials\n\nPaper What it showed Consequence\n\nThe reassurance figure for consent to GBR.\n\nHämmerle and colleagues (2002); Donos and colleagues (2008) Implants placed in sites augmented with barrier membranes survive at rates comparable with pristine bone — of the order of 96–100% over 2–8 years\n\nThe technique a general dentist with implant experience can defend using.\n\nWessing, Lettner and Zechner (2017) GBR with particulate graft and a resorbable collagen membrane is effective for lateral ridge augmentation\n\nDraws your competence line for you.\n\nMilinkovic and Cordaro (2014) Dehiscences and fenestrations are treated predictably by simultaneous GBR; larger horizontal and vertical defects need staged and more advanced approaches\n\nJustifies over-contouring the buccal aspect with a slowly resorbing material.\n\nBuser (2013); Dahlin and colleagues (2009) Early placement with contour augmentation and GBR with a xenogeneic filler remained stable at five years and beyond"},{"sourcePage":80,"text":"Paper What it showed Consequence\n\nSay “reduces, does not prevent”. That distinction is the mark.\n\nMacBeth and colleagues (2016) Alveolar ridge preservation reduces but does not abolish dimensional change — a benefit of roughly 0.7–0.8 mm of mid-buccal height over an untreated socket\n\nThe material combination to name if asked what you would actually use.\n\nJung and colleagues (2018); Lim and colleagues (2019) Deproteinised bovine bone mineral with collagen under a native bilayer collagen membrane performed well for ridge preservation, including molar sites left to heal open\n\nExplains why particle and scaffold architecture matter, not just chemistry.\n\nKarageorgiou and Kaplan (2005) Pore size of at least 100 𝜇m is needed for cell migration; above about 300 𝜇m favours vascular ingrowth and direct osteogenesis\n\nThe trade-off behind particle-size selection.\n\nZhou and colleagues (2011); Zaner and Yukna (1984) Smaller particles give more contact with new bone but less inter-particle space; larger particles maintain space better\n\nThe maxillary sinus — mostly a referral, but know the argument\n\nPaper What it showed Consequence\n\nThe answer that keeps a case within your competence.\n\nEsposito and colleagues (2014) — Cochrane review Short implants without augmentation may be preferable to longer implants with sinus augmentation in the atrophic posterior maxilla, with fewer complications\n\nThe figure that supports the paragraph above.\n\nAnnibali and colleagues (2012) Short implants gave a cumulative survival of about 99% over roughly three years\n\nUseful for transcrestal cases; the graftless argument.\n\nThor and colleagues (2007); Fouad and colleagues (2017) Bone formed at the sinus floor after membrane elevation without graft material, with simultaneous implant placement\n\nCite it to explain why you would refer, which is the point of the question.\n\nTestori and colleagues (2019) Risk factors for lateral window surgery — anatomy, septa, membrane thickness, sinus health, the medical history\n\nPeri-implant disease\n\nPaper The figure Consequence\n\nQuote as a range and say the ranges are wide because case definitions differ.\n\nZitzmann and Berglundh (2008) Mucositis around 80% of subjects and 50% of implants; peri-implantitis around 28–56% of subjects and 12–43% of implants\n\nThe single strongest argument for the recall interval.\n\nCosta and colleagues (2012) Patients with existing mucositis and no maintenance had a much higher incidence of peri-implantitis at five years\n\nWhy “watch and see” is not a plan.\n\nFransson and colleagues (2010) Bone loss was non-linear and accelerating; a substantial minority of affected implants lost 2 mm or more\n\nRestorative contour is a biological decision. Excellent to volunteer.\n\nKatafuchi and colleagues (2018) Peri-implantitis was significantly more common where the emergence angle exceeded 30° at bone-level implants (about 31% versus 15%)\n\nSupports the history-of-periodontitis risk statement.\n\nMatarasso and colleagues (2010) Over ten years, periodontally compromised patients showed higher bone loss rates than periodontally healthy ones\n\nThe treat/refer boundary in one sentence.\n\nRenvert and colleagues (2007, 2008) Non-surgical therapy is effective for mucositis and adjunctive antiseptics help, but non-surgical therapy alone is insufficient for peri-implantitis"},{"sourcePage":81,"text":"Paper The figure Consequence\n\nThe structure of a surgical answer if pushed.\n\nHeitz-Mayfield and colleagues (2008, 2012) Named the risk indicators, and showed an anti-infective surgical protocol with decontamination and systemic antimicrobials to be effective at 12 months\n\nName it as a specialist procedure, and refer.\n\nSchwarz and colleagues (2014) Combined therapy — access flap, implantoplasty of supracrestal exposed surfaces, grafting the intrabony component\n\nThe reason bleeding on probing is your monitoring endpoint.\n\nLang and colleagues (2009) Sites bleeding consistently lost markedly more attachment than sites consistently non-inflamed\n\nProsthetics, retention and maintenance\n\nPaper What it showed Consequence\n\nAlways separate survival from complication-free survival.\n\nPjetursson and colleagues (2007) Compared survival and complications of tooth-supported FDPs, implant-supported FDPs and single crowns; complication rates for implant reconstructions are higher than survival figures suggest\n\nThe number that makes the maintenance conversation honest.\n\nPapaspyridakos and colleagues (2012) For full-arch fixed rehabilitation, prostheses free of any complication were about 29% at five years and 9% at ten\n\nThe defensible default in most cases.\n\nWittneben and colleagues (2014) No difference in survival, but screw-retained reconstructions showed fewer technical and biological complications and are retrievable\n\nThe sentence to quote whenever cement is mentioned.\n\nLinkevicius and colleagues (2013) The deeper the cement margin, the more undetected cement — and radiographs are not reliable for detecting it\n\nVindasiute and colleagues (2015) Undercuts and margin position govern how much excess can be removed at all Design decision, not a cleaning problem.\n\nThe fair counter-argument.\n\nVigolo and colleagues (2014) At four years, no difference in peri-implant bone or soft tissue between cemented and screw-retained single crowns when margins were supragingival\n\nAnswer with “it depends on length, bone and hygiene access”.\n\nBatista and colleagues (2018, 2019) Splinting versus not splinting adjacent implants made no difference to marginal bone loss; a small survival advantage to splinting in some analyses, particularly short implants\n\nThe impression answer, with a reason.\n\nPapaspyridakos (2014), Moreira (2015), Lee (2008) Splinted, open-tray impressions were the most accurate for multiple implants, particularly in the edentulous jaw\n\nA complication candidates routinely forget; volunteering it scores.\n\nFrench and colleagues (2019) Interproximal contact loss occurred at about 17% of implants and increased with time\n\nMisch and colleagues (2008) The ICOI health scale for reporting outcomes Use its four categories when asked to grade an existing implant.\n\nStability, micromotion and loading\n\nPaper The figure Consequence\n\nThe mechanism behind every loading rule you give.\n\nSzmukler-Moncler and colleagues (1998, 2000) There is a critical micromotion threshold — commonly quoted at around 50 to 150 𝜇m — above which fibrous encapsulation replaces bone\n\nSay “the threshold exists and is of that order” rather than a single figure.\n\nKohi and colleagues (2021) Systematic review confirming a tolerable-micromotion threshold, with variation between models"},{"sourcePage":82,"text":"Paper The figure Consequence\n\nExplains the stability dip at around two to four weeks — the most examinable curve in the subject.\n\nMeredith (1998); Sennerby and Meredith (2008) Primary stability is mechanical, from bone quality, implant design and technique; secondary stability is biological. RFA measures stiffness at the interface\n\nMinor, but useful if pressed on transcrestal technique.\n\nIto and colleagues (2008) RFA values did not correlate directly with histological bone-to-implant contact Use ISQ as a trend in one patient, not as an absolute threshold. Hsu and colleagues (2016) Primary stability was highest with indirect (transcrestal) sinus elevation among the fixation techniques compared, though differences were not significant\n\nImaging and radiation\n\nPaper What it established Consequence\n\nEAO guidelines (Harris and colleagues, 2012) Consensus on when cross-sectional imaging is justified in implant dentistry Cite when justifying, or declining, a CBCT. Jaju and Jaju (2015) Move from ALARA to ALADA — as low as diagnostically acceptable “I would justify the CBCT for this site individually, at the smallest field of view that answers my question.”\n\nWATCH OUT\n\nDo not recite this section. Nobody is examined on their ability to list papers, and a candidate who produces four citations for a question about a loose denture has misread what is being tested. Each of these is a single clause that licenses a clinical statement you were going to make anyway. If you cannot say the clinical statement without the citation, learn the clinical statement first."}]}]};